window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["lgbrimh-2025"] = {"id": "lgbrimh-2025", "title": "LGBRIMH Tezpur 2025, memory-based", "fullTitle": "LGBRIMH Tezpur M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance 2025 (GURET), memory-based recall", "sub": "62 recalled questions · options filled where the recall had gaps · every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical, Forensic and Health Psychology", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "Who initiated the first systematic work with families in India?", "options": {"a": "N. N. Wig", "b": "Girindrasekhar Bose", "c": "Vidya Sagar", "d": "A. Venkoba Rao"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: Vidya Sagar (1909 to 1978), a psychiatrist at Amritsar Mental Hospital, began the first systematic work with families in India. In the 1950s, as its medical superintendent, he faced an overcrowded hospital and let relatives stay with their patients in tents pitched on the hospital grounds. Family members lived alongside the patient, shared in the daily care, and joined regular group sessions that he held with patients and families together, often drawing on the Bhagavad Gita. Most patients improved and went home with families who had learnt how to look after them. He wrote little about it, so the work is known mostly through later accounts, yet he is often called the father of family therapy in India. It fits a wider Indian pattern: families here usually stay closely involved, and cross-cultural studies find low rates of high expressed emotion in Indian families. Example: when Arjun's mother camps beside his ward, she learns to spot early warning signs and keeps him on his medicine after he goes home. Option c is right because Vidya Sagar's Amritsar tent scheme is the recognised starting point of family work in India. Exam trap: examiners place other Indian pioneers beside him, so link 'first family work' only with Amritsar and the 1950s; Narendra Nath Wig is the name for community psychiatry, not family involvement. Key note: the recalled answer was spelt 'Vidyasagar'; the name is written both ways, and it is the same man, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Family work in Indian psychiatry", "fig": "Vidya Sagar"}, {"q": "What does a partial eta squared value of 0.14 indicate?", "options": {"a": "No effect", "b": "Small effect", "c": "Large effect", "d": "Medium effect"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a partial eta squared of 0.14 indicates a large effect. Eta squared is an effect size for ANOVA: the proportion of variance in the dependent variable that a factor explains. Plain eta squared is SS effect divided by SS total. Partial eta squared is SS effect divided by (SS effect plus SS error): it removes the variance due to other factors from the denominator, so it tells how much of the variance left over belongs to this factor. Jacob Cohen (1988) gave the benchmarks: about 0.01 is small, 0.06 medium and 0.14 large. So 0.14 means the factor accounts for about 14 percent of the relevant variance, right at the large cut-off. Effect size is separate from significance: a p value only says how unlikely the result would be if there were no effect, the effect size says how big the effect is, and with a large sample even a tiny effect can be significant. Example: Meera compares three therapy groups on anxiety; if SS effect is 28 and SS error is 172, partial eta squared is 28 / 200 = 0.14, a large effect. Option c is right because 0.14 meets Cohen's threshold for a large effect. Exam trap: students mix these cut-offs with those for Cohen's d (0.2, 0.5, 0.8) or r (0.10, 0.30, 0.50) and call 0.14 small; for eta squared, 0.14 is large. Key note: Cohen gave these cut-offs for eta squared; using them for partial eta squared is common practice and what exams expect, though partial values run larger in designs with several factors, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Partial eta squared benchmarks", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to Watson, thinking is:", "options": {"a": "Subvocal speech", "b": "Mental imagery", "c": "Sudden insight into a problem", "d": "Unconscious inference"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: for John B. Watson, thinking is subvocal speech, talking to oneself with tiny, hidden movements of the speech muscles. Watson founded behaviourism with his 1913 manifesto, 'Psychology as the behaviorist views it', and set out his view of thought fully in Behaviorism (1924 to 1925). He wanted psychology to study only observable behaviour, so he could not accept thought as an invisible mental event. His answer was peripheralism: thinking happens in the muscles at the periphery, not in a hidden mind. A child first talks aloud, then whispers, and finally the talk becomes implicit: faint movements of the larynx and tongue that could in principle be recorded with instruments. Thinking, for Watson, is a learned motor habit, no different in kind from playing tennis or golf. Example: when Kabir silently works out a sum, Watson would say his larynx and tongue are making small movements of the words. Later evidence went against the strong form of the idea: in a 1947 study, a volunteer fully paralysed with the drug curare, so that no speech muscle could move, stayed alert and could still perceive, think and remember. Option a is right because subvocal (implicit) speech is Watson's own definition of thinking. Exam trap: mental imagery is the classic distractor, but Watson rejected images as unobservable mentalism; imagery belongs to the structuralists, such as Edward Titchener (1898), who studied it through introspection.", "topic": "Watson's view of thinking", "fig": "John B. Watson"}, {"q": "Tangentiality, incoherence and derailment are examples of:", "options": {"a": "Disorders of the form of thought", "b": "Disorders of the stream of thought", "c": "Disorders of the content of thought", "d": "Disorders of the possession of thought"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: tangentiality, incoherence and derailment are disorders of the form of thought (formal thought disorder). Descriptive psychopathology, as set out in Frank Fish's Clinical Psychopathology (1967) and Andrew Sims's Symptoms in the Mind (1988), sorts thought disorders into four groups. Stream covers the speed and flow of thinking: flight of ideas, retardation, thought block (a sudden stop) and perseveration. Form covers how ideas are linked: the logical connection between one thought and the next breaks down. Content covers what the person thinks: delusions, overvalued ideas, preoccupations. Possession covers the sense that thoughts are one's own: obsessions, thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting. Nancy Andreasen (1979), in her Scale for the Assessment of Thought, Language and Communication, gave working definitions: tangentiality is replying in an oblique or irrelevant way that never reaches the point of the question; derailment (loosening of associations) is ideas slipping off track onto unrelated ones; incoherence (word salad) is speech so disorganised that it cannot be understood even within a sentence. Other formal disorders include neologisms, Knight's move thinking and overinclusion. Example: asked where he lives, Rohan says, 'Houses are made of brick, bricks are red, red is the colour of the flag, flags fly at the cricket ground', drifting further from the question with each link. Option a is right because all three describe a breakdown in the links between ideas, which is form. Exam trap: 'derailment' sounds like an interrupted flow, so students pick stream, the home of thought block and, in Fish, flight of ideas; but stream is about tempo and continuity, while tangentiality, derailment and incoherence are failures in how ideas link, the core formal thought disorders (some texts even move flight of ideas into form).", "topic": "Formal thought disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Who was the first psychiatrist to receive the Padma Shri?", "options": {"a": "Girindrasekhar Bose", "b": "N. C. Bordoloi", "c": "J. E. Dhunjibhoy", "d": "Owen Berkeley-Hill"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: Dr N. C. Bordoloi, superintendent of the mental hospital at Tezpur in Assam, was the first psychiatrist in India to receive the Padma Shri. The hospital opened in April 1876 under British rule as the Tezpur Lunatic Asylum. Dr Bordoloi became its superintendent in 1949, and under him its administration and treatment services improved markedly; the Government of India honoured him with the Padma Shri for that dedicated service. In 1989 the hospital was renamed after Gopinath Bordoloi, the first chief minister of Assam, a different Bordoloi; it is today the Lokopriya Gopinath Bordoloi Regional Institute of Mental Health (LGBRIMH). The shared surname is a coincidence that confuses many students. The Padma awards (Padma Vibhushan, Padma Bhushan and Padma Shri) began only in 1954, which rules out pioneers who died before then. Example: if Priya lists landmarks of psychiatry in north-east India, she should place Tezpur (1876), Dr Bordoloi's superintendence (from 1949) and the 1989 renaming side by side. Option b is right because the institute's own history records Dr N. C. Bordoloi as the first psychiatrist in the country to receive the Padma Shri. Exam trap: the shared surname makes students think option b is the politician after whom the institute is named, and famous Ranchi names such as Owen Berkeley-Hill and J. E. Dhunjibhoy are offered because they sound senior. Key note: the recalled answer named him 'Nani Chandra Bordoloi'; the institute's own history gives only the initials, N. C. Bordoloi, so the initials are used here; it is the same man, and the answer stands.", "topic": "Indian psychiatry pioneers", "fig": "N. C. Bordoloi"}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT an ICD-11 trait domain specifier for personality disorder?", "options": {"a": "Negative affectivity", "b": "Detachment", "c": "Anankastia", "d": "Psychoticism"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: psychoticism is not an ICD-11 trait domain; it belongs to the DSM-5 Alternative Model of Personality Disorders. ICD-11 (World Health Organization, in effect from January 2022) dropped the old list of named personality disorder types. The clinician first decides whether there is a personality disorder and grades its severity as mild, moderate or severe, with personality difficulty as a milder category outside the disorders. Then, optionally, he describes its style with trait domain specifiers: negative affectivity (anxiety, anger, low mood, low self-esteem, mistrust), detachment (social and emotional distance), dissociality (disregard for others' rights and feelings), disinhibition (acting rashly on impulse) and anankastia (perfectionism, rigid control of oneself and others). A borderline pattern specifier was kept as well. The DSM-5 Alternative Model (Section III, 2013) also has five pathological trait domains, but its list is negative affectivity, detachment, antagonism, disinhibition and psychoticism. The lists share three domains (negative affectivity, detachment, disinhibition); ICD-11's dissociality is close to DSM-5's antagonism, ICD-11 adds anankastia, and DSM-5 adds psychoticism. In ICD-11, schizotypal disorder sits in the grouping of schizophrenia or other primary psychotic disorders, not among the personality disorders. Example: Neha's ICD-11 diagnosis might read 'moderate personality disorder with negative affectivity and anankastia'. Option d is right because psychoticism appears only in the DSM-5 model, not in ICD-11. Exam trap: anankastia looks unfamiliar and tempts students to pick it, but it is ICD-11's own domain, the one with no DSM-5 counterpart. Key note: the recalled option d was 'Cognitive', and the recaller was unsure of the word; it is filled here with psychoticism, the classic ICD-11 versus DSM-5 contrast; either way, d is not an ICD-11 domain, so the answer stands.", "topic": "ICD-11 personality trait domains", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In the Health Belief Model, what helps a person most to adhere to treatment?", "options": {"a": "Trust in the healthcare provider", "b": "Perceived vulnerability to the illness", "c": "High self-efficacy", "d": "Fear of the side effects of treatment"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: in the Health Belief Model (HBM), perceived vulnerability (susceptibility) to the illness is the core belief that moves a person to adhere to treatment. The model grew out of United States Public Health Service research in the 1950s, was set out by Irwin Rosenstock (1966), and Marshall Becker (1974) applied it to compliance with medical advice. It says a person takes a health action when two things are true. First, he feels threatened: he believes he is susceptible to the illness and that it is serious (perceived severity). Second, he believes the action works: its benefits outweigh its barriers, such as cost, pain, side effects and inconvenience. Cues to action, such as a symptom, a reminder or a friend's illness, trigger the move. Self-efficacy was added later (Irwin Rosenstock, Victor Strecher and Marshall Becker, 1988). Example: Ravi, told he has high blood pressure, takes his tablets regularly only once he believes that he personally could have a stroke. Option b is right because perceived vulnerability is one of the model's own original beliefs: in the HBM, a sense of personal threat is what gives the person the push to act at all. Exam trap: trust in the provider sounds like a health belief, but it is not an HBM construct at all. Key note: reviews of the evidence (Nancy Janz and Marshall Becker, 1984; Christopher Carpenter, 2010) found perceived barriers, and in Carpenter's analysis benefits too, to be the strongest predictors, with susceptibility weaker for sick-role behaviour such as adherence than for prevention; self-efficacy also belongs to the expanded model, so c has a case. But among these options perceived vulnerability is the model's own core belief that drives action, while self-efficacy was borrowed later from Albert Bandura (1977), so b stands.", "topic": "Health Belief Model and adherence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In a negatively skewed distribution:", "options": {"a": "Mode > Median > Mean", "b": "Median > Mode > Mean", "c": "Mean = Median = Mode", "d": "Mean > Median > Mode"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: in a negatively skewed distribution the order is mode > median > mean. Skewness is a lack of symmetry. In a negatively (left) skewed distribution most scores pile up at the high end and a few very low scores stretch a long tail to the left; an easy test is the classic case, where most students score high and a handful do badly. The three averages react differently to those extreme scores. The mode, the most frequent score, sits at the peak, at the high end. The median, the middle score, is pulled a little towards the tail. The mean uses the value of every score, so the few very low scores drag it furthest towards the tail. Hence the mean is lowest, the median in the middle and the mode highest. A positive (right) skew, such as income, reverses this: mean > median > mode. In a normal distribution all three coincide. Karl Pearson (1857 to 1936) gave an empirical rule for moderately skewed data, mode = 3 median minus 2 mean, which gives the same order. Example: in Ananya's class of 30, 27 students score 85 on an easy quiz but three score 10; the mode and median stay at 85 while the mean drops to 77.5. Option a is right because the mean lies nearest the tail and the mode at the peak. Exam trap: students see 'negative' and reverse the order, or confuse the tail with the hump; remember that the mean follows the tail.", "topic": "Skewness and central tendency", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Who conducted the experiment in which children from poor backgrounds overestimated the size of coins?", "options": {"a": "Jean Piaget", "b": "Bruner and Goodman", "c": "Sigmund Freud", "d": "Frederic Bartlett"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Jerome Bruner and Cecile Goodman (1947) showed that children from poor homes overestimated the size of coins. In their study, 'Value and need as organizing factors in perception', ten-year-old children adjusted a circle of light until it matched the size of coins, from a penny to a half dollar. A control group matched plain grey cardboard discs of the same sizes and judged them much more accurately. The children overestimated the coins, and the overestimate broadly grew with the coin's value. Children from poor homes overestimated more than children from well-off homes. Bruner and Goodman argued that what a thing means to us, its value and our need for it, shapes how we see it. The study became a landmark of the New Look in perception of the late 1940s, which held that motives, values and expectations enter perception itself, not only later judgement. Later replications gave mixed results, and some argued that poor children were simply less familiar with coins. Example: for Sonu, who rarely holds money, a ten rupee coin may look bigger than it does to a child who gets pocket money every day. Option b is right because the coin study is Bruner and Goodman's. Exam trap: Jerome Bruner is better known in exams for his modes of representation (enactive, iconic, symbolic, 1966) and discovery learning, so students who know him only as a developmental theorist doubt him; the coin study is his early perception work.", "topic": "New Look: value and perception", "fig": "Jerome Bruner and Cecile Goodman"}, {"q": "In a one-way repeated measures ANOVA with 4 conditions and a sample of 20, what are the error degrees of freedom?", "options": {"a": "76", "b": "57", "c": "3", "d": "19"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the error degrees of freedom are 57. In a one-way repeated measures ANOVA every participant is tested under every condition, so the same 20 people supply all four sets of scores. The total variation is split into three parts: variation between conditions (the effect being tested), variation between subjects (stable individual differences, which this design removes from the error term) and residual error (the condition by subject interaction). Each part has its own degrees of freedom. With k = 4 conditions and n = 20 participants: df conditions = k minus 1 = 3; df subjects = n minus 1 = 19; df error = (k minus 1)(n minus 1) = 3 x 19 = 57; df total = kn minus 1 = 80 minus 1 = 79. Check: 3 + 19 + 57 = 79. The F ratio is MS conditions divided by MS error, read with df (3, 57). Because individual differences are taken out of the error term, a repeated measures design is more powerful than an independent groups design with the same number of scores. Example: Tara tests the same 20 students' recall after each of four study methods; she reports her result as F(3, 57). Option b is right because the error term in a one-way repeated measures ANOVA has (k minus 1)(n minus 1) = 57 degrees of freedom. Exam trap: 76 is N minus k, the error df for a between-subjects one-way ANOVA with 80 scores in four groups of 20; it is the answer for anyone who forgets that the same people appear in every condition.", "topic": "Repeated measures ANOVA df", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The law of comparative judgement is associated with which scale?", "options": {"a": "Likert scale", "b": "Guttman scale", "c": "Bogardus social distance scale", "d": "Thurstone scale"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the law of comparative judgement is associated with the Thurstone scale. Louis L. Thurstone (1927) published 'A law of comparative judgment', a way to turn simple choices into numbers on a psychological scale. In paired comparisons, judges see two stimuli at a time (two crimes, two statements) and say which is greater on some attribute. The proportion of judges choosing one over the other is converted, through the normal curve, into a distance between the two on the scale: if judges split 50 to 50, the two are equal, and the further the split moves from 50 percent, the further apart they lie. Thurstone then applied this thinking to attitudes. With E. J. Chave in The Measurement of Attitude (1929), he built the method of equal-appearing intervals: judges sort many statements into 11 piles from most unfavourable to most favourable, each statement gets a scale value (the median of its placings), ambiguous items are dropped, and a set spread evenly along the scale is kept. A respondent ticks the statements he agrees with, and his score is the median or mean of their scale values. Example: on an attitude to the church scale, Vikram agrees with items valued 7.5, 8.0 and 8.5, so his score is 8.0. Option d is right because the law of comparative judgement is Thurstone's own foundation for his scaling methods. Exam trap: the Likert scale is the best-known attitude scale and a common pick, but Rensis Likert's method sums agreement ratings and needs no judges or comparisons.", "topic": "Thurstone scaling", "fig": "Louis L. Thurstone"}, {"q": "In qualitative research, the process in which the researcher writes down reflections on the observations made is called:", "options": {"a": "Bracketing", "b": "Memoing", "c": "Member checking", "d": "Open coding"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: writing down one's reflections on the observations made, as the study goes on, is called memoing. Memos are short, informal notes that a qualitative researcher writes to himself about the data: hunches, questions, possible links between codes, ideas about an emerging category and his own reactions. Memoing is a core step in grounded theory, the method of Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss (1967), later developed by Anselm Strauss and Juliet Corbin (1990). In grounded theory, theory is built up from the data rather than tested on it, and memos are the bridge: the researcher codes the data, writes memos about what the codes might mean, compares new data with old (constant comparison) and finally sorts the memos into the structure of the theory. Memos also leave an audit trail, a record of how interpretations developed, which adds to the study's trustworthiness. Example: after interviewing carers of people with schizophrenia, Shreya writes, 'Three mothers spoke of shame only when talking about neighbours; is stigma tied to the neighbourhood rather than the family?' That note is a memo. Option b is right because memoing is the standard name for recording reflections on observations during analysis. Exam trap: bracketing also involves the researcher's own thoughts, but it means setting them aside, not writing them down to build the analysis. Key note: reflexive journaling is close in meaning, but memoing is the standard term for writing down reflections on observations as one goes, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Memoing in grounded theory", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Analysing the data in the middle of a study, before all of it has been collected, is called:", "options": {"a": "Interim analysis", "b": "Meta-analysis", "c": "Secondary analysis", "d": "Post hoc analysis"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: analysing the data part way through a study, before collection is complete, is called interim analysis. It is used mostly in clinical trials that recruit patients over months or years. At planned points, for example after half the patients have finished, the accumulating data are examined to see whether the new treatment is already clearly better, clearly harmful, or so unlikely to show a difference that carrying on would be futile. If so, continuing may be unethical, and the trial can be stopped early. But every extra look at the data is another chance to find a 'significant' result by luck, so repeated looks inflate the Type I error rate. Trials therefore fix the number of looks and the stopping rules in advance and demand stricter thresholds at early looks, as in the boundaries of Peter O'Brien and Thomas Fleming (1979). The interim results are usually reviewed by an independent data monitoring committee, so that the investigators stay blind to how the trial is going. Example: Dr Rahul's trial of a new antidepressant plans to enrol 400 patients; at 200, the committee finds a clear excess of serious side effects and stops the trial. Option a is right because interim analysis is the name for examining the data while the study is still running. Exam trap: post hoc analysis also sounds like a mid-course check, but it comes after the main analysis is finished, such as follow-up tests after a significant ANOVA.", "topic": "Interim analysis in trials", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In forensic psychology, the M'Naghten Rule is:", "options": {"a": "A test of whether the accused is competent to stand trial", "b": "A test of whether the accused could not resist an impulse to commit the act", "c": "A test of insanity: whether, because of a disease of the mind, the accused did not know what he was doing or that it was wrong", "d": "A rule that the accused is not responsible if the act was the product of a mental disease"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the M'Naghten Rule is a test of insanity: whether, because of a disease of the mind, the accused did not know what he was doing or did not know that it was wrong. In 1843 Daniel M'Naghten, who believed he was being persecuted, shot and fatally wounded Edward Drummond, secretary to Prime Minister Robert Peel, probably mistaking him for Peel. He was acquitted as insane, there was a public outcry, and the House of Lords put questions to the judges. Their answers became the M'Naghten Rules: to establish insanity it must be clearly proved that, at the time of the act, the accused was labouring under such a defect of reason, from disease of the mind, as not to know the nature and quality of the act he was doing or, if he did know it, that he did not know he was doing what was wrong. It is a cognitive test: it asks about knowing, not self-control. India follows it: Section 84 of the Indian Penal Code, now Section 22 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (2023), 'act of a person of unsound mind', excuses an act done by a person who, at the time of doing it, by reason of unsoundness of mind, is incapable of knowing the nature of the act or that it is wrong or contrary to law. Example: Suresh, acutely psychotic, kills his neighbour believing he is destroying a demon; he did not know the nature of his act. Option c is right because it states the two limbs of the rule. Exam trap: the Durham rule (option d) also concerns insanity, but it asks whether the act was the product of mental disease, with no test of knowledge.", "topic": "M'Naghten insanity rule", "fig": "Daniel M'Naghten"}, {"q": "The broaden-and-build theory of positive emotions was given by:", "options": {"a": "Martin Seligman", "b": "Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi", "c": "Ed Diener", "d": "Barbara Fredrickson"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the broaden-and-build theory of positive emotions was given by Barbara Fredrickson (1998, 2001). Earlier emotion theories focused on negative emotions, which narrow attention to a specific action tendency: fear prepares us to flee, anger to attack. Fredrickson argued that positive emotions such as joy, interest, contentment and love work differently. In the moment they broaden a person's thought-action repertoire: joy urges play, interest urges exploring, contentment urges savouring. Over time, these broadened moments build lasting personal resources: physical (health), intellectual (knowledge and problem-solving skill), social (friendships) and psychological (resilience, optimism). The resources remain after the emotion has passed and help the person cope later, setting up an upward spiral. She also described an undoing effect: positive emotions speed recovery from the cardiovascular arousal left by negative emotions. Experiments supported broadening: after films that induced amusement or contentment, people listed more things they wanted to do and noticed the overall shape of a figure rather than its details. Example: Isha, laughing with classmates during a group project, comes up with more ideas and builds friendships that later support her through exam stress. Option d is right because broaden-and-build is Fredrickson's theory. Exam trap: Martin Seligman founded positive psychology, so students credit every positive psychology theory to him; broaden-and-build belongs to Fredrickson.", "topic": "Broaden-and-build theory", "fig": "Barbara Fredrickson"}, {"q": "The ethical principles of decision making are:", "options": {"a": "Autonomy, beneficence, justice, fidelity, non-maleficence", "b": "Autonomy, benevolence, fidelity, non-maleficence", "c": "Autonomy, beneficence, fidelity, maleficence", "d": "Autonomy, benevolence, fidelity, maleficence"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the ethical principles of decision making are autonomy, beneficence, justice, fidelity and non-maleficence. Karen Kitchener (1984) proposed these five moral principles as the foundation for ethical decisions in counselling psychology. She built on Tom Beauchamp and James Childress's Principles of Biomedical Ethics (1979), which gave four principles: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice; Kitchener added fidelity. Autonomy is respecting the client's right to make his own choices, the basis of informed consent. Beneficence is actively doing good and promoting the client's welfare. Non-maleficence is avoiding harm, the old rule primum non nocere ('first, do no harm'), often seen as the most basic duty. Justice is fairness: treating people equally and sharing services fairly. Fidelity is keeping promises and staying loyal and honest, which underlies confidentiality and trust. When principles clash, the counsellor has to weigh them against each other. Example: Meenal, a counsellor, respects her client Aman's choice to stop sessions (autonomy) but explains the risks of stopping (beneficence), keeps what he told her confidential (fidelity) and gives him the same standard of care as any other client (justice). Option a is right because it is the only option that lists all five principles under their correct names. Exam trap: the wrong options swap in look-alike words, 'benevolence' (a kindly feeling, not the duty of beneficence) and 'maleficence' (doing harm, the opposite of non-maleficence), and they also drop justice.", "topic": "Ethical principles in counselling", "fig": "Karen Kitchener"}, {"q": "Which section of the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2015 deals with orders that may not be passed against a child in conflict with law?", "options": {"a": "Section 12", "b": "Section 15", "c": "Section 18", "d": "Section 21"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: Section 21 of the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2015 deals with orders that may not be passed against a child in conflict with law. The Act came into force on 15 January 2016, replacing the Juvenile Justice Act of 2000. A child in conflict with law is a person alleged or found to have committed an offence who was under 18 on the date of the offence. Section 21 says that no such child shall be sentenced to death or to life imprisonment without the possibility of release, for any offence, whether under this Act, the Indian Penal Code or any other law. The bar holds even when a child aged 16 to 18 is tried as an adult for a heinous offence in the Children's Court. The surrounding sections set out the process: Section 12 bail, Section 14 inquiry by the Juvenile Justice Board, Section 15 preliminary assessment, Section 17 orders when the child is not found to be in conflict with law, and Section 18 orders when he is. Clinical psychologists matter here because, under Section 15, the Board may take the help of experienced psychologists to assess the child's mental and physical capacity to commit the offence and his ability to understand its consequences. Example: Aakash, 17, is tried as an adult for murder; even if convicted, he cannot be sentenced to death or to life imprisonment without release. Option d is right because Section 21 is headed 'Order that may not be passed against a child in conflict with law'. Exam trap: Section 18 also concerns orders, so students pick it, but it lists the orders the Board may pass, while Section 21 lists those that can never be passed.", "topic": "JJ Act 2015 Section 21", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In persuasion, the boomerang effect is when:", "options": {"a": "The person moves further towards the message than the speaker intended", "b": "The person adopts a position opposite to the one the message argues for", "c": "The message gains impact over time as its source is forgotten", "d": "The person resists a strong attack after first meeting weak counter-arguments"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the boomerang effect is when a person adopts a position opposite to the one the message argues for. A persuasive message is meant to move the listener towards the speaker's view; in a boomerang it moves him away, so attitude change runs in the reverse direction. Two theories explain why. Muzafer Sherif and Carl Hovland's social judgement theory (1961) says each person has a latitude of acceptance (positions he finds acceptable), a latitude of non-commitment and a latitude of rejection around his own view. A message that falls in the latitude of rejection is contrasted: it is seen as even more extreme than it is, dismissed, and may push the person further the other way. Jack Brehm's reactance theory (1966) adds that a heavy-handed message threatens a person's freedom to choose, and he restores that freedom by doing the opposite. Boomerangs are most likely with very discrepant, preachy or controlling messages, and with sources seen as untrustworthy. Example: a school assembly that lectures Kunal harshly, telling him he must never touch a cigarette, leaves him keener to smoke, to show that nobody can control him. Option b is right because a shift opposite to the message's position is the definition of the boomerang effect. Exam trap: the sleeper effect (option c) is another named effect from Carl Hovland's Yale persuasion research, and students mix the two because both are unexpected outcomes; the sleeper effect is a delayed gain in persuasion, the boomerang a reversal.", "topic": "Boomerang effect in persuasion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Excess of dopamine is linked to:", "options": {"a": "Parkinson's disease", "b": "Alzheimer's disease", "c": "Schizophrenia", "d": "Major depression"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: excess dopamine activity is linked to schizophrenia. The dopamine hypothesis holds that the positive symptoms of schizophrenia (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised thinking) come from too much dopamine activity, especially at D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway. Arvid Carlsson showed in the late 1950s that dopamine is a neurotransmitter in its own right, work that earned him a share of the Nobel Prize in 2000. Two lines of evidence support the hypothesis. First, antipsychotic drugs block D2 receptors, and in 1976 Ian Creese, David Burt and Solomon Snyder, and separately Philip Seeman's group, showed that a drug's clinical potency closely tracks how strongly it binds to D2 receptors. Second, drugs that raise dopamine, such as amphetamine and cocaine, can produce a paranoid psychosis that looks like schizophrenia, and they worsen symptoms in patients. The revised hypothesis (Kenneth Davis and colleagues, 1991) adds that dopamine is too high in the mesolimbic pathway but too low in the mesocortical pathway to the prefrontal cortex, which may underlie negative symptoms such as apathy and poverty of speech. Example: Rahul, after weeks of heavy amphetamine use, became convinced that his neighbours were spying on him; his symptoms faded with a D2-blocking antipsychotic. Option c is right because excess dopamine is the classic biochemical account of schizophrenia. Exam trap: Parkinson's disease is offered because it also involves dopamine, but it is the reverse, too little dopamine after loss of neurons in the substantia nigra, which is why dopamine-blocking antipsychotics can cause Parkinson-like side effects.", "topic": "Dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which memory lasts longer?", "options": {"a": "Echoic memory", "b": "Iconic memory", "c": "Both last equally long", "d": "Neither"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: echoic memory lasts longer than iconic memory. Both are parts of sensory memory (the sensory register), the first stage in Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin's (1968) multi-store model: it holds a large amount of raw, unprocessed information for a very short time, one store for each sense. Ulric Neisser (1967) named the visual store iconic memory and the auditory store echoic memory. George Sperling (1960) measured iconic memory with his partial report method. Shown 12 letters for 50 milliseconds, people could report only 4 or 5; but when a tone just after the flash signalled which row to report, they gave about 3 of the 4 letters in any row, so about 9 of the 12 had been briefly available. Delaying the tone by about a second removed the advantage: the icon fades within about a quarter to half a second. Christopher Darwin, Michael Turvey and Robert Crowder (1972), with an auditory version (the 'three-eared man' study), found that echoic memory lasts about 2 to 4 seconds. The difference makes sense: a picture is there all at once, but speech unfolds over time, so the auditory store must hold a few seconds of sound for a sentence to make sense. Example: when Sameer, absorbed in a book, says 'What?' to his mother and then answers before she repeats herself, he is reading her words from echoic memory. Option a is right because echoic memory lasts seconds, while iconic memory lasts under a second. Exam trap: iconic memory is better known because of Sperling's study, so students assume it lasts longer; it is the shorter store.", "topic": "Sensory memory durations", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In ethnography, an etic approach refers to:", "options": {"a": "The insider's view, in the culture's own terms and categories", "b": "The researcher living with the group and taking part in its daily life", "c": "The outsider's view, using the researcher's categories that can be compared across cultures", "d": "Recording members' life stories in their own words"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: an etic approach is the outsider's view, using the researcher's categories that can be compared across cultures. The linguist Kenneth Pike (1954) coined the pair emic and etic from the words phonemic and phonetic. Phonetics describes speech sounds with a universal system that works for any language; phonemics describes the sound differences that matter to speakers of one particular language. By analogy, an emic account describes a culture from the inside, in its members' own concepts and meanings, and holds only for that culture; an etic account describes it from the outside, with concepts the scientist brings, which can be applied to many cultures and compared. Ethnography usually aims first for an emic understanding through long fieldwork and may then add an etic analysis. John Berry (1969) carried the distinction into cross-cultural psychology and warned against an imposed etic, applying Western concepts to another culture as if they were universal; a derived etic is built up only after emic study of several cultures. Example: Divya studies distress in a Tamil village. The villagers' own idiom of 'tension' is an emic category; coding the same complaints as generalised anxiety disorder using DSM criteria is an etic one. Option c is right because an etic approach is defined by the outsider's comparative categories. Exam trap: the two terms are easily reversed, and option a, the definition of emic, is placed first to catch students; remember that etic, like phonetic, uses the outside observer's universal system.", "topic": "Emic and etic approaches", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to Piaget, at what age does a child develop intentional behaviour?", "options": {"a": "4 to 8 months", "b": "8 to 12 months", "c": "12 to 18 months", "d": "18 to 24 months"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: according to Jean Piaget, a child first shows clearly intentional, goal-directed behaviour at about 8 to 12 months. Piaget (1936), drawing on close observation of his own three children, divided the sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years) into six substages. Substage 1 (birth to 1 month) is exercising reflexes such as sucking and grasping. Substage 2, primary circular reactions (1 to 4 months), repeats chance actions centred on the baby's own body, such as thumb sucking. Substage 3, secondary circular reactions (4 to 8 months), repeats actions that happened to produce an interesting effect in the world, such as shaking a rattle. Substage 4, coordination of secondary circular reactions (8 to 12 months), is the turning point: the infant now combines two schemes, one as the means and the other as the end. Baby Riya pushes a cushion aside (the means) to grab the toy hidden behind it (the goal). This is also when she first searches for a fully hidden object, the start of object permanence, although she still makes the A-not-B error. Substage 5, tertiary circular reactions (12 to 18 months), is the 'little scientist' who varies actions to see what happens, and substage 6 (18 to 24 months) brings mental representation and deferred imitation. Option b is right because Piaget's marker of the first truly intentional act, with the means kept apart from the goal, is substage 4. Exam trap: 4 to 8 months looks purposeful, since the baby repeats an action to keep an effect going, but the effect was first found by chance, so it is not yet planned intention. Key note: some textbooks say intentionality begins to show in substage 3; but Piaget's own marker of the first clearly intentional act is substage 4, at 8 to 12 months, so b stands.", "topic": "Piaget's sensorimotor substages", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "Means-end analysis and working backward are examples of:", "options": {"a": "Algorithms", "b": "Heuristics", "c": "Insight", "d": "Trial and error"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: means-end analysis and working backward are heuristics, rules of thumb that speed up problem solving without guaranteeing a solution. Psychologists contrast two broad kinds of strategy. An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that is certain to reach the answer if it is followed exactly, such as long division or trying every possible combination on a padlock; it is reliable but can be painfully slow. A heuristic is a mental shortcut that cuts down the search, usually works, but can fail. Allen Newell and Herbert Simon (Human Problem Solving, 1972), whose earlier General Problem Solver computer program modelled human reasoning, treated means-end analysis as the most important heuristic: note the biggest difference between where you are and where you want to be, set a subgoal that reduces it, and repeat until the gap closes. Working backward starts from the goal and traces the steps back to the starting point, which suits mazes, puzzles and geometry proofs. Arjun, planning his revision, starts from the exam date and counts back to decide what he must finish each week: that is working backward. Hill climbing and reasoning by analogy are other common heuristics. Option b is right because both strategies are shortcuts that narrow the search space but offer no promise of success. Exam trap: students sometimes call means-end analysis an algorithm because it sounds systematic and works in steps; but it only guides the search and does not guarantee a solution, which is exactly what makes it a heuristic.", "topic": "Heuristics versus algorithms", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which is most associated with frequent pairing of stimuli?", "options": {"a": "Backward conditioning", "b": "Trace conditioning", "c": "Simultaneous conditioning", "d": "Paired conditioning"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: paired conditioning, in the sense of repeatedly presenting a conditioned stimulus (CS) together with an unconditioned stimulus (US), is the option that names frequent pairing itself. In classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927), a neutral stimulus such as a bell comes to produce salivation because it is paired again and again with food. Each pairing is a trial, and the conditioned response grows stronger over trials during acquisition. Textbooks then describe several timing arrangements of the pairing. In delayed (forward) conditioning, the CS starts first and overlaps the US: this works best. In trace conditioning, the CS ends before the US begins, leaving a gap the animal must bridge with a memory trace, so it is weaker. In simultaneous conditioning, CS and US start together, which is weak. In backward conditioning, the US comes first; this is the weakest and can even teach that the CS signals no US. Robert Rescorla (1968) later showed that pairing alone is not enough: the CS must also predict the US (contingency). Example: Meera's mother calls her every evening, and her phone plays one particular ringtone each time; after many pairings, that ringtone alone, even from someone else's phone, makes Meera smile. Option d is right because the stem asks about frequent pairing in general, and only d names the pairing itself, while a, b and c are particular timing arrangements. Exam trap: when a question asks which arrangement conditions best, the answer is delayed (forward) conditioning, not trace conditioning, which students confuse because 'delay' sounds like 'gap'. Key note: 'paired conditioning' is not a standard textbook name, and the original options may have been recalled imperfectly (the real question may have offered delayed or forward conditioning); among these four, d is the only option that names repeated stimulus pairing itself, so d stands.", "topic": "Classical conditioning: CS-US pairing", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In Fiedler's contingency model, a high score on the Least Preferred Coworker (LPC) scale indicates which leadership style?", "options": {"a": "Task oriented", "b": "Nurturance oriented", "c": "Power oriented", "d": "Relationship oriented"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: in Fred Fiedler's contingency model (1967), a high score on the Least Preferred Coworker (LPC) scale marks a relationship-oriented leader. Fiedler asked leaders to think of the one person they had least liked working with and to rate that person on bipolar adjectives such as pleasant to unpleasant and friendly to unfriendly. A leader who still describes this difficult coworker in fairly kind terms (high LPC) separates the person from the work and is mainly motivated by good relationships. A leader who rates the person harshly (low LPC) is task-motivated: getting the job done comes first, and a poor worker is simply a bad person to work with. Fiedler treated this style as a fairly fixed trait. Whether it succeeds depends on situational favourableness, judged from three factors: leader-member relations, task structure and the leader's position power. Low-LPC (task-oriented) leaders do best when the situation is very favourable or very unfavourable; high-LPC (relationship-oriented) leaders do best when it is moderately favourable. Since style is hard to change, Fiedler advised changing the situation or matching the leader to it, through his Leader Match training. Example: Kabir, a ward manager who rates his least liked nurse as 'helpful though slow', scores high on the LPC and leads through rapport. Option d is right because a high LPC score shows that the leader values relationships even with a disliked coworker. Exam trap: students reverse the scoring and assume a high score means a demanding, task-focused leader; it is the low LPC score that marks the task-oriented style.", "topic": "Fiedler's LPC contingency model", "fig": "Fred Fiedler"}, {"q": "Crystallised intelligence refers to:", "options": {"a": "Knowledge and skills built up through education and experience", "b": "The ability to reason and solve new problems without prior learning", "c": "The speed with which simple information is processed", "d": "Know-how for solving everyday problems in real-life settings"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: crystallised intelligence is the knowledge and skills a person builds up through education and experience. Raymond Cattell (1963), later joined by his student John Horn (1966), split Charles Spearman's (1904) general intelligence (g) into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the ability to reason and solve new problems for which past learning gives little help: seeing patterns, drawing inferences and thinking flexibly. It depends heavily on the efficiency of the brain, rises through childhood, peaks in early adulthood and then declines slowly with age. Raven's Progressive Matrices and Cattell's Culture Fair Intelligence Test are classic measures. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the stored product of using that fluid ability within a culture: vocabulary, general information, arithmetic facts and professional know-how. It is tapped by vocabulary and information subtests, and it holds steady or keeps rising well into old age. Cattell's investment theory says fluid ability is 'invested' in learning, and the result crystallises into knowledge. Example: Grandmother Lakshmi, aged 70, beats her grandson at crosswords (crystallised) but takes longer than he does to learn a new phone app (fluid). The later Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) model keeps Gf and Gc and adds other broad abilities such as processing speed (Gs). Option a is right because crystallised intelligence is defined by accumulated, culturally learnt knowledge. Exam trap: the two definitions are often swapped; remember that crystals form slowly over time, so crystallised ability grows with experience, while fluid ability flows into any new problem.", "topic": "Fluid and crystallised intelligence", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "Who gave the expectancy theory of motivation?", "options": {"a": "Victor Vroom", "b": "Frederick Herzberg", "c": "B. F. Skinner", "d": "Abraham Maslow"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Victor Vroom gave the expectancy theory of work motivation in his book Work and Motivation (1964). The theory is often called VIE theory after its three beliefs. Expectancy is the belief that effort will lead to good performance ('if I try, can I do it?'). Instrumentality is the belief that performance will lead to an outcome or reward ('if I do it, will I get the promotion?'). Valence is how much the person values that outcome. Vroom combined them by multiplication: motivational force = expectancy x instrumentality x valence. Because it is a product, a zero on any one term wipes out motivation, however high the others are. Example: Neha, a sales executive, is offered a bonus she badly wants (high valence) and believes the targets are reachable (high expectancy), but she has seen bonuses withheld before (low instrumentality), so she does not push herself. Lyman Porter and Edward Lawler (1968) extended the model by adding ability, role perceptions and the satisfaction that follows rewards. Expectancy theory is a process theory: it explains how people decide where to spend effort, rather than listing which needs they have. Option a is right because the expectancy, instrumentality and valence model of motivation is Vroom's. Exam trap: Frederick Herzberg (1959) and Abraham Maslow (1943) are the names students most link with work motivation, but they gave content theories (what motivates people), whereas expectancy theory, a process theory (how effort is chosen), is Vroom's.", "topic": "Vroom's expectancy (VIE) theory", "fig": "Victor Vroom"}, {"q": "In Cattell's theory, the way ergs, sentiments and attitudes are linked in the dynamic lattice is the principle of:", "options": {"a": "Fusion", "b": "Prepotency", "c": "Association", "d": "Subsidiation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: in Raymond Cattell's theory, ergs, sentiments and attitudes are linked in the dynamic lattice by subsidiation. Cattell (1950) divided traits into ability, temperament and dynamic traits; the dynamic traits are the motivational ones. Ergs are innate drives such as hunger, sex, fear, curiosity, gregariousness and self-assertion. Sentiments are learnt attitude structures built around major objects in a person's life, such as a career, home, religion or the self (the self-sentiment ties the rest together). Attitudes are the most specific level: an interest in a particular course of action in a particular situation. Subsidiation means that one is subsidiary to another, a means to it: attitudes serve sentiments, and sentiments serve ergs. Example: Rohan's attitude 'I want to revise pharmacology tonight' serves his sentiment towards his medical career, which in turn serves ergs such as self-assertion and security. Drawn as a web of these means-to-end links, the result is the dynamic lattice. The word goes back to Henry Murray (1938), who described one need as subsidiary to another, needs fusing (one act satisfying several needs at once) and prepotent needs taking priority. Option d is right because subsidiation is the principle Cattell used to chain attitudes to sentiments to ergs. Exam trap: fusion and prepotency sound plausible because they also come from Murray's account of needs, and prepotency is Abraham Maslow's (1943) word for lower needs dominating; but they describe how needs combine or which need wins, not the means-end chain. Key note: the original recall listed option d as 'Principle of substitution' and gave prepotency as the answer; substitution is almost certainly a misremembered 'subsidiation', Cattell's own term for the dynamic lattice, while prepotency is Murray's and Maslow's word for which need wins out, so d (subsidiation) stands.", "topic": "Cattell's dynamic lattice", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "Reciprocal inhibition is associated with:", "options": {"a": "Exposure and response prevention", "b": "Cognitive behaviour therapy", "c": "Flooding", "d": "Systematic desensitisation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: reciprocal inhibition is the principle behind Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation. Wolpe (1958), in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, borrowed the term from Charles Sherrington's (1906) work on spinal reflexes, where contracting one muscle inhibits its opposing muscle. Wolpe argued that two incompatible states cannot occur together: if a response incompatible with anxiety, such as deep muscle relaxation, is made to occur in the presence of an anxiety-evoking stimulus, the bond between that stimulus and anxiety is weakened. Systematic desensitisation has three steps. First, the client learns relaxation, usually a short form of Edmund Jacobson's progressive muscle relaxation (1929). Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, ranking feared scenes from mild to severe, often rated in subjective units of distress (SUDS). Third, while deeply relaxed, the client imagines each scene in turn and moves up only when the current scene no longer brings anxiety. Example: Pooja, afraid of injections, relaxes while picturing a syringe lying on a table, then a nurse holding it, and finally the needle touching her arm. Option d is right because systematic desensitisation is built to pair a relaxed state with feared stimuli so that relaxation inhibits anxiety. Exam trap: exposure methods such as flooding and exposure and response prevention also reduce fear, but they work through habituation and extinction, with no competing response, so they do not rest on reciprocal inhibition.", "topic": "Wolpe's reciprocal inhibition", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "Who wrote The Divided Self?", "options": {"a": "R. D. Laing", "b": "Thomas Szasz", "c": "Erving Goffman", "d": "David Cooper"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: The Divided Self: An Existential Study in Sanity and Madness (1960) was written by R. D. Laing (Ronald David Laing), a Scottish psychiatrist. Laing tried to make schizophrenia understandable as a human experience rather than treating it only as a brain disease. He described ontological insecurity: a person who lacks a firm sense of being real, whole and alive feels constantly threatened by others, fearing being swallowed up (engulfment), being invaded (implosion) or being turned into a thing (petrification). To protect a hidden 'true self', such a person builds a false-self system that complies with others on the surface, and in psychosis this split can break down. In Sanity, Madness and the Family (1964), written with Aaron Esterson, Laing argued that the speech of patients diagnosed with schizophrenia made sense once their family communication was studied. He became a leading figure of anti-psychiatry, alongside Thomas Szasz (1961) and David Cooper (1967), a movement that questioned diagnosis, forced treatment and the mental hospital. Example: Ananya, who seems compliant at home but describes feeling hollow and unreal inside, is the kind of case Laing would read through the false self. Option a is right because Laing wrote The Divided Self. Exam trap: all four options are linked with the critique of psychiatry, so examiners swap their books: The Myth of Mental Illness (1961) is Szasz's, Asylums (1961) is Erving Goffman's, and Psychiatry and Anti-Psychiatry (1967) is Cooper's.", "topic": "Laing and anti-psychiatry", "fig": "R. D. Laing"}, {"q": "Which concept was given by Alfred Adler?", "options": {"a": "Collective unconscious", "b": "Basic anxiety", "c": "Inferiority complex", "d": "Oedipus complex"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: the inferiority complex is Alfred Adler's concept. Adler, founder of individual psychology, broke with Sigmund Freud in 1911. In Study of Organ Inferiority (1907) he argued that people compensate for weak organs, and he soon widened the idea: every child starts small, weak and dependent, so feelings of inferiority are universal and normal. They are the engine of growth, driving a striving for superiority, which Adler later called striving for perfection, meaning completion and mastery rather than dominance. Compensation overcomes a weakness; overcompensation goes beyond it, as when a stammering boy becomes a fine orator. When the feelings are too strong, for instance after organ inferiority, pampering or neglect, they harden into an inferiority complex: a lasting sense of being inadequate that leads the person to give up or hide behind excuses. Its mask can be a superiority complex of boasting and belittling others. Adler also gave style of life, social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefühl), fictional finalism, the creative self and birth order. Example: Vikram, mocked for his height, feels so worthless that he stops trying out for any team; that is an inferiority complex rather than healthy compensation. Option c is right because Adler coined the inferiority complex and built his theory around inferiority feelings. Exam trap: all four are famous psychodynamic terms, so examiners mix up the neo-Freudians: the collective unconscious belongs to Carl Jung, who split from Freud in 1913; basic anxiety to Karen Horney (1937); and the Oedipus complex to Freud himself.", "topic": "Adler's inferiority complex", "fig": "Alfred Adler"}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT a scale of the MMPI?", "options": {"a": "Phobia", "b": "Hypochondriasis", "c": "Psychasthenia", "d": "Hypomania"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: there is no Phobia scale on the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI). Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) built the MMPI by empirical criterion keying: an item was kept if it separated a diagnosed patient group from normal controls, whatever its content. The original test has ten clinical scales, numbered for profile coding: 1 Hypochondriasis (Hs), 2 Depression (D), 3 Hysteria (Hy), 4 Psychopathic deviate (Pd), 5 Masculinity-femininity (Mf), 6 Paranoia (Pa), 7 Psychasthenia (Pt), 8 Schizophrenia (Sc), 9 Hypomania (Ma) and 0 Social introversion (Si). The validity scales (? or Cannot Say, L for Lie, F for Infrequency, K for Correction) check how the person approached the test. Phobic fears are picked up by Psychasthenia, an old term from Pierre Janet (1903) for anxiety, obsessions, compulsions, phobias and excessive doubt; today scale 7 is read as a measure of general anxiety and rumination. The MMPI-2 (1989) kept the same ten clinical scales. Example: Sameer's profile peaks on scale 7, so the psychologist explores his worry and obsessional doubt; there is no separate phobia score to look up. Option a is right because no MMPI clinical or validity scale is named Phobia. Exam trap: phobia sounds like an obvious clinical category, so students assume it must have a scale, while psychasthenia, an unfamiliar word, looks like the invented option; in fact psychasthenia is the real scale and it is the one that covers phobias.", "topic": "MMPI clinical scales", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Resting tremor is characteristic of:", "options": {"a": "Huntington's disease", "b": "Cerebellar disease", "c": "Essential tremor", "d": "Parkinson's disease"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: resting tremor is the classic sign of Parkinson's disease. James Parkinson described the disorder in An Essay on the Shaking Palsy (1817). A resting tremor appears when the limb is relaxed and supported, for example lying in the lap, and lessens or disappears when the person moves it on purpose. In Parkinson's it is typically a slow, rhythmic 'pill-rolling' movement of thumb and fingers, about 4 to 6 beats per second, often starting on one side. The other core signs are rigidity (often 'cogwheel', a ratchety resistance when the limb is moved), bradykinesia (slowness and poverty of movement, with a masked face and small handwriting) and, later, postural instability with a shuffling gait. The cause is the loss of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra, which feed the striatum through the nigrostriatal pathway; surviving neurons contain Lewy bodies. L-dopa, a precursor the brain turns into dopamine, eases the symptoms. Example: Mr Iyer's right hand trembles while he sits watching television but steadies when he reaches for his cup of tea. Option d is right because a tremor at rest that eases on action is the hallmark of Parkinson's disease. Exam trap: examiners contrast resting tremor with intention tremor, which worsens as the hand approaches a target and points to the cerebellum, and with the postural or action tremor of essential tremor, seen when holding a cup or a newspaper.", "topic": "Tremor types: Parkinson's disease", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The ratio of the light reflected from a surface to the total light falling on it is called:", "options": {"a": "Reflectance coefficient", "b": "Brightness index", "c": "Luminance", "d": "Albedo"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the proportion of the light falling on a surface that the surface reflects is called its albedo (reflectance). Albedo is a property of the object itself. White paper reflects most of the light that falls on it (roughly 80 to 90 percent) and black paper very little (well under 10 percent), whatever the lighting. Luminance, by contrast, is the actual amount of light coming from the surface to the eye, which depends on the illumination multiplied by the albedo. So a black sheet in bright sunlight can send more light to the eye than a white sheet in a dim room, yet the black sheet still looks black and the white one white. This is lightness constancy. Hans Wallach (1948) explained it with the ratio principle: we judge lightness from the ratio between the light coming from a surface and the light from its surroundings; a change in illumination changes both by the same factor, so the ratio, and with it perceived lightness, stays the same. Perceived lightness therefore tracks albedo, not luminance. Brightness is the experienced intensity of light, a term used mainly for light sources. Example: Farhan's white kurta looks white in the noon sun and under a weak bulb at night, because its albedo has not changed. Option d is right because albedo is the textbook name for this reflectance ratio in perception. Exam trap: luminance sounds right because it is about light from a surface, but it is an absolute amount that varies with the lighting, not a ratio. Key note: 'reflectance' and albedo mean the same thing, so a looks close; but 'reflectance coefficient' is not the standard term in perception texts, and albedo is, so d stands.", "topic": "Albedo and lightness constancy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Paivio's dual-code hypothesis states that memory is retained better when information is:", "options": {"a": "Coded both in words and as a mental image", "b": "Rehearsed over and over", "c": "Processed for its meaning rather than its sound", "d": "Recalled in the same setting where it was learnt"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Allan Paivio's dual-code hypothesis states that information is remembered better when it is coded both in words and as a mental image. Paivio (1971), in Imagery and Verbal Processes, proposed two separate but linked memory systems. The verbal system stores language units and the non-verbal imagery system stores mental pictures and other sensory images; in his later account (1986) Paivio called these units logogens (a term borrowed from John Morton) and imagens. Concrete words such as 'elephant' or 'mango' easily call up an image, so they receive two memory codes, a word and a picture. Abstract words such as 'justice' are stored mainly in the verbal code. Two codes give two routes to retrieval, so if one fails the other can still bring the item back. This explains the concreteness effect (concrete words are recalled better than abstract ones) and the picture superiority effect (pictures are recalled better than their names), and it underlies imagery mnemonics such as the method of loci. Example: Tanvi learns the word 'stethoscope' while picturing her doctor wearing one, so she has both a verbal and a visual trace. Option a is right because dual coding means storing the same item in both the verbal and the imagery system. Exam trap: the other options are rival memory principles that examiners attach to the wrong name: meaning-based processing is Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart's levels of processing (1972), and recall being best in the learning setting is Endel Tulving and Donald Thomson's encoding specificity (1973). Key note: the original recall gave the answer as 'visual and auditory encoding'; Paivio's two codes are verbal and imaginal (non-verbal), not sight and hearing, and the verbal code covers words whether heard or read, so a stands.", "topic": "Paivio's dual coding theory", "fig": "Allan Paivio"}, {"q": "Which of these is a functional hallucination?", "options": {"a": "Mistaking a rope for a snake in low light", "b": "Hearing voices only when the fan is on", "c": "Seeing a shadow in the dark and believing it is a ghost", "d": "Seeing flashes of colour whenever music plays"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: hearing voices only when the fan is on is a functional hallucination. In descriptive psychopathology, as set out in Frank Fish's Clinical Psychopathology (1967), a functional hallucination is triggered by a real stimulus in one sense, and the hallucination is experienced in that same sense, alongside the real stimulus. The patient hears the whirr of the fan and, at the same time, hears voices; when the fan is switched off, the voices stop. The real noise is not misperceived; it is heard normally, and the voice is an extra perception that comes and goes with it. This separates a functional hallucination from an illusion, in which a real object is itself misperceived and turned into something else. A related form is the reflex hallucination, in which a stimulus in one sense produces a hallucination in a different sense (seeing flashes of colour whenever music plays), a morbid form of synaesthesia. Example: Suresh, who has schizophrenia, reports that a man's voice abuses him every time the tap runs, while he also hears the water clearly. Fish also described extracampine hallucinations (outside the normal sensory field, such as voices from another city) and hypnagogic and hypnopompic ones (on falling asleep and on waking). Option b is right because the voices need the real fan noise to appear, occur in the same sense of hearing, and are heard together with it. Exam trap: the closest distractor is d, because it too needs a real trigger; but the colours appear in vision while the trigger is heard, which makes it a reflex hallucination, whereas a functional hallucination stays in the same sense as its trigger. Mistaking a rope for a snake is the textbook illusion, since the rope itself is misperceived.", "topic": "Functional versus reflex hallucination", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The temporary visual impairment felt on coming out of a dark room into bright light is called:", "options": {"a": "Night blindness", "b": "Dark adaptation", "c": "Light adaptation", "d": "Afterimage"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the brief glare and poor vision felt on stepping from a dark room into bright light is light adaptation. In the dark, the eye has made itself as sensitive as possible: the pupil is wide and the rods, which work in dim light, are loaded with their pigment, rhodopsin. Sudden bright light floods this over-sensitive system, so for a moment everything looks dazzling and washed out. The eye then recovers quickly. The pupil constricts, rhodopsin is bleached so the rods drop out, and the cones, which work in bright light and give colour and fine detail, take over. Within about a minute or less, vision is comfortable again. Dark adaptation is the reverse process and is much slower. On entering a dark cinema, sensitivity rises over about 20 to 30 minutes: the cones adapt within the first 7 to 10 minutes, then the curve shows a rod-cone break and the rods go on gaining sensitivity as rhodopsin regenerates. Example: Aditi walks out of a matinee into the afternoon sun and squints for a few seconds before she can read the signboards. Option c is right because the temporary impairment on moving into brightness is the eye adjusting to light. Exam trap: students pick dark adaptation because they read 'dark room', but the name follows the new condition the eye must adjust to, which here is light.", "topic": "Light and dark adaptation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In Zuckerman's Alternative Five-Factor Model (AFFM), which factor is NOT similar to a factor in Costa and McCrae's Big Five?", "options": {"a": "Activity", "b": "Hostility", "c": "Neuroticism", "d": "Sociability"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: in Marvin Zuckerman's Alternative Five-Factor Model (1993), Activity is the factor with no matching factor in Paul Costa and Robert McCrae's Big Five (1992). Zuckerman, with Michael Kuhlman and colleagues, built the alternative five from traits he thought had a clear biological basis, and measured them with the Zuckerman-Kuhlman Personality Questionnaire (ZKPQ). Its five factors are Neuroticism-Anxiety, Aggression-Hostility, Impulsive Sensation Seeking, Sociability and Activity. Three of them line up with Big Five factors: Neuroticism-Anxiety with Neuroticism, Sociability with Extraversion, and Aggression-Hostility with low Agreeableness. Impulsive Sensation Seeking relates to low Conscientiousness. Activity, meaning a need for constant activity, a liking for hard and challenging work and restlessness when idle, has no factor of its own in the Big Five; there it appears only as one facet of Extraversion (facet E4, Activity, in the NEO PI-R). The alternative five also has no counterpart to Openness to experience, which Zuckerman did not regard as a basic, biologically based trait, so Activity, rather than Openness, completes his five. Example: Karan cannot sit still on holidays and fills every free hour with tasks; he would score high on ZKPQ Activity. Option a is right because Activity is the alternative factor that is not one of the Big Five. Exam trap: 'Hostility' looks foreign to the Big Five because no Big Five factor carries that name, but it maps onto the low pole of Agreeableness. Key note: the original recall marked a as 'unsure', and later studies place Activity partly with Extraversion; but Activity is the factor with no matching Big Five factor, while hostility, neuroticism and sociability each map onto one, so a stands.", "topic": "Zuckerman's Alternative Five", "fig": "Marvin Zuckerman"}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT one of the trigunas?", "options": {"a": "Purity", "b": "Inertia", "c": "Energy", "d": "Spirituality"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: spirituality is not one of the three gunas. In Samkhya philosophy, reality has two principles: purusha, pure consciousness, and prakriti, matter or nature. Prakriti is woven from three strands or qualities, the trigunas, which are always present together in varying proportions. Sattva is purity, goodness, light, harmony and knowledge. Rajas is energy, activity, passion, desire and restlessness. Tamas is inertia, darkness, lethargy, dullness and ignorance. The Bhagavad Gita applies them to personality (chapter 14, then chapters 17 and 18): whichever guna dominates colours a person's thinking, food, work and choices. Indian psychologists have built measures from this idea, such as the Vedic Personality Inventory (David Wolf, 1999), which scores sattva, rajas and tamas. Spiritual liberation (moksha) is described as going beyond all three gunas; a person who has done so is called gunatita, 'beyond the gunas'. Spirituality is therefore a goal that transcends the gunas, not a fourth guna. Example: on the night before an exam, Ananya revises calmly and sleeps on time (sattva), Rohit stays up anxiously cramming (rajas), and Dev oversleeps and skips revision (tamas). Option d is right because purity, energy and inertia are the English names of sattva, rajas and tamas, and spirituality matches none of them. Exam trap: the options are English translations, so students who know only the Sanskrit names may fail to recognise purity as sattva and pick it as the odd one out. Key note: the original recall marked a (Purity), but purity is sattva itself, one of the three; spirituality is the only option that is not a guna, so d stands.", "topic": "The trigunas of Samkhya", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of these does NOT help to reduce social loafing?", "options": {"a": "Assigning general tasks", "b": "Encouraging interpersonal relationships between members", "c": "Individual accountability", "d": "Maintaining the number of members"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: assigning general, vague tasks does not reduce social loafing; it makes loafing worse. Social loafing is the tendency to put in less effort when working in a group whose output is pooled than when working alone. Max Ringelmann's rope-pulling studies (published 1913) first showed that the more people pulled together, the less each one pulled. Bibb Latane, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) named the effect: students asked to clap and shout as loudly as they could made less noise per person as the group grew, even when they only believed others were joining in. Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993), in their collective effort model, explained that people work hard when they expect their own effort to matter and to lead to outcomes they value. Loafing therefore falls when each person's contribution is identifiable and evaluated, when the task is meaningful, specific and challenging, when members have distinct roles, when the group is small, and when members care about the group and one another. Example: in a college project, Ishaan does almost nothing when the lecturer says 'the group will prepare a report', but works hard once each member is given a named chapter that will be marked separately. Option a is right because general tasks hide individual effort and remove personal responsibility, the very conditions that breed loafing. Exam trap: in this NOT question, the three helpful options sound like ordinary group advice, so students search them for a flaw; the option that fails is the vague, shared task. Key note: 'maintaining the number of members' is loosely worded; read as keeping the group small and fixed (not letting it grow), it reduces loafing, since loafing rises with group size; so a stands.", "topic": "Reducing social loafing", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of these dysfunctional thoughts will maintain OCD?", "options": {"a": "Everyone gets intrusive thoughts occasionally", "b": "I can distract myself and let the thought pass", "c": "Thinking about hurting someone is just as bad as doing it", "d": "It is okay to have strange thoughts as long as I don't act on them"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: 'thinking about hurting someone is just as bad as doing it' is a dysfunctional belief that maintains OCD. The cognitive model of OCD starts from a surprising finding: Stanley Rachman and Padmal de Silva (1978) showed that most people without OCD, nearly 80 percent in their sample, have unwanted intrusive thoughts much like clinical obsessions. Paul Salkovskis (1985) argued that the difference lies not in the thought but in how it is appraised. A person who reads the intrusion as meaningful, dangerous and a sign of personal responsibility for harm becomes anxious and feels driven to neutralise it through checking, praying, reassurance seeking or other compulsions. Neutralising brings brief relief, so it is negatively reinforced, and it stops the person from learning that the thought was harmless. Thought-action fusion (Roz Shafran, Dana Thordarson and Stanley Rachman, 1996) names two such beliefs: moral fusion, that having a bad thought is morally equal to doing the act, and likelihood fusion, that thinking of an event makes it more likely to happen. Other appraisals include inflated responsibility, overimportance of thoughts and the need to control them. Example: Nikhil has a flash of pushing his baby niece down the stairs, decides he must be a terrible person, and now avoids holding her. Option c is right because it is moral thought-action fusion, an appraisal that turns a normal intrusion into an obsession. Exam trap: a, b and d sound like the calm self-talk a therapist would teach, so in a 'which maintains' question they are the healthy answers, not the problem.", "topic": "Cognitive model of OCD", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of these would work against social anxiety disorder?", "options": {"a": "Post-event processing", "b": "Memory biases", "c": "Reduced self-focused attention", "d": "Avoiding the situation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: reduced self-focused attention would work against social anxiety disorder, because heightened self-focus is one of its main maintaining processes. David Clark and Adrian Wells (1995) proposed the leading cognitive model of social anxiety. When a socially anxious person enters a feared situation, beliefs such as 'I will look stupid' are activated and attention turns inward. The person monitors a racing heart, trembling and blushing, and uses these feelings to build a distorted image of how he looks to others, often pictured from an observer's point of view. He also uses safety behaviours, such as avoiding eye contact, gripping a glass tightly or rehearsing sentences, which can make him seem odd and stop him learning that disaster would not occur. After the event, post-event processing replays the encounter in detail and recalls it as worse than it was, helped by memory biases for negative evaluation. Avoiding situations altogether blocks any chance of disconfirming the fears. Clark's cognitive therapy for social anxiety disorder therefore trains attention outward onto the task and other people, drops safety behaviours and uses video feedback to correct the self-image. Example: Divya, giving a seminar, stops checking whether her voice shakes and looks at her listeners' faces; her anxiety falls and she notices they are interested. Option c is right because cutting down self-focused attention removes a core maintaining factor. Exam trap: in a 'works against' question, students sometimes pick avoidance because it lowers anxiety in the moment, but avoidance maintains the disorder over time.", "topic": "Clark and Wells social anxiety model", "fig": "David Clark and Adrian Wells"}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT associated with PTSD?", "options": {"a": "Flashbacks", "b": "Elevated mood", "c": "Avoidance", "d": "Hypervigilance"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: elevated mood is not a feature of post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). PTSD can follow exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence, whether the person lives through it, witnesses it, learns that it happened to someone close, or meets its grim details again and again at work. In DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013; kept in DSM-5-TR, 2022) the symptoms fall into four clusters lasting more than one month. Intrusion: unwanted memories, nightmares and flashbacks, in which the person feels or acts as if the event were happening again. Avoidance: keeping away from thoughts, places or people that bring it back. Negative changes in thinking and mood: blame, fear, guilt, detachment, gaps in memory for the event, and a persistent inability to feel positive emotions. Changes in arousal and reactivity: hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, irritability, poor concentration and poor sleep. If symptoms last only 3 days to 1 month, the diagnosis is acute stress disorder instead. ICD-11 (World Health Organization) keeps a shorter core: re-experiencing in the here and now, avoidance, and a persistent sense of current threat. Example: after a bus crash, Arjun relives the skid in flashbacks, takes long detours to avoid the highway and jumps at every horn, yet he cannot feel happy even at his sister's wedding. Option b is right because PTSD pushes mood down and numbs joy; a high, euphoric or expansive mood belongs to a manic or hypomanic episode, the opposite pole. Exam trap: emotional numbing and the inability to feel positive emotions are PTSD symptoms, so a decoy such as 'loss of pleasure' actually belongs; only raised, elated mood is out.", "topic": "PTSD symptom clusters", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A person has an enhanced memory of an object, as if he were still seeing it when it is no longer there. This is:", "options": {"a": "Afterimage", "b": "Eidetic imagery", "c": "Hallucination", "d": "Flashbulb memory"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: a vivid image of an object that a person still 'sees' after it has gone is eidetic imagery. In eidetic imagery (popularly called photographic memory) a person looks at a picture for some seconds, and after it is removed he goes on seeing a detailed image of it, as if it were still out there on the blank surface. He can move his eyes over the image and read off details, such as the number of stripes on a cat's tail, and the image keeps the original colours. It lasts from some seconds to a few minutes and then fades. The German psychologist Erich Jaensch studied it in the 1920s. Ralph Haber and Ruth Haber (1964) tested children in an American elementary school and found true eidetic imagery in only about 8 percent of them; in adults it is rarer still. Example: Meera, aged 8, glances at a picture of a market scene; the card is taken away, yet she keeps pointing at the empty board, describing the colour of each fruit as if it were still in front of her. Option b is right because the stem describes an image of a seen object that persists after the object is removed and is experienced as still being in front of the eyes. Exam trap: an afterimage also outlasts the stimulus. But an afterimage is a brief retinal effect after staring, often in the complementary colour, and it moves when the eyes move; an eidetic image keeps the original's colours and detail, stays put on the surface and can be scanned like a real picture.", "topic": "Eidetic imagery", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A girl is nervous about a presentation, but tells herself, \"I have done this before, so I will be able to do it again.\" In Lazarus's theory this is:", "options": {"a": "Primary appraisal", "b": "Secondary appraisal", "c": "Reappraisal", "d": "Emotion-focused coping"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: telling herself 'I have done this before, so I can do it again' is secondary appraisal. Richard Lazarus (1966), and later Lazarus with Susan Folkman (1984) in the transactional model of stress, argued that stress does not lie in an event itself but in how a person appraises the relation between the event and his resources. Primary appraisal asks 'Does this matter to me, and how?' The answer may be irrelevant, benign-positive or stressful; a stressful event is judged as harm or loss (already done), threat (harm to come) or challenge (a chance to gain). Secondary appraisal asks 'What can I do about it? Are my coping resources and options enough?' Stress is greatest when demands look high and resources look thin. Reappraisal is a later revision of either judgement as new information comes in. Coping follows: problem-focused coping changes the situation (preparing slides, rehearsing), while emotion-focused coping regulates the feeling (deep breathing, venting, distraction). Example: Riya's stomach knots before her seminar presentation; that sense of threat is primary appraisal. Then she recalls the three seminars she has already given, judges that her skills are enough, and calms down. Option b is right because her self-talk weighs her own past success and ability, that is, her coping resources, which is exactly what secondary appraisal evaluates. Exam trap: 'secondary' does not mean 'happening later' or 'less important'; the two appraisals run together and feed each other. Her nervousness alone would be primary appraisal, and a calming thought is not automatically emotion-focused coping.", "topic": "Lazarus primary and secondary appraisal", "fig": "Richard Lazarus"}, {"q": "A man sees a bear, his body is aroused, he runs and only then feels fear. Which theory of emotion does this show?", "options": {"a": "Cannon-Bard", "b": "James-Lange", "c": "Schachter-Singer", "d": "Lazarus"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: seeing the bear, being aroused, running, and only then feeling fear is the James-Lange theory. Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid and therefore run. William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885), working separately, reversed the order: the stimulus first sets off bodily changes and actions, and the emotion is our perception of those changes. In James's words, we feel sorry because we cry, angry because we strike, afraid because we tremble. So each emotion should have its own pattern of bodily arousal. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked this: the viscera react slowly, much the same arousal comes with very different emotions, and injecting adrenaline does not produce a true emotion. He proposed instead, backed by the animal studies of Philip Bard (1928), that the thalamus sends signals at the same moment to the cortex (the feeling) and to the body (the arousal). Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer (1962) added cognition: arousal plus a label drawn from the situation produces the emotion (the two-factor theory). Richard Lazarus (1991) put appraisal first. Example: Kabir hears a growl on a forest trail, his heart races and he sprints, and only when he notices his pounding heart and shaking legs does he feel terror. Option b is right because the stem places arousal and action before the feeling, and makes the feeling a reading of the body. Exam trap: in Cannon-Bard, arousal and feeling happen at the same time and independently; if an option says 'simultaneously', it is Cannon-Bard, and if it adds a cognitive label for the arousal, it is Schachter-Singer.", "topic": "James-Lange theory of emotion", "fig": "William James and Carl Lange"}, {"q": "Which nerve carries visual information from the eyes to the brain?", "options": {"a": "Oculomotor nerve", "b": "Trochlear nerve", "c": "Optic nerve", "d": "Abducens nerve"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the optic nerve carries visual information from the eye to the brain. The optic nerve is cranial nerve II. It is made of the axons of the retinal ganglion cells, the output cells of the retina, and it leaves the back of the eye at the optic disc, which has no rods or cones and so forms the blind spot. The two optic nerves meet at the optic chiasm, where fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross to the other side, while fibres from the temporal halves stay on the same side. Beyond the chiasm the fibres run as the optic tracts to the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus, and the optic radiations then carry the signal to the primary visual cortex (V1) in the occipital lobe. As a result, the left visual field ends up in the right hemisphere. Option c is right because the optic nerve is the only sensory nerve for vision among the four; the other three move the eyeball. Exam trap: the oculomotor (III), trochlear (IV) and abducens (VI) nerves all supply the eye, so they sound visual, but they are motor nerves that aim the eye and carry no image. A handy memory aid for the eye muscles is 'LR6, SO4, the rest 3': lateral rectus by VI, superior oblique by IV, all the others by III.", "topic": "Optic nerve and visual pathway", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Paradoxical intention is a part of which therapy?", "options": {"a": "Behaviour therapy", "b": "Existential therapy", "c": "Logotherapy", "d": "Psychoanalysis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: paradoxical intention is a technique of logotherapy. Logotherapy is the school of the Viennese psychiatrist Viktor Frankl (Man's Search for Meaning, 1946), who survived the Nazi concentration camps. Its central idea is the will to meaning: people are driven most deeply by the search for meaning, and an 'existential vacuum' breeds distress. It is often called the third Viennese school of psychotherapy, after psychoanalysis and individual psychology. Frankl first described paradoxical intention in print in 1939. It targets anticipatory anxiety, the vicious circle in which fear of a symptom brings on the symptom. The patient is asked to wish for, or deliberately try to produce, the very thing he fears, ideally with humour; the fear loses its grip because one cannot fear and intend the same thing at once. The technique draws on the human capacity for self-distancing. Its partner technique is dereflection, which turns attention away from oneself and onto something meaningful. Example: Rohan cannot sleep because he worries that he will not sleep; told to lie in bed and try to stay awake as long as he can, he soon drifts off. Option c is right because paradoxical intention is Frankl's own named logotherapy technique. Exam trap: behaviour therapists later tested it, for instance L. Michael Ascher and Jay Efran (1978) in a programme for sleep-onset insomnia, so behaviour therapy looks tempting; but they borrowed it rather than invented it. Key note: logotherapy is itself one of the existential therapies, so b has a case; but paradoxical intention belongs specifically to Frankl's logotherapy, the more precise answer, so c stands.", "topic": "Logotherapy: paradoxical intention", "fig": "Viktor Frankl"}, {"q": "In the Ponzo illusion, two equal lines look unequal because:", "options": {"a": "The arrowhead fins at their ends make one look longer", "b": "The size of the circles around them makes one look larger", "c": "Vertical lines are overestimated compared with horizontal ones", "d": "The converging lines act as a depth cue, so the line seen as farther away looks larger"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: in the Ponzo illusion the converging lines act as a depth cue, so the bar seen as farther away looks longer. The figure is named after the Italian psychologist Mario Ponzo (1912; some books give 1911 or 1913). It shows two identical horizontal bars placed between two lines that converge towards the top, like railway tracks running into the distance. The upper bar, nearer the point where the lines meet, looks longer. The usual explanation is misapplied size constancy, argued by Richard Gregory (1963). Converging lines are the linear perspective cue to depth, so the visual system treats the upper bar as farther away. Normally an object that is farther away casts a smaller image on the retina, and the brain scales it up so that its size seems constant. Here both bars cast the same retinal image, so the one registered as farther is scaled up and looks bigger. The moon illusion is often explained along similar lines. Example: Ananya lays two equal matchsticks across a photograph of railway tracks; the one near the horizon looks longer, although a ruler shows they are the same. Option d is right because it names both the depth cue and the size scaling that produce the effect. Exam trap: each distractor is a real illusion with its own cause, so match the figure to the name: arrowhead fins mean Muller-Lyer, surrounding circles mean Ebbinghaus, and an inverted T means the vertical-horizontal illusion.", "topic": "Ponzo illusion and size constancy", "fig": "Mario Ponzo"}, {"q": "The phi phenomenon is best explained as:", "options": {"a": "Real movement", "b": "Induced movement", "c": "Autokinetic effect", "d": "Apparent movement"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the phi phenomenon is a form of apparent movement. Apparent movement is the perception of motion when nothing actually moves. Max Wertheimer (1912), working in Frankfurt with Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka as his observers, flashed two lines one after the other in slightly different places. When the gap between the flashes was very short (about 30 milliseconds) the lines looked simultaneous; when it was long (about 200 milliseconds) they looked like two separate flashes; but at about 60 milliseconds a single line seemed to move from one place to the other. Because the motion lay in neither stimulus, it could not be built up from separate sensations, and the paper founded Gestalt psychology, with its claim that the whole is different from the sum of its parts. The same principle runs the cinema (a series of still frames), flipbooks and the 'running' lights of a shop sign. Example: at a Diwali market, Ishaan watches a string of bulbs that only switch on and off in sequence, yet he sees a single light chasing round the frame. Option d is right because phi is motion seen without any physical movement, which is the definition of apparent (stroboscopic) movement. Exam trap: induced movement and the autokinetic effect are also illusions of motion, so the three non-real options look alike; but phi alone depends on separate stimuli flashed in quick succession.", "topic": "Phi phenomenon and apparent movement", "fig": "Max Wertheimer"}, {"q": "In Atkinson's theory of achievement motivation, a person whose motive to succeed is stronger than his motive to avoid failure prefers tasks that are:", "options": {"a": "Very easy", "b": "Very hard", "c": "Of moderate difficulty", "d": "Of any difficulty, so long as the reward is high"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: a person whose motive to succeed is stronger than his motive to avoid failure prefers tasks of moderate difficulty. John W. Atkinson (1957, 1964) built a formal theory on David McClelland's need for achievement, which McClelland and colleagues (1953) scored from Thematic Apperception Test stories. The tendency to approach success equals Ms x Ps x Is: the motive to succeed (Ms), the subjective probability of success (Ps) and the incentive value of success (Is). Atkinson set Is = 1 minus Ps, because success on a hard task brings more pride. So Ps x (1 minus Ps) is largest when Ps = 0.5: it is 0.25 there, but only 0.09 when Ps is 0.9 or 0.1. A matching tendency to avoid failure is driven by the motive to avoid failure (Maf, often measured as test anxiety), and it is also strongest at moderate difficulty, where failure is both likely and shameful. When Ms is stronger than Maf the net pull is towards moderate risks; when Maf is stronger the person retreats to very easy tasks (success is sure) or very hard ones (failure is no disgrace). In the ring-toss study of Atkinson and George Litwin (1960), students high in achievement motive and low in test anxiety mostly threw from a middle distance. Example: in a quiz, Aditya, who loves a challenge, picks the medium questions, while his anxious friend chooses only the easiest or the near-impossible ones. Option c is right because moderate difficulty gives the most motivating mix of a fair chance and real pride. Exam trap: the U-shaped choice of very easy or very hard tasks belongs to the failure-avoidant person, not the success-oriented one.", "topic": "Atkinson's achievement motivation theory", "fig": "John W. Atkinson"}, {"q": "A girl scores 130 on an IQ test with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. What is her z score?", "options": {"a": "1.5", "b": "2", "c": "2.5", "d": "3"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: her z score is 2. A z score tells how many standard deviations a raw score lies above or below the mean: z = (X minus mean) / SD. Here z = (130 minus 100) / 15 = 30 / 15 = 2. A positive z lies above the mean, a negative one below it, and the mean itself has z = 0. Because z scores always have a mean of 0 and an SD of 1, they let us compare scores from tests with quite different scales. On the normal curve about 68 percent of scores fall within 1 SD of the mean, about 95 percent within 2 SD and about 99.7 percent within 3 SD, so a z of 2 sits at roughly the 98th percentile (97.7). The same score is T = 50 + 10z = 70. Deviation IQs, introduced by David Wechsler (1939), use a mean of 100 and an SD of 15 for exactly this kind of reading. Example: Sneha's 130 puts her ahead of about 98 in every 100 children of her age. Option b is right because the 30-point gap above the mean is exactly two SDs of 15. Exam trap: the decoys come from slips in the arithmetic; dividing the 30-point gap by 10, the SD of the T scale, gives 3, and 1.5 or 2.5 would need scores of 122.5 or 137.5.", "topic": "Computing a z score", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of these gives the most information about the data?", "options": {"a": "Funnel plot", "b": "Scree plot", "c": "Box plot", "d": "Forest plot"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: of these four, the box plot gives the most information about a set of data. John Tukey (1977) introduced the box plot (box-and-whisker plot) in Exploratory Data Analysis. It draws the five-number summary: the minimum, the first quartile (Q1), the median, the third quartile (Q3) and the maximum. The box runs from Q1 to Q3, so its length is the interquartile range, the spread of the middle 50 percent of scores, and a line inside it marks the median. Whiskers reach out towards the lowest and highest scores; in Tukey's version they stop at the most extreme score lying within 1.5 interquartile ranges of the box, and scores beyond that are plotted as separate outlier points. In one small figure the reader sees the centre, the spread, the skew (a median off-centre in the box and one long whisker) and any outliers, and several boxes drawn side by side compare groups at a glance. Example: Dr Kavya plots anxiety scores for three hospital wards; one look shows that ward B has the highest median, the widest box and two extreme outliers. Option c is right because the box plot summarises the whole distribution of the data themselves. Exam trap: the other three are specialised plots that sound technical and therefore informative, but each answers one narrow question: publication bias (funnel), how many factors to keep (scree) or study effects (forest). Key note: forest and funnel plots also pack in a great deal, but about the studies in a meta-analysis rather than about one set of scores, and a scree plot shows only eigenvalues; among these four, the box plot is the one that describes a data distribution, so c stands.", "topic": "Box plot and other graphs", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A bow-shaped ROC curve suggests that the test:", "options": {"a": "Is no better than chance", "b": "Gives many false positives and nothing else", "c": "Is being used where the disorder is very common", "d": "Discriminates well between cases and non-cases"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a ROC curve that bows up towards the top left corner shows a test that discriminates well between cases and non-cases. The receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve comes from signal detection theory (David Green and John Swets, 1966). A test with a cut-off score gives, at every possible cut-off, a pair of values: sensitivity (the true positive rate, the share of real cases the test catches) and 1 minus specificity (the false positive rate, the share of non-cases wrongly flagged). The ROC curve plots sensitivity on the vertical axis against 1 minus specificity on the horizontal axis across all cut-offs. A useless test that merely guesses gives the straight diagonal from bottom left to top right, with an area under the curve (AUC) of 0.5. The more the curve bows towards the top left corner, where sensitivity is high and false positives are few, the better the test; a perfect test has an AUC of 1.0. A common rule of thumb reads 0.7 to 0.8 as acceptable, 0.8 to 0.9 as excellent and 0.9 or more as outstanding. Example: Dr Nikhil checks a new depression screening questionnaire against diagnostic interviews; its ROC curve hugs the top left corner with an AUC of 0.90, so it separates depressed from non-depressed patients well. Option d is right because the bow means that, across cut-offs, true positives rise much faster than false positives. Exam trap: prevalence changes the positive and negative predictive values, but sensitivity and specificity, and so the ROC curve, do not depend on how common the disorder is.", "topic": "ROC curve and discrimination", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of these does NOT come under specific learning disorder?", "options": {"a": "Dyspraxia", "b": "Dyslexia", "c": "Dyspnoea", "d": "Dyscalculia"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: dyspnoea (American spelling 'dyspnea') does not come under specific learning disorder; it means shortness of breath. In DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013), specific learning disorder is a neurodevelopmental disorder: persistent difficulty in learning and using academic skills for at least 6 months despite targeted help, with skills well below those expected for age, starting in the school years, and not better explained by intellectual disability, sensory problems, poor teaching or a language barrier. It is diagnosed with one or more specifiers. With impairment in reading covers word reading accuracy, reading rate and fluency, and comprehension; dyslexia is the accepted alternative name for this pattern. With impairment in written expression covers spelling, grammar and punctuation, and clarity of written work (often called dysgraphia). With impairment in mathematics covers number sense, memory for arithmetic facts, accurate calculation and mathematical reasoning; dyscalculia is the alternative name. Example: Tanvi, aged 9, has an average IQ but still reads single words slowly and hesitantly and often guesses at them, after two years of good teaching. Option c is right because dyspnoea is a respiratory symptom, listed among panic attack symptoms, and has nothing to do with learning. Exam trap: all four words begin with 'dys' (Greek for 'bad' or 'difficult'), so the odd one out hides by sound; ask what each ending means: '-lexia' words, '-calculia' calculation, '-praxia' action, '-pnoea' breathing. Key note: strictly, DSM-5 places dyspraxia under developmental coordination disorder, a motor disorder, not under specific learning disorder, though India's Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 lists dyspraxia with dyslexia, dysgraphia and dyscalculia under specific learning disabilities, and UK practice also groups it with the specific learning difficulties; dyspnoea is not a learning problem at all, so c stands.", "topic": "Specific learning disorder types", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In Sternberg's triarchic theory, practical intelligence is:", "options": {"a": "The ability to analyse, compare and evaluate", "b": "The ability to deal with new situations and create", "c": "The general factor running through all mental tests", "d": "The ability to adapt to, shape and select one's everyday environment"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: in the triarchic theory of Robert Sternberg (1985), practical intelligence is the ability to adapt to, shape and select one's everyday environment. In Beyond IQ, Sternberg argued that IQ tests catch only part of intelligence and proposed three linked aspects, later grouped as successful intelligence. Analytical (componential) intelligence analyses, compares, judges and evaluates; it is what school tests and IQ tests reward. Creative (experiential) intelligence deals with novel situations and turns new skills into automatic routines; it invents and imagines. Practical (contextual) intelligence applies knowledge to real life: adapting oneself to the environment, shaping the environment to suit oneself, or, when neither works, selecting a different environment. It leans on tacit knowledge, the unspoken know-how that is rarely taught (how to handle a difficult boss), the 'street smarts' of everyday life. A classic illustration comes from Brazil: Terezinha Nunes Carraher, David Carraher and Analucia Schliemann (1985) found that children selling goods in street markets did complex sums correctly while trading but failed the same sums set as school problems. Example: Farhan cannot study in his noisy hostel; he first tries earplugs (adapting), then persuades his roommates to keep quiet hours (shaping), and finally moves to the library (selecting). Option d is right because adapting, shaping and selecting are Sternberg's own three functions of contextual, practical intelligence. Exam trap: options a and b describe the other two arms of the same triarchic theory, so knowing the theory's name will not pick the answer; match analytical to evaluating, creative to novelty and practical to the everyday context.", "topic": "Sternberg's practical intelligence", "fig": "Robert Sternberg"}, {"q": "Functional fixedness is best described as:", "options": {"a": "A mental set that always helps in problem solving", "b": "A mental set that sometimes helps and sometimes complicates problem solving", "c": "A mental set that always complicates problem solving", "d": "A sudden insight that solves the problem at once"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: functional fixedness is a mental set that always complicates problem solving. It is the tendency to see an object only in its usual function, which blocks the solver from seeing a new use for it. Karl Duncker (1945) showed it with the candle problem: people were given a candle, a box of drawing pins and matches and asked to fix the candle to a wall so that it could burn. Far fewer people thought of emptying the box and pinning it up as a shelf when it was handed over full of pins than when it came empty, because a full box looks only like a container. Norman Maier's two-string problem (1931) is similar: the solution is to tie a pair of pliers to one string and swing it as a pendulum. Functional fixedness is one kind of mental set, a readiness to approach a problem in a fixed way. Abraham Luchins (1942) showed the broader set (Einstellung) with water jars: people drilled on a long formula kept using it even when a shorter one worked. Example: Pooja needs to tighten a loose screw and spends ten minutes hunting for a screwdriver, never thinking of the coin in her pocket. Option c is right because functional fixedness is by definition the obstacle, the failure to see beyond an object's familiar use. Exam trap: mental set in general can help (a method that worked before often works again), so b fits mental set but not the named concept of functional fixedness. Key note: some texts describe functional fixedness, like any mental set, as a habit that usually saves time; but as a named concept it is defined as an obstacle that blocks new uses of an object, so c stands.", "topic": "Functional fixedness and mental set", "fig": "Karl Duncker"}, {"q": "According to Guilford, creativity does NOT involve:", "options": {"a": "Fluency", "b": "Flexibility", "c": "Novelty", "d": "Originality"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: according to Joy Paul Guilford (1950), novelty is not one of the named components of creativity. Guilford used his 1950 American Psychological Association presidential address to launch the scientific study of creativity. In his structure of intellect model (1956, 1967) he separated convergent production (finding the one correct answer) from divergent production (generating many possible answers), and tied creativity to divergent thinking. He scored divergent production on fluency, the number of ideas produced; flexibility, the number of different categories the ideas come from, or the ease of shifting between them; originality, how unusual or statistically rare the ideas are; and elaboration, the amount of detail added. He also discussed sensitivity to problems and redefinition. E. Paul Torrance's Tests of Creative Thinking (1966) originally scored the same four. Example: asked for uses of a brick, Siddharth lists 12 (fluency) from building, defence, art and exercise (flexibility), including 'a pestle for grinding spices', which no one else in his class thought of (originality), and explains how he would shape it into a doorstop with a handle (elaboration). Option c is right because 'novelty' is not one of Guilford's factor names, while fluency, flexibility and originality are. Exam trap: novelty sounds almost like originality, and creativity is often defined as producing what is novel and useful, so examiners count on that overlap. Key note: novelty overlaps with originality in everyday meaning, so c can look like a trick; but it is not one of Guilford's factor names, while the other three are, so c stands.", "topic": "Guilford's divergent thinking factors", "fig": "Joy Paul Guilford"}, {"q": "Which drug is used in a narcoanalysis test to induce a semi-conscious state?", "options": {"a": "Lithium", "b": "Sodium pentothal", "c": "Haloperidol", "d": "Chlorpromazine"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: sodium pentothal is used in narcoanalysis to produce a semi-conscious state. Sodium pentothal is a trade name for thiopental sodium (thiopentone), a fast-acting barbiturate long used to start general anaesthesia. In narcoanalysis a small dose is injected slowly into a vein, with sodium amytal (amobarbital) as the usual alternative, until the person is drowsy but can still talk. In this twilight state inhibitions fall and people speak more freely, which earned it the popular name 'truth serum'. J. Stephen Horsley (1936) coined the term narcoanalysis and used it as a short cut in psychotherapy; in the Second World War it helped mute, stuporous or amnesic soldiers recall and abreact battle trauma. In fact drugged people can still lie, fantasise and be led by suggestion. In India investigators often put suspects through it, but in Selvi v. State of Karnataka (2010) the Supreme Court held that compulsory narcoanalysis, polygraph and brain-mapping tests violate Article 20(3), the right against self-incrimination, and Article 21, personal liberty. They may be given only with free consent, and the statements made cannot be used as evidence, though facts later discovered with the help of a voluntary test may be. Example: Raju, accused of theft, refuses a narcoanalysis test; under the Selvi ruling, the police cannot force it on him. Option b is right because a barbiturate such as sodium pentothal is what produces the drowsy, disinhibited state. Exam trap: haloperidol and chlorpromazine are sedating, so they sound plausible, but they are antipsychotics, not narcoanalysis agents.", "topic": "Narcoanalysis and sodium pentothal", "fig": ""}, {"q": "World No Tobacco Day is observed on:", "options": {"a": "7 April", "b": "31 May", "c": "10 October", "d": "26 June"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: World No Tobacco Day is observed every year on 31 May. The member states of the World Health Organization created it at the World Health Assembly in 1987 to draw attention to the death and disease caused by tobacco use. The first one was actually held on 7 April 1988, the 40th anniversary of WHO; from 1989 onward it has been held on 31 May. Each year WHO chooses a theme, such as protecting young people from the tobacco industry's tactics, and gives awards to people and groups who have done notable work against tobacco. In India, tobacco control rests on the Cigarettes and Other Tobacco Products Act (COTPA, 2003), which bans smoking in public places and tobacco advertising, and on the National Tobacco Control Programme, launched in 2007 to 2008. Option b is right because 31 May has been the fixed annual date since 1989. Exam trap: 7 April is World Health Day, marking the founding of WHO in 1948, and it was also the date of the very first No Tobacco Day in 1988, so it is the most dangerous decoy; 10 October is World Mental Health Day, and 26 June is the International Day against Drug Abuse and Illicit Trafficking.", "topic": "World No Tobacco Day", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In Tangney's Test of Self-Conscious Affect (TOSCA), alpha pride and beta pride differ in that:", "options": {"a": "Alpha pride is pride in one's whole self; beta pride is pride in a specific behaviour", "b": "Alpha pride is pride in a specific behaviour; beta pride is pride in one's whole self", "c": "Alpha pride is felt in public; beta pride is felt in private", "d": "Alpha pride is pride in oneself; beta pride is pride in one's group or family"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: alpha pride is pride in one's whole self, and beta pride is pride in a specific behaviour. The distinction comes from June Tangney (1989), whose Test of Self-Conscious Affect (TOSCA, first published with colleagues in 1989, revised as TOSCA-3 in 2000) describes everyday scenarios and asks how likely the person is to react in each of several listed ways. It builds on Helen Block Lewis (1971), who separated shame from guilt by the focus of the judgement. Shame is a negative judgement of the whole self ('I am a bad person'); guilt is a negative judgement of a specific act ('I did a bad thing'). Tangney applied the same split to pride. Alpha pride is pride focused on the global self ('I am brilliant'), the positive mirror of shame. Beta pride is pride focused on what one did ('I did that well'), the positive mirror of guilt. Besides the two pride scales, the TOSCA scores shame-proneness, guilt-proneness, externalisation and detachment. A related split came later from Jessica Tracy and Richard Robins (2007): authentic pride, tied to effort and achievement, and hubristic pride, a global, arrogant glorification of the self. Example: after Zoya tops her statistics exam, thinking 'I am a genius' is alpha pride, while 'my weekly practice paid off' is beta pride. Option a is right because the TOSCA scores pride by its focus: pride in the global self is alpha pride, and pride in a particular behaviour is beta pride. Exam trap: option b simply swaps the two; remember that alpha pairs with shame (whole self) and beta with guilt (specific act).", "topic": "Alpha and beta pride (TOSCA)", "fig": "June Tangney"}, {"q": "On a test, the 25th percentile (Q1) is 42 and the 75th percentile (Q3) is 66. What is the interquartile range?", "options": {"a": "24", "b": "12", "c": "54", "d": "108"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the interquartile range is 24. The interquartile range (IQR) is the distance between the third quartile and the first quartile: IQR = Q3 minus Q1 = 66 minus 42 = 24. Q1 is the score below which 25 percent of the group falls, and Q3 the score below which 75 percent falls, so the IQR is the spread of the middle 50 percent of scores. Because it ignores the top and bottom quarters, extreme scores barely affect it, which makes it the preferred measure of spread for skewed data, reported alongside the median. It is the length of the box in a box plot, and John Tukey (1977) used it to flag outliers: scores more than 1.5 x IQR beyond the quartiles, here below 42 minus 36 = 6 or above 66 + 36 = 102. Half the IQR is the quartile deviation, or semi-interquartile range: Q = (Q3 minus Q1) / 2 = 12. Example: if Harsh's class shows these quartiles, the middle half of his classmates scored between 42 and 66, a band 24 marks wide. Option a is right because the IQR is simply Q3 minus Q1, with no halving. Exam trap: examiners place the quartile deviation (12) next to the IQR (24); 'semi' means halve it, while plain 'interquartile range' does not.", "topic": "Interquartile range calculation", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"lgbrimh-2025": [{"q": "Who initiated the first systematic work with families in India?", "concept": "The answer is c: Vidya Sagar (1909 to 1978), a psychiatrist at Amritsar Mental Hospital, began the first systematic work with families in India. In the 1950s, as its medical superintendent, he faced an overcrowded hospital and let relatives stay with their patients in tents pitched on the hospital grounds. Family members lived alongside the patient, shared in the daily care, and joined regular group sessions that he held with patients and families together, often drawing on the Bhagavad Gita. Most patients improved and went home with families who had learnt how to look after them. He wrote little about it, so the work is known mostly through later accounts, yet he is often called the father of family therapy in India. It fits a wider Indian pattern: families here usually stay closely involved, and cross-cultural studies find low rates of high expressed emotion in Indian families. Example: when Arjun's mother camps beside his ward, she learns to spot early warning signs and keeps him on his medicine after he goes home. Option c is right because Vidya Sagar's Amritsar tent scheme is the recognised starting point of family work in India. Exam trap: examiners place other Indian pioneers beside him, so link 'first family work' only with Amritsar and the 1950s; Narendra Nath Wig is the name for community psychiatry, not family involvement. Key note: the recalled answer was spelt 'Vidyasagar'; the name is written both ways, and it is the same man, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Narendra Nath Wig, of PGIMER Chandigarh, is known for community psychiatry, such as the Raipur Rani project of the 1970s that trained rural health workers to give mental health care; he did not start family work.", "b": "Girindrasekhar Bose founded the Indian Psychoanalytical Society in 1922 and is the father of Indian psychoanalysis; his work was individual analysis, not bringing families into hospital care.", "d": "A. Venkoba Rao, of Madurai, is remembered for research on depression and suicide in India, not for starting family involvement in treatment."}}, {"q": "What does a partial eta squared value of 0.14 indicate?", "concept": "The answer is c: a partial eta squared of 0.14 indicates a large effect. Eta squared is an effect size for ANOVA: the proportion of variance in the dependent variable that a factor explains. Plain eta squared is SS effect divided by SS total. Partial eta squared is SS effect divided by (SS effect plus SS error): it removes the variance due to other factors from the denominator, so it tells how much of the variance left over belongs to this factor. Jacob Cohen (1988) gave the benchmarks: about 0.01 is small, 0.06 medium and 0.14 large. So 0.14 means the factor accounts for about 14 percent of the relevant variance, right at the large cut-off. Effect size is separate from significance: a p value only says how unlikely the result would be if there were no effect, the effect size says how big the effect is, and with a large sample even a tiny effect can be significant. Example: Meera compares three therapy groups on anxiety; if SS effect is 28 and SS error is 172, partial eta squared is 28 / 200 = 0.14, a large effect. Option c is right because 0.14 meets Cohen's threshold for a large effect. Exam trap: students mix these cut-offs with those for Cohen's d (0.2, 0.5, 0.8) or r (0.10, 0.30, 0.50) and call 0.14 small; for eta squared, 0.14 is large. Key note: Cohen gave these cut-offs for eta squared; using them for partial eta squared is common practice and what exams expect, though partial values run larger in designs with several factors, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "No effect would mean a value of about 0, the factor explaining none of the variance; 0.14 means about 14 percent is explained.", "b": "Small is Cohen's label for about 0.01; it is the trap for anyone reading 0.14 against the Cohen's d scale, where 0.2 is small.", "d": "Medium is Cohen's label for about 0.06; 0.14 is more than twice that and sits at the large cut-off."}}, {"q": "According to Watson, thinking is:", "concept": "The answer is a: for John B. Watson, thinking is subvocal speech, talking to oneself with tiny, hidden movements of the speech muscles. Watson founded behaviourism with his 1913 manifesto, 'Psychology as the behaviorist views it', and set out his view of thought fully in Behaviorism (1924 to 1925). He wanted psychology to study only observable behaviour, so he could not accept thought as an invisible mental event. His answer was peripheralism: thinking happens in the muscles at the periphery, not in a hidden mind. A child first talks aloud, then whispers, and finally the talk becomes implicit: faint movements of the larynx and tongue that could in principle be recorded with instruments. Thinking, for Watson, is a learned motor habit, no different in kind from playing tennis or golf. Example: when Kabir silently works out a sum, Watson would say his larynx and tongue are making small movements of the words. Later evidence went against the strong form of the idea: in a 1947 study, a volunteer fully paralysed with the drug curare, so that no speech muscle could move, stayed alert and could still perceive, think and remember. Option a is right because subvocal (implicit) speech is Watson's own definition of thinking. Exam trap: mental imagery is the classic distractor, but Watson rejected images as unobservable mentalism; imagery belongs to the structuralists, such as Edward Titchener (1898), who studied it through introspection.", "traps": {"b": "Mental imagery is what structuralists such as Edward Titchener studied through introspection; Watson dismissed images as unobservable mental contents.", "c": "Sudden insight is Wolfgang Kohler's Gestalt account of problem solving, from his chimpanzee studies (The Mentality of Apes, 1925), in which the whole situation is suddenly restructured; it is not Watson's.", "d": "Unconscious inference is Hermann von Helmholtz's (1867) account of perception, in which the brain makes fast, automatic guesses from sensory cues; it concerns perceiving, not Watson's view of thought."}}, {"q": "Tangentiality, incoherence and derailment are examples of:", "concept": "The answer is a: tangentiality, incoherence and derailment are disorders of the form of thought (formal thought disorder). Descriptive psychopathology, as set out in Frank Fish's Clinical Psychopathology (1967) and Andrew Sims's Symptoms in the Mind (1988), sorts thought disorders into four groups. Stream covers the speed and flow of thinking: flight of ideas, retardation, thought block (a sudden stop) and perseveration. Form covers how ideas are linked: the logical connection between one thought and the next breaks down. Content covers what the person thinks: delusions, overvalued ideas, preoccupations. Possession covers the sense that thoughts are one's own: obsessions, thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting. Nancy Andreasen (1979), in her Scale for the Assessment of Thought, Language and Communication, gave working definitions: tangentiality is replying in an oblique or irrelevant way that never reaches the point of the question; derailment (loosening of associations) is ideas slipping off track onto unrelated ones; incoherence (word salad) is speech so disorganised that it cannot be understood even within a sentence. Other formal disorders include neologisms, Knight's move thinking and overinclusion. Example: asked where he lives, Rohan says, 'Houses are made of brick, bricks are red, red is the colour of the flag, flags fly at the cricket ground', drifting further from the question with each link. Option a is right because all three describe a breakdown in the links between ideas, which is form. Exam trap: 'derailment' sounds like an interrupted flow, so students pick stream, the home of thought block and, in Fish, flight of ideas; but stream is about tempo and continuity, while tangentiality, derailment and incoherence are failures in how ideas link, the core formal thought disorders (some texts even move flight of ideas into form).", "traps": {"b": "Stream of thought concerns the speed and continuity of thinking: flight of ideas, retardation, thought block and perseveration; the three terms in the stem are about broken links, not speed.", "c": "Content of thought is what is thought, such as delusions, overvalued ideas and preoccupations; a person can be tangential while believing nothing false.", "d": "Possession of thought concerns ownership and control of one's thoughts: obsessions, thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting, several of them Kurt Schneider's (1959) first-rank symptoms."}}, {"q": "Who was the first psychiatrist to receive the Padma Shri?", "concept": "The answer is b: Dr N. C. Bordoloi, superintendent of the mental hospital at Tezpur in Assam, was the first psychiatrist in India to receive the Padma Shri. The hospital opened in April 1876 under British rule as the Tezpur Lunatic Asylum. Dr Bordoloi became its superintendent in 1949, and under him its administration and treatment services improved markedly; the Government of India honoured him with the Padma Shri for that dedicated service. In 1989 the hospital was renamed after Gopinath Bordoloi, the first chief minister of Assam, a different Bordoloi; it is today the Lokopriya Gopinath Bordoloi Regional Institute of Mental Health (LGBRIMH). The shared surname is a coincidence that confuses many students. The Padma awards (Padma Vibhushan, Padma Bhushan and Padma Shri) began only in 1954, which rules out pioneers who died before then. Example: if Priya lists landmarks of psychiatry in north-east India, she should place Tezpur (1876), Dr Bordoloi's superintendence (from 1949) and the 1989 renaming side by side. Option b is right because the institute's own history records Dr N. C. Bordoloi as the first psychiatrist in the country to receive the Padma Shri. Exam trap: the shared surname makes students think option b is the politician after whom the institute is named, and famous Ranchi names such as Owen Berkeley-Hill and J. E. Dhunjibhoy are offered because they sound senior. Key note: the recalled answer named him 'Nani Chandra Bordoloi'; the institute's own history gives only the initials, N. C. Bordoloi, so the initials are used here; it is the same man, and the answer stands.", "traps": {"a": "Girindrasekhar Bose founded the Indian Psychoanalytical Society in 1922 and is the father of Indian psychoanalysis, but he died in 1953, before the Padma awards began in 1954.", "c": "J. E. Dhunjibhoy became the first Indian medical superintendent of the Indian Mental Hospital at Ranchi (now RINPAS) in 1925 and was the first president of the Indian Psychiatric Society; he is not the first Padma Shri psychiatrist.", "d": "Owen Berkeley-Hill, a British psychiatrist, led the European Mental Hospital at Ranchi (now the Central Institute of Psychiatry) and died in 1944, ten years before the Padma awards began."}}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT an ICD-11 trait domain specifier for personality disorder?", "concept": "The answer is d: psychoticism is not an ICD-11 trait domain; it belongs to the DSM-5 Alternative Model of Personality Disorders. ICD-11 (World Health Organization, in effect from January 2022) dropped the old list of named personality disorder types. The clinician first decides whether there is a personality disorder and grades its severity as mild, moderate or severe, with personality difficulty as a milder category outside the disorders. Then, optionally, he describes its style with trait domain specifiers: negative affectivity (anxiety, anger, low mood, low self-esteem, mistrust), detachment (social and emotional distance), dissociality (disregard for others' rights and feelings), disinhibition (acting rashly on impulse) and anankastia (perfectionism, rigid control of oneself and others). A borderline pattern specifier was kept as well. The DSM-5 Alternative Model (Section III, 2013) also has five pathological trait domains, but its list is negative affectivity, detachment, antagonism, disinhibition and psychoticism. The lists share three domains (negative affectivity, detachment, disinhibition); ICD-11's dissociality is close to DSM-5's antagonism, ICD-11 adds anankastia, and DSM-5 adds psychoticism. In ICD-11, schizotypal disorder sits in the grouping of schizophrenia or other primary psychotic disorders, not among the personality disorders. Example: Neha's ICD-11 diagnosis might read 'moderate personality disorder with negative affectivity and anankastia'. Option d is right because psychoticism appears only in the DSM-5 model, not in ICD-11. Exam trap: anankastia looks unfamiliar and tempts students to pick it, but it is ICD-11's own domain, the one with no DSM-5 counterpart. Key note: the recalled option d was 'Cognitive', and the recaller was unsure of the word; it is filled here with psychoticism, the classic ICD-11 versus DSM-5 contrast; either way, d is not an ICD-11 domain, so the answer stands.", "traps": {"a": "Negative affectivity, a tendency to feel a wide range of distressing emotions, is one of ICD-11's five trait domains, and the DSM-5 alternative model has it too.", "b": "Detachment, keeping social and emotional distance from others, is an ICD-11 trait domain, shared with the DSM-5 alternative model.", "c": "Anankastia (perfectionism, rigidity, tight control of behaviour) is an ICD-11 trait domain; it is the one with no matching domain in the DSM-5 alternative model."}}, {"q": "In the Health Belief Model, what helps a person most to adhere to treatment?", "concept": "The answer is b: in the Health Belief Model (HBM), perceived vulnerability (susceptibility) to the illness is the core belief that moves a person to adhere to treatment. The model grew out of United States Public Health Service research in the 1950s, was set out by Irwin Rosenstock (1966), and Marshall Becker (1974) applied it to compliance with medical advice. It says a person takes a health action when two things are true. First, he feels threatened: he believes he is susceptible to the illness and that it is serious (perceived severity). Second, he believes the action works: its benefits outweigh its barriers, such as cost, pain, side effects and inconvenience. Cues to action, such as a symptom, a reminder or a friend's illness, trigger the move. Self-efficacy was added later (Irwin Rosenstock, Victor Strecher and Marshall Becker, 1988). Example: Ravi, told he has high blood pressure, takes his tablets regularly only once he believes that he personally could have a stroke. Option b is right because perceived vulnerability is one of the model's own original beliefs: in the HBM, a sense of personal threat is what gives the person the push to act at all. Exam trap: trust in the provider sounds like a health belief, but it is not an HBM construct at all. Key note: reviews of the evidence (Nancy Janz and Marshall Becker, 1984; Christopher Carpenter, 2010) found perceived barriers, and in Carpenter's analysis benefits too, to be the strongest predictors, with susceptibility weaker for sick-role behaviour such as adherence than for prevention; self-efficacy also belongs to the expanded model, so c has a case. But among these options perceived vulnerability is the model's own core belief that drives action, while self-efficacy was borrowed later from Albert Bandura (1977), so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Trust in the healthcare provider belongs to work on doctor and patient communication, such as Philip Ley's cognitive hypothesis model, where understanding, recall and satisfaction drive compliance; it is not an HBM belief.", "c": "High self-efficacy (Albert Bandura, 1977) does predict adherence and was added to the HBM in 1988, but it is a later borrowing, not one of the model's original beliefs about the illness.", "d": "Fear of the side effects of treatment is a perceived barrier in the HBM, so it lowers adherence rather than helping it."}}, {"q": "In a negatively skewed distribution:", "concept": "The answer is a: in a negatively skewed distribution the order is mode > median > mean. Skewness is a lack of symmetry. In a negatively (left) skewed distribution most scores pile up at the high end and a few very low scores stretch a long tail to the left; an easy test is the classic case, where most students score high and a handful do badly. The three averages react differently to those extreme scores. The mode, the most frequent score, sits at the peak, at the high end. The median, the middle score, is pulled a little towards the tail. The mean uses the value of every score, so the few very low scores drag it furthest towards the tail. Hence the mean is lowest, the median in the middle and the mode highest. A positive (right) skew, such as income, reverses this: mean > median > mode. In a normal distribution all three coincide. Karl Pearson (1857 to 1936) gave an empirical rule for moderately skewed data, mode = 3 median minus 2 mean, which gives the same order. Example: in Ananya's class of 30, 27 students score 85 on an easy quiz but three score 10; the mode and median stay at 85 while the mean drops to 77.5. Option a is right because the mean lies nearest the tail and the mode at the peak. Exam trap: students see 'negative' and reverse the order, or confuse the tail with the hump; remember that the mean follows the tail.", "traps": {"b": "Median > mode > mean is not a standard pattern: in a typical single-peaked skewed distribution the median lies between the mode and the mean.", "c": "Mean = median = mode holds for a symmetrical distribution such as the normal curve, where there is no skew.", "d": "Mean > median > mode is the positive (right) skew pattern, as with income, where a few very high scores pull the mean up."}}, {"q": "Who conducted the experiment in which children from poor backgrounds overestimated the size of coins?", "concept": "The answer is b: Jerome Bruner and Cecile Goodman (1947) showed that children from poor homes overestimated the size of coins. In their study, 'Value and need as organizing factors in perception', ten-year-old children adjusted a circle of light until it matched the size of coins, from a penny to a half dollar. A control group matched plain grey cardboard discs of the same sizes and judged them much more accurately. The children overestimated the coins, and the overestimate broadly grew with the coin's value. Children from poor homes overestimated more than children from well-off homes. Bruner and Goodman argued that what a thing means to us, its value and our need for it, shapes how we see it. The study became a landmark of the New Look in perception of the late 1940s, which held that motives, values and expectations enter perception itself, not only later judgement. Later replications gave mixed results, and some argued that poor children were simply less familiar with coins. Example: for Sonu, who rarely holds money, a ten rupee coin may look bigger than it does to a child who gets pocket money every day. Option b is right because the coin study is Bruner and Goodman's. Exam trap: Jerome Bruner is better known in exams for his modes of representation (enactive, iconic, symbolic, 1966) and discovery learning, so students who know him only as a developmental theorist doubt him; the coin study is his early perception work.", "traps": {"a": "Jean Piaget, from the 1920s onwards, studied how children's thinking develops through stages, using tasks such as conservation; he did not run the coin-size experiment.", "c": "Sigmund Freud (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900) founded psychoanalysis; the New Look borrowed the broad idea that needs colour perception, but Freud ran no such experiment.", "d": "Frederic Bartlett (Remembering, 1932) showed with 'The War of the Ghosts' that memory is reconstructed through schemas; his work is on memory, not on the perceived size of coins."}}, {"q": "In a one-way repeated measures ANOVA with 4 conditions and a sample of 20, what are the error degrees of freedom?", "concept": "The answer is b: the error degrees of freedom are 57. In a one-way repeated measures ANOVA every participant is tested under every condition, so the same 20 people supply all four sets of scores. The total variation is split into three parts: variation between conditions (the effect being tested), variation between subjects (stable individual differences, which this design removes from the error term) and residual error (the condition by subject interaction). Each part has its own degrees of freedom. With k = 4 conditions and n = 20 participants: df conditions = k minus 1 = 3; df subjects = n minus 1 = 19; df error = (k minus 1)(n minus 1) = 3 x 19 = 57; df total = kn minus 1 = 80 minus 1 = 79. Check: 3 + 19 + 57 = 79. The F ratio is MS conditions divided by MS error, read with df (3, 57). Because individual differences are taken out of the error term, a repeated measures design is more powerful than an independent groups design with the same number of scores. Example: Tara tests the same 20 students' recall after each of four study methods; she reports her result as F(3, 57). Option b is right because the error term in a one-way repeated measures ANOVA has (k minus 1)(n minus 1) = 57 degrees of freedom. Exam trap: 76 is N minus k, the error df for a between-subjects one-way ANOVA with 80 scores in four groups of 20; it is the answer for anyone who forgets that the same people appear in every condition.", "traps": {"a": "76 is N minus k = 80 minus 4, the within-groups df for an independent groups (between-subjects) ANOVA; it ignores that the same 20 people give all the scores.", "c": "3 is k minus 1, the df for conditions (the numerator of F), not for error.", "d": "19 is n minus 1, the df for subjects, the individual-differences part that this design removes from the error term."}}, {"q": "The law of comparative judgement is associated with which scale?", "concept": "The answer is d: the law of comparative judgement is associated with the Thurstone scale. Louis L. Thurstone (1927) published 'A law of comparative judgment', a way to turn simple choices into numbers on a psychological scale. In paired comparisons, judges see two stimuli at a time (two crimes, two statements) and say which is greater on some attribute. The proportion of judges choosing one over the other is converted, through the normal curve, into a distance between the two on the scale: if judges split 50 to 50, the two are equal, and the further the split moves from 50 percent, the further apart they lie. Thurstone then applied this thinking to attitudes. With E. J. Chave in The Measurement of Attitude (1929), he built the method of equal-appearing intervals: judges sort many statements into 11 piles from most unfavourable to most favourable, each statement gets a scale value (the median of its placings), ambiguous items are dropped, and a set spread evenly along the scale is kept. A respondent ticks the statements he agrees with, and his score is the median or mean of their scale values. Example: on an attitude to the church scale, Vikram agrees with items valued 7.5, 8.0 and 8.5, so his score is 8.0. Option d is right because the law of comparative judgement is Thurstone's own foundation for his scaling methods. Exam trap: the Likert scale is the best-known attitude scale and a common pick, but Rensis Likert's method sums agreement ratings and needs no judges or comparisons.", "traps": {"a": "The Likert scale (Rensis Likert, 1932) is a method of summated ratings: people rate their agreement with each statement, usually on five points, and the ratings are added; it uses no paired comparisons.", "b": "The Guttman scale (Louis Guttman, 1944) is a cumulative scalogram whose items form a ladder, so agreeing with a strong item implies agreeing with all the milder ones; it rests on that cumulative pattern, not on comparative judgements.", "c": "The Bogardus social distance scale (Emory Bogardus, 1925) asks how close a relationship, from kinship by marriage to exclusion from the country, a person would accept with members of a group; it ranks social distance directly and uses no paired comparisons."}}, {"q": "In qualitative research, the process in which the researcher writes down reflections on the observations made is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: writing down one's reflections on the observations made, as the study goes on, is called memoing. Memos are short, informal notes that a qualitative researcher writes to himself about the data: hunches, questions, possible links between codes, ideas about an emerging category and his own reactions. Memoing is a core step in grounded theory, the method of Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss (1967), later developed by Anselm Strauss and Juliet Corbin (1990). In grounded theory, theory is built up from the data rather than tested on it, and memos are the bridge: the researcher codes the data, writes memos about what the codes might mean, compares new data with old (constant comparison) and finally sorts the memos into the structure of the theory. Memos also leave an audit trail, a record of how interpretations developed, which adds to the study's trustworthiness. Example: after interviewing carers of people with schizophrenia, Shreya writes, 'Three mothers spoke of shame only when talking about neighbours; is stigma tied to the neighbourhood rather than the family?' That note is a memo. Option b is right because memoing is the standard name for recording reflections on observations during analysis. Exam trap: bracketing also involves the researcher's own thoughts, but it means setting them aside, not writing them down to build the analysis. Key note: reflexive journaling is close in meaning, but memoing is the standard term for writing down reflections on observations as one goes, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Bracketing (epoché, from Edmund Husserl's phenomenology, 1913) means setting aside one's own assumptions so that participants' experience can be seen freshly; it suspends views rather than recording reflections.", "c": "Member checking (respondent validation; Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba, 1985) means taking findings back to participants to confirm that they ring true, a test of credibility.", "d": "Open coding is the first stage of coding in grounded theory, breaking the data into pieces and giving each a conceptual label; memos are written about codes, but coding itself is labelling, not reflecting."}}, {"q": "Analysing the data in the middle of a study, before all of it has been collected, is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: analysing the data part way through a study, before collection is complete, is called interim analysis. It is used mostly in clinical trials that recruit patients over months or years. At planned points, for example after half the patients have finished, the accumulating data are examined to see whether the new treatment is already clearly better, clearly harmful, or so unlikely to show a difference that carrying on would be futile. If so, continuing may be unethical, and the trial can be stopped early. But every extra look at the data is another chance to find a 'significant' result by luck, so repeated looks inflate the Type I error rate. Trials therefore fix the number of looks and the stopping rules in advance and demand stricter thresholds at early looks, as in the boundaries of Peter O'Brien and Thomas Fleming (1979). The interim results are usually reviewed by an independent data monitoring committee, so that the investigators stay blind to how the trial is going. Example: Dr Rahul's trial of a new antidepressant plans to enrol 400 patients; at 200, the committee finds a clear excess of serious side effects and stops the trial. Option a is right because interim analysis is the name for examining the data while the study is still running. Exam trap: post hoc analysis also sounds like a mid-course check, but it comes after the main analysis is finished, such as follow-up tests after a significant ANOVA.", "traps": {"b": "Meta-analysis, a term coined by Gene Glass in 1976, statistically pools the results of many completed studies; it works across studies, not within one that is still running.", "c": "Secondary analysis is reanalysing data collected by someone else or for another purpose, such as census or survey data; the original data collection is already over.", "d": "Post hoc analysis comes after the main analysis, such as follow-up comparisons to find which groups differ after a significant ANOVA, or unplanned subgroup analyses; it is not done mid-study."}}, {"q": "In forensic psychology, the M'Naghten Rule is:", "concept": "The answer is c: the M'Naghten Rule is a test of insanity: whether, because of a disease of the mind, the accused did not know what he was doing or did not know that it was wrong. In 1843 Daniel M'Naghten, who believed he was being persecuted, shot and fatally wounded Edward Drummond, secretary to Prime Minister Robert Peel, probably mistaking him for Peel. He was acquitted as insane, there was a public outcry, and the House of Lords put questions to the judges. Their answers became the M'Naghten Rules: to establish insanity it must be clearly proved that, at the time of the act, the accused was labouring under such a defect of reason, from disease of the mind, as not to know the nature and quality of the act he was doing or, if he did know it, that he did not know he was doing what was wrong. It is a cognitive test: it asks about knowing, not self-control. India follows it: Section 84 of the Indian Penal Code, now Section 22 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (2023), 'act of a person of unsound mind', excuses an act done by a person who, at the time of doing it, by reason of unsoundness of mind, is incapable of knowing the nature of the act or that it is wrong or contrary to law. Example: Suresh, acutely psychotic, kills his neighbour believing he is destroying a demon; he did not know the nature of his act. Option c is right because it states the two limbs of the rule. Exam trap: the Durham rule (option d) also concerns insanity, but it asks whether the act was the product of mental disease, with no test of knowledge.", "traps": {"a": "Competence to stand trial concerns the accused's mental state at the time of the trial, not of the offence (Dusky v. United States, 1960: a rational and factual understanding of the proceedings and the ability to consult a lawyer).", "b": "The irresistible impulse test excuses a person who knew the act was wrong but could not control himself because of mental disease; some American courts added it because M'Naghten ignores self-control.", "d": "This is the Durham rule (Durham v. United States, 1954): not responsible if the act was the product of mental disease or defect; it was later abandoned as too vague."}}, {"q": "The broaden-and-build theory of positive emotions was given by:", "concept": "The answer is d: the broaden-and-build theory of positive emotions was given by Barbara Fredrickson (1998, 2001). Earlier emotion theories focused on negative emotions, which narrow attention to a specific action tendency: fear prepares us to flee, anger to attack. Fredrickson argued that positive emotions such as joy, interest, contentment and love work differently. In the moment they broaden a person's thought-action repertoire: joy urges play, interest urges exploring, contentment urges savouring. Over time, these broadened moments build lasting personal resources: physical (health), intellectual (knowledge and problem-solving skill), social (friendships) and psychological (resilience, optimism). The resources remain after the emotion has passed and help the person cope later, setting up an upward spiral. She also described an undoing effect: positive emotions speed recovery from the cardiovascular arousal left by negative emotions. Experiments supported broadening: after films that induced amusement or contentment, people listed more things they wanted to do and noticed the overall shape of a figure rather than its details. Example: Isha, laughing with classmates during a group project, comes up with more ideas and builds friendships that later support her through exam stress. Option d is right because broaden-and-build is Fredrickson's theory. Exam trap: Martin Seligman founded positive psychology, so students credit every positive psychology theory to him; broaden-and-build belongs to Fredrickson.", "traps": {"a": "Martin Seligman (learned helplessness, 1967; the positive psychology movement from 1998; the PERMA model, 2011) founded positive psychology but did not propose broaden-and-build.", "b": "Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi (Flow, 1990) described flow, the absorbed state when skill matches challenge; it is not a theory of what positive emotions do.", "c": "Ed Diener studied subjective well-being and built the Satisfaction with Life Scale (1985); he measured happiness rather than proposing broaden-and-build."}}, {"q": "The ethical principles of decision making are:", "concept": "The answer is a: the ethical principles of decision making are autonomy, beneficence, justice, fidelity and non-maleficence. Karen Kitchener (1984) proposed these five moral principles as the foundation for ethical decisions in counselling psychology. She built on Tom Beauchamp and James Childress's Principles of Biomedical Ethics (1979), which gave four principles: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice; Kitchener added fidelity. Autonomy is respecting the client's right to make his own choices, the basis of informed consent. Beneficence is actively doing good and promoting the client's welfare. Non-maleficence is avoiding harm, the old rule primum non nocere ('first, do no harm'), often seen as the most basic duty. Justice is fairness: treating people equally and sharing services fairly. Fidelity is keeping promises and staying loyal and honest, which underlies confidentiality and trust. When principles clash, the counsellor has to weigh them against each other. Example: Meenal, a counsellor, respects her client Aman's choice to stop sessions (autonomy) but explains the risks of stopping (beneficence), keeps what he told her confidential (fidelity) and gives him the same standard of care as any other client (justice). Option a is right because it is the only option that lists all five principles under their correct names. Exam trap: the wrong options swap in look-alike words, 'benevolence' (a kindly feeling, not the duty of beneficence) and 'maleficence' (doing harm, the opposite of non-maleficence), and they also drop justice.", "traps": {"b": "This list uses benevolence, a feeling of goodwill, in place of beneficence, the duty to do good, and it leaves out justice.", "c": "This list has the correct beneficence, but 'maleficence' means doing harm, the opposite of the principle of non-maleficence, and justice is missing.", "d": "This list makes both errors at once, benevolence for beneficence and maleficence for non-maleficence, and it also leaves out justice."}}, {"q": "Which section of the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2015 deals with orders that may not be passed against a child in conflict with law?", "concept": "The answer is d: Section 21 of the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2015 deals with orders that may not be passed against a child in conflict with law. The Act came into force on 15 January 2016, replacing the Juvenile Justice Act of 2000. A child in conflict with law is a person alleged or found to have committed an offence who was under 18 on the date of the offence. Section 21 says that no such child shall be sentenced to death or to life imprisonment without the possibility of release, for any offence, whether under this Act, the Indian Penal Code or any other law. The bar holds even when a child aged 16 to 18 is tried as an adult for a heinous offence in the Children's Court. The surrounding sections set out the process: Section 12 bail, Section 14 inquiry by the Juvenile Justice Board, Section 15 preliminary assessment, Section 17 orders when the child is not found to be in conflict with law, and Section 18 orders when he is. Clinical psychologists matter here because, under Section 15, the Board may take the help of experienced psychologists to assess the child's mental and physical capacity to commit the offence and his ability to understand its consequences. Example: Aakash, 17, is tried as an adult for murder; even if convicted, he cannot be sentenced to death or to life imprisonment without release. Option d is right because Section 21 is headed 'Order that may not be passed against a child in conflict with law'. Exam trap: Section 18 also concerns orders, so students pick it, but it lists the orders the Board may pass, while Section 21 lists those that can never be passed.", "traps": {"a": "Section 12 deals with bail to a person who is apparently a child alleged to be in conflict with law; such a person is normally released on bail, with or without surety.", "b": "Section 15 deals with the Board's preliminary assessment into heinous offences by a child aged 16 to 18: his mental and physical capacity to commit the offence, ability to understand its consequences and the circumstances.", "c": "Section 18 deals with orders regarding a child found to be in conflict with law, such as admonition, group counselling, community service or a stay of up to three years in a special home; these are orders the Board may pass, not barred ones."}}, {"q": "In persuasion, the boomerang effect is when:", "concept": "The answer is b: the boomerang effect is when a person adopts a position opposite to the one the message argues for. A persuasive message is meant to move the listener towards the speaker's view; in a boomerang it moves him away, so attitude change runs in the reverse direction. Two theories explain why. Muzafer Sherif and Carl Hovland's social judgement theory (1961) says each person has a latitude of acceptance (positions he finds acceptable), a latitude of non-commitment and a latitude of rejection around his own view. A message that falls in the latitude of rejection is contrasted: it is seen as even more extreme than it is, dismissed, and may push the person further the other way. Jack Brehm's reactance theory (1966) adds that a heavy-handed message threatens a person's freedom to choose, and he restores that freedom by doing the opposite. Boomerangs are most likely with very discrepant, preachy or controlling messages, and with sources seen as untrustworthy. Example: a school assembly that lectures Kunal harshly, telling him he must never touch a cigarette, leaves him keener to smoke, to show that nobody can control him. Option b is right because a shift opposite to the message's position is the definition of the boomerang effect. Exam trap: the sleeper effect (option c) is another named effect from Carl Hovland's Yale persuasion research, and students mix the two because both are unexpected outcomes; the sleeper effect is a delayed gain in persuasion, the boomerang a reversal.", "traps": {"a": "Moving further towards the message than intended has no standard name; the closest idea is assimilation in social judgement theory, where a message within the latitude of acceptance is seen as closer to one's own view than it really is.", "c": "This is the sleeper effect, shown in Carl Hovland's Yale research (Carl Hovland and Walter Weiss, 1951): a message from a low-credibility source gains impact over time as the source is forgotten or separated from the message.", "d": "This is William McGuire's inoculation theory (1961): meeting weak counter-arguments together with their refutation builds resistance to later strong attacks, as a vaccine builds immunity."}}, {"q": "Excess of dopamine is linked to:", "concept": "The answer is c: excess dopamine activity is linked to schizophrenia. The dopamine hypothesis holds that the positive symptoms of schizophrenia (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised thinking) come from too much dopamine activity, especially at D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway. Arvid Carlsson showed in the late 1950s that dopamine is a neurotransmitter in its own right, work that earned him a share of the Nobel Prize in 2000. Two lines of evidence support the hypothesis. First, antipsychotic drugs block D2 receptors, and in 1976 Ian Creese, David Burt and Solomon Snyder, and separately Philip Seeman's group, showed that a drug's clinical potency closely tracks how strongly it binds to D2 receptors. Second, drugs that raise dopamine, such as amphetamine and cocaine, can produce a paranoid psychosis that looks like schizophrenia, and they worsen symptoms in patients. The revised hypothesis (Kenneth Davis and colleagues, 1991) adds that dopamine is too high in the mesolimbic pathway but too low in the mesocortical pathway to the prefrontal cortex, which may underlie negative symptoms such as apathy and poverty of speech. Example: Rahul, after weeks of heavy amphetamine use, became convinced that his neighbours were spying on him; his symptoms faded with a D2-blocking antipsychotic. Option c is right because excess dopamine is the classic biochemical account of schizophrenia. Exam trap: Parkinson's disease is offered because it also involves dopamine, but it is the reverse, too little dopamine after loss of neurons in the substantia nigra, which is why dopamine-blocking antipsychotics can cause Parkinson-like side effects.", "traps": {"a": "Parkinson's disease (James Parkinson, 1817) is caused by too little dopamine, after loss of dopamine neurons in the substantia nigra; it is treated with L-dopa, which raises dopamine.", "b": "Alzheimer's disease (Alois Alzheimer, 1906) involves loss of acetylcholine neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert, amyloid plaques and neurofibrillary tangles, not an excess of dopamine.", "d": "Major depression is linked by the monoamine hypothesis mainly to low serotonin and noradrenaline, a shortage rather than an excess."}}, {"q": "Which memory lasts longer?", "concept": "The answer is a: echoic memory lasts longer than iconic memory. Both are parts of sensory memory (the sensory register), the first stage in Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin's (1968) multi-store model: it holds a large amount of raw, unprocessed information for a very short time, one store for each sense. Ulric Neisser (1967) named the visual store iconic memory and the auditory store echoic memory. George Sperling (1960) measured iconic memory with his partial report method. Shown 12 letters for 50 milliseconds, people could report only 4 or 5; but when a tone just after the flash signalled which row to report, they gave about 3 of the 4 letters in any row, so about 9 of the 12 had been briefly available. Delaying the tone by about a second removed the advantage: the icon fades within about a quarter to half a second. Christopher Darwin, Michael Turvey and Robert Crowder (1972), with an auditory version (the 'three-eared man' study), found that echoic memory lasts about 2 to 4 seconds. The difference makes sense: a picture is there all at once, but speech unfolds over time, so the auditory store must hold a few seconds of sound for a sentence to make sense. Example: when Sameer, absorbed in a book, says 'What?' to his mother and then answers before she repeats herself, he is reading her words from echoic memory. Option a is right because echoic memory lasts seconds, while iconic memory lasts under a second. Exam trap: iconic memory is better known because of Sperling's study, so students assume it lasts longer; it is the shorter store.", "traps": {"b": "Iconic memory, the visual sensory store measured by George Sperling (1960), fades within about a quarter to half a second, much faster than the echoic store.", "c": "The two stores do not last equally long: Sperling's visual studies and the auditory studies of Christopher Darwin, Michael Turvey and Robert Crowder give clearly different durations, under a second against about 2 to 4 seconds.", "d": "'Neither' does not fit: both stores have been measured, and the auditory one lasts longer."}}, {"q": "In ethnography, an etic approach refers to:", "concept": "The answer is c: an etic approach is the outsider's view, using the researcher's categories that can be compared across cultures. The linguist Kenneth Pike (1954) coined the pair emic and etic from the words phonemic and phonetic. Phonetics describes speech sounds with a universal system that works for any language; phonemics describes the sound differences that matter to speakers of one particular language. By analogy, an emic account describes a culture from the inside, in its members' own concepts and meanings, and holds only for that culture; an etic account describes it from the outside, with concepts the scientist brings, which can be applied to many cultures and compared. Ethnography usually aims first for an emic understanding through long fieldwork and may then add an etic analysis. John Berry (1969) carried the distinction into cross-cultural psychology and warned against an imposed etic, applying Western concepts to another culture as if they were universal; a derived etic is built up only after emic study of several cultures. Example: Divya studies distress in a Tamil village. The villagers' own idiom of 'tension' is an emic category; coding the same complaints as generalised anxiety disorder using DSM criteria is an etic one. Option c is right because an etic approach is defined by the outsider's comparative categories. Exam trap: the two terms are easily reversed, and option a, the definition of emic, is placed first to catch students; remember that etic, like phonetic, uses the outside observer's universal system.", "traps": {"a": "This is the emic approach, the insider's view in the culture's own terms and categories, the opposite of etic.", "b": "This is participant observation, the fieldwork method made famous by Bronislaw Malinowski in the Trobriand Islands (Argonauts of the Western Pacific, 1922); it is a method of ethnography that usually serves the emic aim, not a definition of the etic stance.", "d": "This is the life history (narrative) method, in which members tell their own lives in their own words; it gathers insider, emic material."}}, {"q": "According to Piaget, at what age does a child develop intentional behaviour?", "concept": "The answer is b: according to Jean Piaget, a child first shows clearly intentional, goal-directed behaviour at about 8 to 12 months. Piaget (1936), drawing on close observation of his own three children, divided the sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years) into six substages. Substage 1 (birth to 1 month) is exercising reflexes such as sucking and grasping. Substage 2, primary circular reactions (1 to 4 months), repeats chance actions centred on the baby's own body, such as thumb sucking. Substage 3, secondary circular reactions (4 to 8 months), repeats actions that happened to produce an interesting effect in the world, such as shaking a rattle. Substage 4, coordination of secondary circular reactions (8 to 12 months), is the turning point: the infant now combines two schemes, one as the means and the other as the end. Baby Riya pushes a cushion aside (the means) to grab the toy hidden behind it (the goal). This is also when she first searches for a fully hidden object, the start of object permanence, although she still makes the A-not-B error. Substage 5, tertiary circular reactions (12 to 18 months), is the 'little scientist' who varies actions to see what happens, and substage 6 (18 to 24 months) brings mental representation and deferred imitation. Option b is right because Piaget's marker of the first truly intentional act, with the means kept apart from the goal, is substage 4. Exam trap: 4 to 8 months looks purposeful, since the baby repeats an action to keep an effect going, but the effect was first found by chance, so it is not yet planned intention. Key note: some textbooks say intentionality begins to show in substage 3; but Piaget's own marker of the first clearly intentional act is substage 4, at 8 to 12 months, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "4 to 8 months is substage 3, secondary circular reactions: the baby repeats an action that by chance had an interesting effect (shaking a rattle), so the goal is discovered after the act rather than planned before it.", "c": "12 to 18 months is substage 5, tertiary circular reactions: the toddler deliberately varies actions to see new results, like a little scientist; intention is already present by then, so this is too late for its first appearance.", "d": "18 to 24 months is substage 6, the start of mental representation (deferred imitation, pretend play, solving problems in the head); it closes the sensorimotor stage rather than marking the first intentional act."}}, {"q": "Means-end analysis and working backward are examples of:", "concept": "The answer is b: means-end analysis and working backward are heuristics, rules of thumb that speed up problem solving without guaranteeing a solution. Psychologists contrast two broad kinds of strategy. An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that is certain to reach the answer if it is followed exactly, such as long division or trying every possible combination on a padlock; it is reliable but can be painfully slow. A heuristic is a mental shortcut that cuts down the search, usually works, but can fail. Allen Newell and Herbert Simon (Human Problem Solving, 1972), whose earlier General Problem Solver computer program modelled human reasoning, treated means-end analysis as the most important heuristic: note the biggest difference between where you are and where you want to be, set a subgoal that reduces it, and repeat until the gap closes. Working backward starts from the goal and traces the steps back to the starting point, which suits mazes, puzzles and geometry proofs. Arjun, planning his revision, starts from the exam date and counts back to decide what he must finish each week: that is working backward. Hill climbing and reasoning by analogy are other common heuristics. Option b is right because both strategies are shortcuts that narrow the search space but offer no promise of success. Exam trap: students sometimes call means-end analysis an algorithm because it sounds systematic and works in steps; but it only guides the search and does not guarantee a solution, which is exactly what makes it a heuristic.", "traps": {"a": "An algorithm is a fixed procedure that guarantees a solution if followed, such as trying every padlock code; means-end analysis and working backward give no such guarantee, so they are not algorithms.", "c": "Insight is the sudden 'aha' restructuring of a problem that Wolfgang Kohler (1925) described in chimpanzees stacking boxes to reach bananas; it is an experience of seeing the solution, not a deliberate step-by-step strategy.", "d": "Trial and error is the unguided, random attempting that Edward Thorndike (1898) saw in cats escaping puzzle boxes; heuristics are the opposite, because they direct the search instead of trying everything blindly."}}, {"q": "Which is most associated with frequent pairing of stimuli?", "concept": "The answer is d: paired conditioning, in the sense of repeatedly presenting a conditioned stimulus (CS) together with an unconditioned stimulus (US), is the option that names frequent pairing itself. In classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927), a neutral stimulus such as a bell comes to produce salivation because it is paired again and again with food. Each pairing is a trial, and the conditioned response grows stronger over trials during acquisition. Textbooks then describe several timing arrangements of the pairing. In delayed (forward) conditioning, the CS starts first and overlaps the US: this works best. In trace conditioning, the CS ends before the US begins, leaving a gap the animal must bridge with a memory trace, so it is weaker. In simultaneous conditioning, CS and US start together, which is weak. In backward conditioning, the US comes first; this is the weakest and can even teach that the CS signals no US. Robert Rescorla (1968) later showed that pairing alone is not enough: the CS must also predict the US (contingency). Example: Meera's mother calls her every evening, and her phone plays one particular ringtone each time; after many pairings, that ringtone alone, even from someone else's phone, makes Meera smile. Option d is right because the stem asks about frequent pairing in general, and only d names the pairing itself, while a, b and c are particular timing arrangements. Exam trap: when a question asks which arrangement conditions best, the answer is delayed (forward) conditioning, not trace conditioning, which students confuse because 'delay' sounds like 'gap'. Key note: 'paired conditioning' is not a standard textbook name, and the original options may have been recalled imperfectly (the real question may have offered delayed or forward conditioning); among these four, d is the only option that names repeated stimulus pairing itself, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Backward conditioning presents the US before the CS (food first, then the bell); it is the weakest arrangement and often fails or produces inhibition, so it is not what frequent pairing is known for.", "b": "Trace conditioning presents the CS and lets it end before the US begins, leaving a gap; it is one timing pattern of pairing, weaker than delayed conditioning, not pairing as such.", "c": "Simultaneous conditioning starts the CS and US at the same moment; because the CS gives no advance warning, it conditions poorly, and it too is only one timing arrangement."}}, {"q": "In Fiedler's contingency model, a high score on the Least Preferred Coworker (LPC) scale indicates which leadership style?", "concept": "The answer is d: in Fred Fiedler's contingency model (1967), a high score on the Least Preferred Coworker (LPC) scale marks a relationship-oriented leader. Fiedler asked leaders to think of the one person they had least liked working with and to rate that person on bipolar adjectives such as pleasant to unpleasant and friendly to unfriendly. A leader who still describes this difficult coworker in fairly kind terms (high LPC) separates the person from the work and is mainly motivated by good relationships. A leader who rates the person harshly (low LPC) is task-motivated: getting the job done comes first, and a poor worker is simply a bad person to work with. Fiedler treated this style as a fairly fixed trait. Whether it succeeds depends on situational favourableness, judged from three factors: leader-member relations, task structure and the leader's position power. Low-LPC (task-oriented) leaders do best when the situation is very favourable or very unfavourable; high-LPC (relationship-oriented) leaders do best when it is moderately favourable. Since style is hard to change, Fiedler advised changing the situation or matching the leader to it, through his Leader Match training. Example: Kabir, a ward manager who rates his least liked nurse as 'helpful though slow', scores high on the LPC and leads through rapport. Option d is right because a high LPC score shows that the leader values relationships even with a disliked coworker. Exam trap: students reverse the scoring and assume a high score means a demanding, task-focused leader; it is the low LPC score that marks the task-oriented style.", "traps": {"a": "Task oriented is the style marked by a low LPC score: the leader rates the least preferred coworker harshly because getting the job done matters most to him.", "b": "Nurturance is not one of Fiedler's two styles; the nearest idea is Jai B. P. Sinha's nurturant-task leader (1980), an Indian model of a leader whose warmth depends on the subordinate's hard work.", "c": "Power is not a leadership style in Fiedler's model; position power is one of his three situational factors (with leader-member relations and task structure) that decide how favourable the situation is."}}, {"q": "Crystallised intelligence refers to:", "concept": "The answer is a: crystallised intelligence is the knowledge and skills a person builds up through education and experience. Raymond Cattell (1963), later joined by his student John Horn (1966), split Charles Spearman's (1904) general intelligence (g) into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the ability to reason and solve new problems for which past learning gives little help: seeing patterns, drawing inferences and thinking flexibly. It depends heavily on the efficiency of the brain, rises through childhood, peaks in early adulthood and then declines slowly with age. Raven's Progressive Matrices and Cattell's Culture Fair Intelligence Test are classic measures. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the stored product of using that fluid ability within a culture: vocabulary, general information, arithmetic facts and professional know-how. It is tapped by vocabulary and information subtests, and it holds steady or keeps rising well into old age. Cattell's investment theory says fluid ability is 'invested' in learning, and the result crystallises into knowledge. Example: Grandmother Lakshmi, aged 70, beats her grandson at crosswords (crystallised) but takes longer than he does to learn a new phone app (fluid). The later Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) model keeps Gf and Gc and adds other broad abilities such as processing speed (Gs). Option a is right because crystallised intelligence is defined by accumulated, culturally learnt knowledge. Exam trap: the two definitions are often swapped; remember that crystals form slowly over time, so crystallised ability grows with experience, while fluid ability flows into any new problem.", "traps": {"b": "Reasoning on new problems without relying on prior learning is fluid intelligence (Gf), the other half of Cattell and Horn's split, measured by tests such as Raven's Progressive Matrices.", "c": "The speed of handling simple information is processing speed (Gs), a separate broad ability in the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model, not stored, crystallised knowledge.", "d": "Know-how for everyday real-life problems is Robert Sternberg's practical intelligence (1985), built on tacit knowledge; it concerns adapting to one's surroundings, not stored school-type knowledge."}}, {"q": "Who gave the expectancy theory of motivation?", "concept": "The answer is a: Victor Vroom gave the expectancy theory of work motivation in his book Work and Motivation (1964). The theory is often called VIE theory after its three beliefs. Expectancy is the belief that effort will lead to good performance ('if I try, can I do it?'). Instrumentality is the belief that performance will lead to an outcome or reward ('if I do it, will I get the promotion?'). Valence is how much the person values that outcome. Vroom combined them by multiplication: motivational force = expectancy x instrumentality x valence. Because it is a product, a zero on any one term wipes out motivation, however high the others are. Example: Neha, a sales executive, is offered a bonus she badly wants (high valence) and believes the targets are reachable (high expectancy), but she has seen bonuses withheld before (low instrumentality), so she does not push herself. Lyman Porter and Edward Lawler (1968) extended the model by adding ability, role perceptions and the satisfaction that follows rewards. Expectancy theory is a process theory: it explains how people decide where to spend effort, rather than listing which needs they have. Option a is right because the expectancy, instrumentality and valence model of motivation is Vroom's. Exam trap: Frederick Herzberg (1959) and Abraham Maslow (1943) are the names students most link with work motivation, but they gave content theories (what motivates people), whereas expectancy theory, a process theory (how effort is chosen), is Vroom's.", "traps": {"b": "Frederick Herzberg (1959) gave the two-factor (motivator-hygiene) theory: motivators such as achievement and recognition satisfy, while hygiene factors such as salary only prevent dissatisfaction; it says what motivates, not how expectancies combine.", "c": "B. F. Skinner (1938) explained behaviour through reinforcement and its schedules; at work this underlies reinforcement theory and behaviour modification, which set aside beliefs such as expectancy.", "d": "Abraham Maslow (1943) gave the hierarchy of needs, from physiological needs up to self-actualisation; it is a content theory of needs, not the expectancy, instrumentality and valence model."}}, {"q": "In Cattell's theory, the way ergs, sentiments and attitudes are linked in the dynamic lattice is the principle of:", "concept": "The answer is d: in Raymond Cattell's theory, ergs, sentiments and attitudes are linked in the dynamic lattice by subsidiation. Cattell (1950) divided traits into ability, temperament and dynamic traits; the dynamic traits are the motivational ones. Ergs are innate drives such as hunger, sex, fear, curiosity, gregariousness and self-assertion. Sentiments are learnt attitude structures built around major objects in a person's life, such as a career, home, religion or the self (the self-sentiment ties the rest together). Attitudes are the most specific level: an interest in a particular course of action in a particular situation. Subsidiation means that one is subsidiary to another, a means to it: attitudes serve sentiments, and sentiments serve ergs. Example: Rohan's attitude 'I want to revise pharmacology tonight' serves his sentiment towards his medical career, which in turn serves ergs such as self-assertion and security. Drawn as a web of these means-to-end links, the result is the dynamic lattice. The word goes back to Henry Murray (1938), who described one need as subsidiary to another, needs fusing (one act satisfying several needs at once) and prepotent needs taking priority. Option d is right because subsidiation is the principle Cattell used to chain attitudes to sentiments to ergs. Exam trap: fusion and prepotency sound plausible because they also come from Murray's account of needs, and prepotency is Abraham Maslow's (1943) word for lower needs dominating; but they describe how needs combine or which need wins, not the means-end chain. Key note: the original recall listed option d as 'Principle of substitution' and gave prepotency as the answer; substitution is almost certainly a misremembered 'subsidiation', Cattell's own term for the dynamic lattice, while prepotency is Murray's and Maslow's word for which need wins out, so d (subsidiation) stands.", "traps": {"a": "Fusion is Henry Murray's term for a single action that satisfies several needs at once (a working lunch feeds both hunger and affiliation); it describes needs combining, not the chain linking attitudes to sentiments to ergs.", "b": "Prepotency is the idea that the most urgent need takes precedence, used by Henry Murray and by Abraham Maslow for lower needs dominating until they are met; it ranks needs rather than linking them as means to ends.", "c": "Association is the general learning idea that things experienced together become linked, as in the British associationist tradition; it is not the named principle of Cattell's dynamic lattice."}}, {"q": "Reciprocal inhibition is associated with:", "concept": "The answer is d: reciprocal inhibition is the principle behind Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation. Wolpe (1958), in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, borrowed the term from Charles Sherrington's (1906) work on spinal reflexes, where contracting one muscle inhibits its opposing muscle. Wolpe argued that two incompatible states cannot occur together: if a response incompatible with anxiety, such as deep muscle relaxation, is made to occur in the presence of an anxiety-evoking stimulus, the bond between that stimulus and anxiety is weakened. Systematic desensitisation has three steps. First, the client learns relaxation, usually a short form of Edmund Jacobson's progressive muscle relaxation (1929). Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, ranking feared scenes from mild to severe, often rated in subjective units of distress (SUDS). Third, while deeply relaxed, the client imagines each scene in turn and moves up only when the current scene no longer brings anxiety. Example: Pooja, afraid of injections, relaxes while picturing a syringe lying on a table, then a nurse holding it, and finally the needle touching her arm. Option d is right because systematic desensitisation is built to pair a relaxed state with feared stimuli so that relaxation inhibits anxiety. Exam trap: exposure methods such as flooding and exposure and response prevention also reduce fear, but they work through habituation and extinction, with no competing response, so they do not rest on reciprocal inhibition.", "traps": {"a": "Exposure and response prevention, developed by Victor Meyer (1966) and later Edna Foa, exposes a person with OCD to triggers while blocking rituals; anxiety falls by habituation and extinction, not by a competing relaxed response.", "b": "Cognitive behaviour therapy, rooted in Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy (1976), is a broad family that changes distorted thoughts and behaviour; it is not tied specifically to the reciprocal inhibition principle.", "c": "Flooding exposes the person to the most feared stimulus at full intensity, without relaxation or graded steps, until anxiety dies away by extinction; it deliberately skips the incompatible response."}}, {"q": "Who wrote The Divided Self?", "concept": "The answer is a: The Divided Self: An Existential Study in Sanity and Madness (1960) was written by R. D. Laing (Ronald David Laing), a Scottish psychiatrist. Laing tried to make schizophrenia understandable as a human experience rather than treating it only as a brain disease. He described ontological insecurity: a person who lacks a firm sense of being real, whole and alive feels constantly threatened by others, fearing being swallowed up (engulfment), being invaded (implosion) or being turned into a thing (petrification). To protect a hidden 'true self', such a person builds a false-self system that complies with others on the surface, and in psychosis this split can break down. In Sanity, Madness and the Family (1964), written with Aaron Esterson, Laing argued that the speech of patients diagnosed with schizophrenia made sense once their family communication was studied. He became a leading figure of anti-psychiatry, alongside Thomas Szasz (1961) and David Cooper (1967), a movement that questioned diagnosis, forced treatment and the mental hospital. Example: Ananya, who seems compliant at home but describes feeling hollow and unreal inside, is the kind of case Laing would read through the false self. Option a is right because Laing wrote The Divided Self. Exam trap: all four options are linked with the critique of psychiatry, so examiners swap their books: The Myth of Mental Illness (1961) is Szasz's, Asylums (1961) is Erving Goffman's, and Psychiatry and Anti-Psychiatry (1967) is Cooper's.", "traps": {"b": "Thomas Szasz wrote The Myth of Mental Illness (1961), arguing that mental illness is a metaphor for problems in living; he is often grouped with anti-psychiatry (though he rejected the label) but did not write The Divided Self.", "c": "Erving Goffman, a sociologist, wrote Asylums (1961), describing mental hospitals as total institutions that strip inmates of their identity; it studies institutions, not the divided self.", "d": "David Cooper coined the term anti-psychiatry in Psychiatry and Anti-Psychiatry (1967) and worked closely with Laing, but The Divided Self is Laing's own book."}}, {"q": "Which concept was given by Alfred Adler?", "concept": "The answer is c: the inferiority complex is Alfred Adler's concept. Adler, founder of individual psychology, broke with Sigmund Freud in 1911. In Study of Organ Inferiority (1907) he argued that people compensate for weak organs, and he soon widened the idea: every child starts small, weak and dependent, so feelings of inferiority are universal and normal. They are the engine of growth, driving a striving for superiority, which Adler later called striving for perfection, meaning completion and mastery rather than dominance. Compensation overcomes a weakness; overcompensation goes beyond it, as when a stammering boy becomes a fine orator. When the feelings are too strong, for instance after organ inferiority, pampering or neglect, they harden into an inferiority complex: a lasting sense of being inadequate that leads the person to give up or hide behind excuses. Its mask can be a superiority complex of boasting and belittling others. Adler also gave style of life, social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefühl), fictional finalism, the creative self and birth order. Example: Vikram, mocked for his height, feels so worthless that he stops trying out for any team; that is an inferiority complex rather than healthy compensation. Option c is right because Adler coined the inferiority complex and built his theory around inferiority feelings. Exam trap: all four are famous psychodynamic terms, so examiners mix up the neo-Freudians: the collective unconscious belongs to Carl Jung, who split from Freud in 1913; basic anxiety to Karen Horney (1937); and the Oedipus complex to Freud himself.", "traps": {"a": "The collective unconscious, an inherited layer of the psyche holding archetypes shared by all humans, belongs to Carl Jung's analytical psychology, not Adler's individual psychology.", "b": "Basic anxiety, a child's feeling of being isolated and helpless in a potentially hostile world, is Karen Horney's concept (1937), which she linked to neurotic needs and to moving towards, against or away from people.", "d": "The Oedipus complex, the boy's wish for his mother and rivalry with his father in the phallic stage, is Sigmund Freud's; Adler rejected Freud's sexual emphasis in favour of striving for superiority."}}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT a scale of the MMPI?", "concept": "The answer is a: there is no Phobia scale on the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI). Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) built the MMPI by empirical criterion keying: an item was kept if it separated a diagnosed patient group from normal controls, whatever its content. The original test has ten clinical scales, numbered for profile coding: 1 Hypochondriasis (Hs), 2 Depression (D), 3 Hysteria (Hy), 4 Psychopathic deviate (Pd), 5 Masculinity-femininity (Mf), 6 Paranoia (Pa), 7 Psychasthenia (Pt), 8 Schizophrenia (Sc), 9 Hypomania (Ma) and 0 Social introversion (Si). The validity scales (? or Cannot Say, L for Lie, F for Infrequency, K for Correction) check how the person approached the test. Phobic fears are picked up by Psychasthenia, an old term from Pierre Janet (1903) for anxiety, obsessions, compulsions, phobias and excessive doubt; today scale 7 is read as a measure of general anxiety and rumination. The MMPI-2 (1989) kept the same ten clinical scales. Example: Sameer's profile peaks on scale 7, so the psychologist explores his worry and obsessional doubt; there is no separate phobia score to look up. Option a is right because no MMPI clinical or validity scale is named Phobia. Exam trap: phobia sounds like an obvious clinical category, so students assume it must have a scale, while psychasthenia, an unfamiliar word, looks like the invented option; in fact psychasthenia is the real scale and it is the one that covers phobias.", "traps": {"b": "Hypochondriasis (Hs) is clinical scale 1 of the MMPI, measuring excessive concern with bodily health and vague physical complaints, so it is a real scale.", "c": "Psychasthenia (Pt) is clinical scale 7, Pierre Janet's old term for anxiety, obsessions, compulsions and phobic fears; it is a real scale and is the one that actually covers phobias.", "d": "Hypomania (Ma) is clinical scale 9, measuring raised energy, excitability, grandiosity and flight of ideas, so it is a real MMPI scale."}}, {"q": "Resting tremor is characteristic of:", "concept": "The answer is d: resting tremor is the classic sign of Parkinson's disease. James Parkinson described the disorder in An Essay on the Shaking Palsy (1817). A resting tremor appears when the limb is relaxed and supported, for example lying in the lap, and lessens or disappears when the person moves it on purpose. In Parkinson's it is typically a slow, rhythmic 'pill-rolling' movement of thumb and fingers, about 4 to 6 beats per second, often starting on one side. The other core signs are rigidity (often 'cogwheel', a ratchety resistance when the limb is moved), bradykinesia (slowness and poverty of movement, with a masked face and small handwriting) and, later, postural instability with a shuffling gait. The cause is the loss of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra, which feed the striatum through the nigrostriatal pathway; surviving neurons contain Lewy bodies. L-dopa, a precursor the brain turns into dopamine, eases the symptoms. Example: Mr Iyer's right hand trembles while he sits watching television but steadies when he reaches for his cup of tea. Option d is right because a tremor at rest that eases on action is the hallmark of Parkinson's disease. Exam trap: examiners contrast resting tremor with intention tremor, which worsens as the hand approaches a target and points to the cerebellum, and with the postural or action tremor of essential tremor, seen when holding a cup or a newspaper.", "traps": {"a": "Huntington's disease, described by George Huntington (1872), shows chorea: jerky, irregular, dance-like movements, with dementia; it comes from a CAG repeat on chromosome 4 and loss of caudate neurons, not a resting tremor.", "b": "Cerebellar disease produces intention tremor, which worsens as the hand nears a target (the finger-to-nose test), along with ataxia; the tremor is absent when the limb is at rest.", "c": "Essential tremor is an action or postural tremor, seen when holding a cup or with the arms outstretched; it often runs in families and may ease with a little alcohol, but it is not a resting tremor."}}, {"q": "The ratio of the light reflected from a surface to the total light falling on it is called:", "concept": "The answer is d: the proportion of the light falling on a surface that the surface reflects is called its albedo (reflectance). Albedo is a property of the object itself. White paper reflects most of the light that falls on it (roughly 80 to 90 percent) and black paper very little (well under 10 percent), whatever the lighting. Luminance, by contrast, is the actual amount of light coming from the surface to the eye, which depends on the illumination multiplied by the albedo. So a black sheet in bright sunlight can send more light to the eye than a white sheet in a dim room, yet the black sheet still looks black and the white one white. This is lightness constancy. Hans Wallach (1948) explained it with the ratio principle: we judge lightness from the ratio between the light coming from a surface and the light from its surroundings; a change in illumination changes both by the same factor, so the ratio, and with it perceived lightness, stays the same. Perceived lightness therefore tracks albedo, not luminance. Brightness is the experienced intensity of light, a term used mainly for light sources. Example: Farhan's white kurta looks white in the noon sun and under a weak bulb at night, because its albedo has not changed. Option d is right because albedo is the textbook name for this reflectance ratio in perception. Exam trap: luminance sounds right because it is about light from a surface, but it is an absolute amount that varies with the lighting, not a ratio. Key note: 'reflectance' and albedo mean the same thing, so a looks close; but 'reflectance coefficient' is not the standard term in perception texts, and albedo is, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Reflectance coefficient is a physics-style phrase for the same idea, but perception textbooks name this ratio albedo; it is the near-miss distractor, not the standard term.", "b": "Brightness index is not a standard term; brightness is the experienced intensity of light, a psychological dimension used mainly for light sources, not the physical reflectance ratio of a surface.", "c": "Luminance is the absolute amount of light reaching the eye from a surface (illumination times albedo); it changes with the lighting, whereas albedo is a fixed proportion."}}, {"q": "Paivio's dual-code hypothesis states that memory is retained better when information is:", "concept": "The answer is a: Allan Paivio's dual-code hypothesis states that information is remembered better when it is coded both in words and as a mental image. Paivio (1971), in Imagery and Verbal Processes, proposed two separate but linked memory systems. The verbal system stores language units and the non-verbal imagery system stores mental pictures and other sensory images; in his later account (1986) Paivio called these units logogens (a term borrowed from John Morton) and imagens. Concrete words such as 'elephant' or 'mango' easily call up an image, so they receive two memory codes, a word and a picture. Abstract words such as 'justice' are stored mainly in the verbal code. Two codes give two routes to retrieval, so if one fails the other can still bring the item back. This explains the concreteness effect (concrete words are recalled better than abstract ones) and the picture superiority effect (pictures are recalled better than their names), and it underlies imagery mnemonics such as the method of loci. Example: Tanvi learns the word 'stethoscope' while picturing her doctor wearing one, so she has both a verbal and a visual trace. Option a is right because dual coding means storing the same item in both the verbal and the imagery system. Exam trap: the other options are rival memory principles that examiners attach to the wrong name: meaning-based processing is Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart's levels of processing (1972), and recall being best in the learning setting is Endel Tulving and Donald Thomson's encoding specificity (1973). Key note: the original recall gave the answer as 'visual and auditory encoding'; Paivio's two codes are verbal and imaginal (non-verbal), not sight and hearing, and the verbal code covers words whether heard or read, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Repeating information over and over is rote (maintenance) rehearsal, the process that in Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin's multi-store model (1968) holds items in the short-term store; it uses one code, not two.", "c": "Processing for meaning rather than sound is Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart's levels of processing (1972): deep, semantic processing beats shallow, phonemic processing; it is about depth, not dual codes.", "d": "Recall being best in the same setting as learning is encoding specificity (Endel Tulving and Donald Thomson, 1973), shown in Duncan Godden and Alan Baddeley's divers study (1975); it is about retrieval cues, not dual coding."}}, {"q": "Which of these is a functional hallucination?", "concept": "The answer is b: hearing voices only when the fan is on is a functional hallucination. In descriptive psychopathology, as set out in Frank Fish's Clinical Psychopathology (1967), a functional hallucination is triggered by a real stimulus in one sense, and the hallucination is experienced in that same sense, alongside the real stimulus. The patient hears the whirr of the fan and, at the same time, hears voices; when the fan is switched off, the voices stop. The real noise is not misperceived; it is heard normally, and the voice is an extra perception that comes and goes with it. This separates a functional hallucination from an illusion, in which a real object is itself misperceived and turned into something else. A related form is the reflex hallucination, in which a stimulus in one sense produces a hallucination in a different sense (seeing flashes of colour whenever music plays), a morbid form of synaesthesia. Example: Suresh, who has schizophrenia, reports that a man's voice abuses him every time the tap runs, while he also hears the water clearly. Fish also described extracampine hallucinations (outside the normal sensory field, such as voices from another city) and hypnagogic and hypnopompic ones (on falling asleep and on waking). Option b is right because the voices need the real fan noise to appear, occur in the same sense of hearing, and are heard together with it. Exam trap: the closest distractor is d, because it too needs a real trigger; but the colours appear in vision while the trigger is heard, which makes it a reflex hallucination, whereas a functional hallucination stays in the same sense as its trigger. Mistaking a rope for a snake is the textbook illusion, since the rope itself is misperceived.", "traps": {"a": "Mistaking a rope for a snake in low light is an illusion: a real object is misperceived, helped by poor light and fear (a completion or affect illusion); nothing extra is perceived beside the rope.", "c": "Seeing a shadow and believing it is a ghost is a misinterpretation of a real stimulus, an affect illusion coloured by fear; the shadow itself is transformed, so no hallucination occurs alongside it.", "d": "Seeing flashes of colour whenever music plays is a reflex hallucination: a stimulus in one sense (hearing) produces a hallucination in another (vision), a morbid form of synaesthesia; a functional hallucination stays in the same sense."}}, {"q": "The temporary visual impairment felt on coming out of a dark room into bright light is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: the brief glare and poor vision felt on stepping from a dark room into bright light is light adaptation. In the dark, the eye has made itself as sensitive as possible: the pupil is wide and the rods, which work in dim light, are loaded with their pigment, rhodopsin. Sudden bright light floods this over-sensitive system, so for a moment everything looks dazzling and washed out. The eye then recovers quickly. The pupil constricts, rhodopsin is bleached so the rods drop out, and the cones, which work in bright light and give colour and fine detail, take over. Within about a minute or less, vision is comfortable again. Dark adaptation is the reverse process and is much slower. On entering a dark cinema, sensitivity rises over about 20 to 30 minutes: the cones adapt within the first 7 to 10 minutes, then the curve shows a rod-cone break and the rods go on gaining sensitivity as rhodopsin regenerates. Example: Aditi walks out of a matinee into the afternoon sun and squints for a few seconds before she can read the signboards. Option c is right because the temporary impairment on moving into brightness is the eye adjusting to light. Exam trap: students pick dark adaptation because they read 'dark room', but the name follows the new condition the eye must adjust to, which here is light.", "traps": {"a": "Night blindness (nyctalopia) is a lasting difficulty seeing in dim light because the rods do not work properly, often from vitamin A deficiency; it is a disorder, not a normal, brief adjustment to brightness.", "b": "Dark adaptation is the slow gain in sensitivity, over about 20 to 30 minutes, on going from light into darkness; the stem describes the opposite move, from dark into light.", "d": "An afterimage is an image that lingers after a stimulus is removed, such as a coloured spot after staring at a bright lamp, often in complementary colours as Ewald Hering's opponent-process theory explains; it is not the glare of light adaptation."}}, {"q": "In Zuckerman's Alternative Five-Factor Model (AFFM), which factor is NOT similar to a factor in Costa and McCrae's Big Five?", "concept": "The answer is a: in Marvin Zuckerman's Alternative Five-Factor Model (1993), Activity is the factor with no matching factor in Paul Costa and Robert McCrae's Big Five (1992). Zuckerman, with Michael Kuhlman and colleagues, built the alternative five from traits he thought had a clear biological basis, and measured them with the Zuckerman-Kuhlman Personality Questionnaire (ZKPQ). Its five factors are Neuroticism-Anxiety, Aggression-Hostility, Impulsive Sensation Seeking, Sociability and Activity. Three of them line up with Big Five factors: Neuroticism-Anxiety with Neuroticism, Sociability with Extraversion, and Aggression-Hostility with low Agreeableness. Impulsive Sensation Seeking relates to low Conscientiousness. Activity, meaning a need for constant activity, a liking for hard and challenging work and restlessness when idle, has no factor of its own in the Big Five; there it appears only as one facet of Extraversion (facet E4, Activity, in the NEO PI-R). The alternative five also has no counterpart to Openness to experience, which Zuckerman did not regard as a basic, biologically based trait, so Activity, rather than Openness, completes his five. Example: Karan cannot sit still on holidays and fills every free hour with tasks; he would score high on ZKPQ Activity. Option a is right because Activity is the alternative factor that is not one of the Big Five. Exam trap: 'Hostility' looks foreign to the Big Five because no Big Five factor carries that name, but it maps onto the low pole of Agreeableness. Key note: the original recall marked a as 'unsure', and later studies place Activity partly with Extraversion; but Activity is the factor with no matching Big Five factor, while hostility, neuroticism and sociability each map onto one, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Aggression-Hostility corresponds to the low end of Big Five Agreeableness (antagonism, quarrelsomeness, rudeness), so it does have a Big Five counterpart.", "c": "Neuroticism-Anxiety corresponds directly to Big Five Neuroticism (emotional instability, worry, tension), so it is the clearest match of all.", "d": "Sociability corresponds to Big Five Extraversion (liking people, parties and company), so it is similar to a Big Five factor."}}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT one of the trigunas?", "concept": "The answer is d: spirituality is not one of the three gunas. In Samkhya philosophy, reality has two principles: purusha, pure consciousness, and prakriti, matter or nature. Prakriti is woven from three strands or qualities, the trigunas, which are always present together in varying proportions. Sattva is purity, goodness, light, harmony and knowledge. Rajas is energy, activity, passion, desire and restlessness. Tamas is inertia, darkness, lethargy, dullness and ignorance. The Bhagavad Gita applies them to personality (chapter 14, then chapters 17 and 18): whichever guna dominates colours a person's thinking, food, work and choices. Indian psychologists have built measures from this idea, such as the Vedic Personality Inventory (David Wolf, 1999), which scores sattva, rajas and tamas. Spiritual liberation (moksha) is described as going beyond all three gunas; a person who has done so is called gunatita, 'beyond the gunas'. Spirituality is therefore a goal that transcends the gunas, not a fourth guna. Example: on the night before an exam, Ananya revises calmly and sleeps on time (sattva), Rohit stays up anxiously cramming (rajas), and Dev oversleeps and skips revision (tamas). Option d is right because purity, energy and inertia are the English names of sattva, rajas and tamas, and spirituality matches none of them. Exam trap: the options are English translations, so students who know only the Sanskrit names may fail to recognise purity as sattva and pick it as the odd one out. Key note: the original recall marked a (Purity), but purity is sattva itself, one of the three; spirituality is the only option that is not a guna, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Purity is sattva, the guna of goodness, light, harmony and knowledge, so it is one of the three gunas.", "b": "Inertia is tamas, the guna of darkness, lethargy, dullness and ignorance, so it is one of the three gunas.", "c": "Energy is rajas, the guna of activity, passion, desire and restlessness, so it is one of the three gunas."}}, {"q": "Which of these does NOT help to reduce social loafing?", "concept": "The answer is a: assigning general, vague tasks does not reduce social loafing; it makes loafing worse. Social loafing is the tendency to put in less effort when working in a group whose output is pooled than when working alone. Max Ringelmann's rope-pulling studies (published 1913) first showed that the more people pulled together, the less each one pulled. Bibb Latane, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) named the effect: students asked to clap and shout as loudly as they could made less noise per person as the group grew, even when they only believed others were joining in. Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993), in their collective effort model, explained that people work hard when they expect their own effort to matter and to lead to outcomes they value. Loafing therefore falls when each person's contribution is identifiable and evaluated, when the task is meaningful, specific and challenging, when members have distinct roles, when the group is small, and when members care about the group and one another. Example: in a college project, Ishaan does almost nothing when the lecturer says 'the group will prepare a report', but works hard once each member is given a named chapter that will be marked separately. Option a is right because general tasks hide individual effort and remove personal responsibility, the very conditions that breed loafing. Exam trap: in this NOT question, the three helpful options sound like ordinary group advice, so students search them for a flaw; the option that fails is the vague, shared task. Key note: 'maintaining the number of members' is loosely worded; read as keeping the group small and fixed (not letting it grow), it reduces loafing, since loafing rises with group size; so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Encouraging friendly relationships among members raises group cohesion; Steven Karau and Kipling Williams found that people loaf less when they value the group and its members, so this helps.", "c": "Individual accountability, making each person's effort identifiable and evaluated, is the strongest remedy for social loafing, because people can no longer hide in the crowd.", "d": "Keeping the group small and its size fixed helps, since loafing grows as members are added (as in Ringelmann's rope pulling); stopping the group from swelling keeps each person's share visible."}}, {"q": "Which of these dysfunctional thoughts will maintain OCD?", "concept": "The answer is c: 'thinking about hurting someone is just as bad as doing it' is a dysfunctional belief that maintains OCD. The cognitive model of OCD starts from a surprising finding: Stanley Rachman and Padmal de Silva (1978) showed that most people without OCD, nearly 80 percent in their sample, have unwanted intrusive thoughts much like clinical obsessions. Paul Salkovskis (1985) argued that the difference lies not in the thought but in how it is appraised. A person who reads the intrusion as meaningful, dangerous and a sign of personal responsibility for harm becomes anxious and feels driven to neutralise it through checking, praying, reassurance seeking or other compulsions. Neutralising brings brief relief, so it is negatively reinforced, and it stops the person from learning that the thought was harmless. Thought-action fusion (Roz Shafran, Dana Thordarson and Stanley Rachman, 1996) names two such beliefs: moral fusion, that having a bad thought is morally equal to doing the act, and likelihood fusion, that thinking of an event makes it more likely to happen. Other appraisals include inflated responsibility, overimportance of thoughts and the need to control them. Example: Nikhil has a flash of pushing his baby niece down the stairs, decides he must be a terrible person, and now avoids holding her. Option c is right because it is moral thought-action fusion, an appraisal that turns a normal intrusion into an obsession. Exam trap: a, b and d sound like the calm self-talk a therapist would teach, so in a 'which maintains' question they are the healthy answers, not the problem.", "traps": {"a": "Recognising that everyone has intrusive thoughts at times is the normalising message of CBT for OCD, drawn from Rachman and de Silva's finding; it lowers the threat value of the thought rather than maintaining the disorder.", "b": "Letting a thought pass without engaging with it is a non-threatening, accepting appraisal; active suppression can backfire (Daniel Wegner's white bear studies, 1987), but as phrased this is a healthy response, not a dysfunctional belief.", "d": "Seeing strange thoughts as acceptable as long as one does not act on them separates thinking from doing, the opposite of thought-action fusion, and is what therapy aims to build."}}, {"q": "Which of these would work against social anxiety disorder?", "concept": "The answer is c: reduced self-focused attention would work against social anxiety disorder, because heightened self-focus is one of its main maintaining processes. David Clark and Adrian Wells (1995) proposed the leading cognitive model of social anxiety. When a socially anxious person enters a feared situation, beliefs such as 'I will look stupid' are activated and attention turns inward. The person monitors a racing heart, trembling and blushing, and uses these feelings to build a distorted image of how he looks to others, often pictured from an observer's point of view. He also uses safety behaviours, such as avoiding eye contact, gripping a glass tightly or rehearsing sentences, which can make him seem odd and stop him learning that disaster would not occur. After the event, post-event processing replays the encounter in detail and recalls it as worse than it was, helped by memory biases for negative evaluation. Avoiding situations altogether blocks any chance of disconfirming the fears. Clark's cognitive therapy for social anxiety disorder therefore trains attention outward onto the task and other people, drops safety behaviours and uses video feedback to correct the self-image. Example: Divya, giving a seminar, stops checking whether her voice shakes and looks at her listeners' faces; her anxiety falls and she notices they are interested. Option c is right because cutting down self-focused attention removes a core maintaining factor. Exam trap: in a 'works against' question, students sometimes pick avoidance because it lowers anxiety in the moment, but avoidance maintains the disorder over time.", "traps": {"a": "Post-event processing, the detailed replaying of a social event after it ends, is part of the Clark and Wells model and maintains social anxiety by making the person remember the event as worse than it was.", "b": "Memory biases, recalling negative feedback and one's own mistakes more readily, keep the fear of evaluation alive, so they maintain social anxiety rather than work against it.", "d": "Avoiding the situation reduces anxiety in the short term but stops the person discovering that the feared outcome does not happen, so it maintains the disorder."}}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT associated with PTSD?", "concept": "The answer is b: elevated mood is not a feature of post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). PTSD can follow exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence, whether the person lives through it, witnesses it, learns that it happened to someone close, or meets its grim details again and again at work. In DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013; kept in DSM-5-TR, 2022) the symptoms fall into four clusters lasting more than one month. Intrusion: unwanted memories, nightmares and flashbacks, in which the person feels or acts as if the event were happening again. Avoidance: keeping away from thoughts, places or people that bring it back. Negative changes in thinking and mood: blame, fear, guilt, detachment, gaps in memory for the event, and a persistent inability to feel positive emotions. Changes in arousal and reactivity: hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, irritability, poor concentration and poor sleep. If symptoms last only 3 days to 1 month, the diagnosis is acute stress disorder instead. ICD-11 (World Health Organization) keeps a shorter core: re-experiencing in the here and now, avoidance, and a persistent sense of current threat. Example: after a bus crash, Arjun relives the skid in flashbacks, takes long detours to avoid the highway and jumps at every horn, yet he cannot feel happy even at his sister's wedding. Option b is right because PTSD pushes mood down and numbs joy; a high, euphoric or expansive mood belongs to a manic or hypomanic episode, the opposite pole. Exam trap: emotional numbing and the inability to feel positive emotions are PTSD symptoms, so a decoy such as 'loss of pleasure' actually belongs; only raised, elated mood is out.", "traps": {"a": "Flashbacks are dissociative reactions in the DSM-5 intrusion cluster, in which the person feels or acts as if the trauma were recurring, so they are a core PTSD symptom, not the odd one out.", "c": "Avoidance of trauma reminders, internal (thoughts, feelings) or external (people, places), is a whole DSM-5 cluster of its own and one of the three core elements in ICD-11.", "d": "Hypervigilance belongs to the DSM-5 arousal and reactivity cluster, alongside exaggerated startle and poor sleep, and matches ICD-11's persistent sense of current threat."}}, {"q": "A person has an enhanced memory of an object, as if he were still seeing it when it is no longer there. This is:", "concept": "The answer is b: a vivid image of an object that a person still 'sees' after it has gone is eidetic imagery. In eidetic imagery (popularly called photographic memory) a person looks at a picture for some seconds, and after it is removed he goes on seeing a detailed image of it, as if it were still out there on the blank surface. He can move his eyes over the image and read off details, such as the number of stripes on a cat's tail, and the image keeps the original colours. It lasts from some seconds to a few minutes and then fades. The German psychologist Erich Jaensch studied it in the 1920s. Ralph Haber and Ruth Haber (1964) tested children in an American elementary school and found true eidetic imagery in only about 8 percent of them; in adults it is rarer still. Example: Meera, aged 8, glances at a picture of a market scene; the card is taken away, yet she keeps pointing at the empty board, describing the colour of each fruit as if it were still in front of her. Option b is right because the stem describes an image of a seen object that persists after the object is removed and is experienced as still being in front of the eyes. Exam trap: an afterimage also outlasts the stimulus. But an afterimage is a brief retinal effect after staring, often in the complementary colour, and it moves when the eyes move; an eidetic image keeps the original's colours and detail, stays put on the surface and can be scanned like a real picture.", "traps": {"a": "An afterimage is a retinal fatigue effect seen after staring at a stimulus; a negative afterimage shows complementary colours (Ewald Hering's opponent processes). It is brief and moves with the eyes, so it is not an enhanced memory of the object.", "c": "A hallucination is a perception without any external stimulus at all; the eidetic image is based on a real object that was just seen, and it carries that object's actual detail.", "d": "A flashbulb memory (Roger Brown and James Kulik, 1977) is a vivid, confident memory of the circumstances in which one heard shocking news, not a picture-like image of an object that has just been removed."}}, {"q": "A girl is nervous about a presentation, but tells herself, \"I have done this before, so I will be able to do it again.\" In Lazarus's theory this is:", "concept": "The answer is b: telling herself 'I have done this before, so I can do it again' is secondary appraisal. Richard Lazarus (1966), and later Lazarus with Susan Folkman (1984) in the transactional model of stress, argued that stress does not lie in an event itself but in how a person appraises the relation between the event and his resources. Primary appraisal asks 'Does this matter to me, and how?' The answer may be irrelevant, benign-positive or stressful; a stressful event is judged as harm or loss (already done), threat (harm to come) or challenge (a chance to gain). Secondary appraisal asks 'What can I do about it? Are my coping resources and options enough?' Stress is greatest when demands look high and resources look thin. Reappraisal is a later revision of either judgement as new information comes in. Coping follows: problem-focused coping changes the situation (preparing slides, rehearsing), while emotion-focused coping regulates the feeling (deep breathing, venting, distraction). Example: Riya's stomach knots before her seminar presentation; that sense of threat is primary appraisal. Then she recalls the three seminars she has already given, judges that her skills are enough, and calms down. Option b is right because her self-talk weighs her own past success and ability, that is, her coping resources, which is exactly what secondary appraisal evaluates. Exam trap: 'secondary' does not mean 'happening later' or 'less important'; the two appraisals run together and feed each other. Her nervousness alone would be primary appraisal, and a calming thought is not automatically emotion-focused coping.", "traps": {"a": "Primary appraisal is the judgement of whether the presentation matters and whether it is a harm, threat or challenge; her nervousness reflects it, but her self-talk is about her resources, not the stakes.", "c": "Reappraisal is a later revision of earlier appraisals in the light of new information or feedback (for example, after the first slide goes well); her thought is a judgement of present ability, not a revision.", "d": "Emotion-focused coping is an effort to manage the feeling itself, such as breathing exercises, distraction or venting; her thought is an evaluation of her resources, the appraisal that comes before and guides coping."}}, {"q": "A man sees a bear, his body is aroused, he runs and only then feels fear. Which theory of emotion does this show?", "concept": "The answer is b: seeing the bear, being aroused, running, and only then feeling fear is the James-Lange theory. Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid and therefore run. William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885), working separately, reversed the order: the stimulus first sets off bodily changes and actions, and the emotion is our perception of those changes. In James's words, we feel sorry because we cry, angry because we strike, afraid because we tremble. So each emotion should have its own pattern of bodily arousal. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked this: the viscera react slowly, much the same arousal comes with very different emotions, and injecting adrenaline does not produce a true emotion. He proposed instead, backed by the animal studies of Philip Bard (1928), that the thalamus sends signals at the same moment to the cortex (the feeling) and to the body (the arousal). Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer (1962) added cognition: arousal plus a label drawn from the situation produces the emotion (the two-factor theory). Richard Lazarus (1991) put appraisal first. Example: Kabir hears a growl on a forest trail, his heart races and he sprints, and only when he notices his pounding heart and shaking legs does he feel terror. Option b is right because the stem places arousal and action before the feeling, and makes the feeling a reading of the body. Exam trap: in Cannon-Bard, arousal and feeling happen at the same time and independently; if an option says 'simultaneously', it is Cannon-Bard, and if it adds a cognitive label for the arousal, it is Schachter-Singer.", "traps": {"a": "Cannon-Bard (Walter Cannon and Philip Bard) holds that the thalamus triggers the feeling of fear and the bodily arousal at the same moment and independently, so fear would not wait until after he runs.", "c": "The Schachter-Singer two-factor theory (1962) needs arousal plus a cognitive label taken from the situation; the stem says nothing about interpreting or labelling the arousal.", "d": "Richard Lazarus's cognitive appraisal theory puts an appraisal of the bear as a threat before any emotion; the stem gives no appraisal step and has the feeling follow the body."}}, {"q": "Which nerve carries visual information from the eyes to the brain?", "concept": "The answer is c: the optic nerve carries visual information from the eye to the brain. The optic nerve is cranial nerve II. It is made of the axons of the retinal ganglion cells, the output cells of the retina, and it leaves the back of the eye at the optic disc, which has no rods or cones and so forms the blind spot. The two optic nerves meet at the optic chiasm, where fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross to the other side, while fibres from the temporal halves stay on the same side. Beyond the chiasm the fibres run as the optic tracts to the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus, and the optic radiations then carry the signal to the primary visual cortex (V1) in the occipital lobe. As a result, the left visual field ends up in the right hemisphere. Option c is right because the optic nerve is the only sensory nerve for vision among the four; the other three move the eyeball. Exam trap: the oculomotor (III), trochlear (IV) and abducens (VI) nerves all supply the eye, so they sound visual, but they are motor nerves that aim the eye and carry no image. A handy memory aid for the eye muscles is 'LR6, SO4, the rest 3': lateral rectus by VI, superior oblique by IV, all the others by III.", "traps": {"a": "The oculomotor nerve (III) moves most of the eye muscles (superior, inferior and medial rectus, inferior oblique), lifts the eyelid and constricts the pupil; it is a motor nerve, not a visual one.", "b": "The trochlear nerve (IV) supplies only the superior oblique muscle, which helps turn the eye downward, as when walking downstairs; it carries no visual signal.", "d": "The abducens nerve (VI) supplies the lateral rectus, which turns the eye outward; damage causes a squint and double vision, not loss of sight."}}, {"q": "Paradoxical intention is a part of which therapy?", "concept": "The answer is c: paradoxical intention is a technique of logotherapy. Logotherapy is the school of the Viennese psychiatrist Viktor Frankl (Man's Search for Meaning, 1946), who survived the Nazi concentration camps. Its central idea is the will to meaning: people are driven most deeply by the search for meaning, and an 'existential vacuum' breeds distress. It is often called the third Viennese school of psychotherapy, after psychoanalysis and individual psychology. Frankl first described paradoxical intention in print in 1939. It targets anticipatory anxiety, the vicious circle in which fear of a symptom brings on the symptom. The patient is asked to wish for, or deliberately try to produce, the very thing he fears, ideally with humour; the fear loses its grip because one cannot fear and intend the same thing at once. The technique draws on the human capacity for self-distancing. Its partner technique is dereflection, which turns attention away from oneself and onto something meaningful. Example: Rohan cannot sleep because he worries that he will not sleep; told to lie in bed and try to stay awake as long as he can, he soon drifts off. Option c is right because paradoxical intention is Frankl's own named logotherapy technique. Exam trap: behaviour therapists later tested it, for instance L. Michael Ascher and Jay Efran (1978) in a programme for sleep-onset insomnia, so behaviour therapy looks tempting; but they borrowed it rather than invented it. Key note: logotherapy is itself one of the existential therapies, so b has a case; but paradoxical intention belongs specifically to Frankl's logotherapy, the more precise answer, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Behaviour therapy rests on conditioning techniques such as systematic desensitisation, exposure and reinforcement; behaviour therapists later borrowed paradoxical intention for insomnia, but it began in logotherapy.", "b": "Existential therapy (Rollo May, Irvin Yalom) is the broad family dealing with freedom, death, isolation and meaninglessness; logotherapy sits inside it, but paradoxical intention is logotherapy's own technique, so the narrower answer wins.", "d": "Psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) works through free association, dream analysis and the interpretation of transference and resistance; it never asks patients to intend their symptoms."}}, {"q": "In the Ponzo illusion, two equal lines look unequal because:", "concept": "The answer is d: in the Ponzo illusion the converging lines act as a depth cue, so the bar seen as farther away looks longer. The figure is named after the Italian psychologist Mario Ponzo (1912; some books give 1911 or 1913). It shows two identical horizontal bars placed between two lines that converge towards the top, like railway tracks running into the distance. The upper bar, nearer the point where the lines meet, looks longer. The usual explanation is misapplied size constancy, argued by Richard Gregory (1963). Converging lines are the linear perspective cue to depth, so the visual system treats the upper bar as farther away. Normally an object that is farther away casts a smaller image on the retina, and the brain scales it up so that its size seems constant. Here both bars cast the same retinal image, so the one registered as farther is scaled up and looks bigger. The moon illusion is often explained along similar lines. Example: Ananya lays two equal matchsticks across a photograph of railway tracks; the one near the horizon looks longer, although a ruler shows they are the same. Option d is right because it names both the depth cue and the size scaling that produce the effect. Exam trap: each distractor is a real illusion with its own cause, so match the figure to the name: arrowhead fins mean Muller-Lyer, surrounding circles mean Ebbinghaus, and an inverted T means the vertical-horizontal illusion.", "traps": {"a": "Arrowhead fins at the ends of equal lines describe the Muller-Lyer illusion (Franz Muller-Lyer, 1889), where the line with outward fins looks longer; Ponzo bars have no fins.", "b": "Equal circles that look different because of the size of the circles around them is the Ebbinghaus (Titchener circles) illusion, a size contrast effect rather than a depth cue.", "c": "Overestimating vertical lines compared with equal horizontal ones is the vertical-horizontal illusion (the inverted T); in the Ponzo figure both bars are horizontal."}}, {"q": "The phi phenomenon is best explained as:", "concept": "The answer is d: the phi phenomenon is a form of apparent movement. Apparent movement is the perception of motion when nothing actually moves. Max Wertheimer (1912), working in Frankfurt with Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka as his observers, flashed two lines one after the other in slightly different places. When the gap between the flashes was very short (about 30 milliseconds) the lines looked simultaneous; when it was long (about 200 milliseconds) they looked like two separate flashes; but at about 60 milliseconds a single line seemed to move from one place to the other. Because the motion lay in neither stimulus, it could not be built up from separate sensations, and the paper founded Gestalt psychology, with its claim that the whole is different from the sum of its parts. The same principle runs the cinema (a series of still frames), flipbooks and the 'running' lights of a shop sign. Example: at a Diwali market, Ishaan watches a string of bulbs that only switch on and off in sequence, yet he sees a single light chasing round the frame. Option d is right because phi is motion seen without any physical movement, which is the definition of apparent (stroboscopic) movement. Exam trap: induced movement and the autokinetic effect are also illusions of motion, so the three non-real options look alike; but phi alone depends on separate stimuli flashed in quick succession.", "traps": {"a": "Real movement is the perception of an object that actually changes position, sweeping its image across the retina (a bird flying past); in phi nothing moves at all.", "b": "Induced movement is when a still object seems to move because its surrounding frame moves, as the moon seems to race through drifting clouds; phi needs no moving frame.", "c": "The autokinetic effect is the apparent wandering of a single stationary point of light in a dark room, which Muzafer Sherif (1935) used to study how group norms form; it involves one steady light, not two in succession."}}, {"q": "In Atkinson's theory of achievement motivation, a person whose motive to succeed is stronger than his motive to avoid failure prefers tasks that are:", "concept": "The answer is c: a person whose motive to succeed is stronger than his motive to avoid failure prefers tasks of moderate difficulty. John W. Atkinson (1957, 1964) built a formal theory on David McClelland's need for achievement, which McClelland and colleagues (1953) scored from Thematic Apperception Test stories. The tendency to approach success equals Ms x Ps x Is: the motive to succeed (Ms), the subjective probability of success (Ps) and the incentive value of success (Is). Atkinson set Is = 1 minus Ps, because success on a hard task brings more pride. So Ps x (1 minus Ps) is largest when Ps = 0.5: it is 0.25 there, but only 0.09 when Ps is 0.9 or 0.1. A matching tendency to avoid failure is driven by the motive to avoid failure (Maf, often measured as test anxiety), and it is also strongest at moderate difficulty, where failure is both likely and shameful. When Ms is stronger than Maf the net pull is towards moderate risks; when Maf is stronger the person retreats to very easy tasks (success is sure) or very hard ones (failure is no disgrace). In the ring-toss study of Atkinson and George Litwin (1960), students high in achievement motive and low in test anxiety mostly threw from a middle distance. Example: in a quiz, Aditya, who loves a challenge, picks the medium questions, while his anxious friend chooses only the easiest or the near-impossible ones. Option c is right because moderate difficulty gives the most motivating mix of a fair chance and real pride. Exam trap: the U-shaped choice of very easy or very hard tasks belongs to the failure-avoidant person, not the success-oriented one.", "traps": {"a": "Very easy tasks are chosen by the failure-avoidant person (motive to avoid failure stronger), because success is almost certain; for a success-oriented person the incentive of an easy win is too low to motivate.", "b": "Very hard tasks are also the failure-avoider's choice, since failing at a near-impossible task brings no shame; for the success-oriented person the probability of success is too low.", "d": "In Atkinson's model the incentive is the pride of success, Is = 1 minus Ps, so it is tied to difficulty rather than to an outside reward; reward value as a separate factor fits Victor Vroom's expectancy theory better."}}, {"q": "A girl scores 130 on an IQ test with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. What is her z score?", "concept": "The answer is b: her z score is 2. A z score tells how many standard deviations a raw score lies above or below the mean: z = (X minus mean) / SD. Here z = (130 minus 100) / 15 = 30 / 15 = 2. A positive z lies above the mean, a negative one below it, and the mean itself has z = 0. Because z scores always have a mean of 0 and an SD of 1, they let us compare scores from tests with quite different scales. On the normal curve about 68 percent of scores fall within 1 SD of the mean, about 95 percent within 2 SD and about 99.7 percent within 3 SD, so a z of 2 sits at roughly the 98th percentile (97.7). The same score is T = 50 + 10z = 70. Deviation IQs, introduced by David Wechsler (1939), use a mean of 100 and an SD of 15 for exactly this kind of reading. Example: Sneha's 130 puts her ahead of about 98 in every 100 children of her age. Option b is right because the 30-point gap above the mean is exactly two SDs of 15. Exam trap: the decoys come from slips in the arithmetic; dividing the 30-point gap by 10, the SD of the T scale, gives 3, and 1.5 or 2.5 would need scores of 122.5 or 137.5.", "traps": {"a": "A z of 1.5 means 1.5 SDs above the mean, a score of 100 + 1.5 x 15 = 122.5, not 130.", "c": "A z of 2.5 corresponds to 100 + 2.5 x 15 = 137.5; it overstates the 30-point gap.", "d": "A z of 3 would be a score of 145; it is what you get by wrongly dividing 30 by 10, the SD of T scores, instead of 15."}}, {"q": "Which of these gives the most information about the data?", "concept": "The answer is c: of these four, the box plot gives the most information about a set of data. John Tukey (1977) introduced the box plot (box-and-whisker plot) in Exploratory Data Analysis. It draws the five-number summary: the minimum, the first quartile (Q1), the median, the third quartile (Q3) and the maximum. The box runs from Q1 to Q3, so its length is the interquartile range, the spread of the middle 50 percent of scores, and a line inside it marks the median. Whiskers reach out towards the lowest and highest scores; in Tukey's version they stop at the most extreme score lying within 1.5 interquartile ranges of the box, and scores beyond that are plotted as separate outlier points. In one small figure the reader sees the centre, the spread, the skew (a median off-centre in the box and one long whisker) and any outliers, and several boxes drawn side by side compare groups at a glance. Example: Dr Kavya plots anxiety scores for three hospital wards; one look shows that ward B has the highest median, the widest box and two extreme outliers. Option c is right because the box plot summarises the whole distribution of the data themselves. Exam trap: the other three are specialised plots that sound technical and therefore informative, but each answers one narrow question: publication bias (funnel), how many factors to keep (scree) or study effects (forest). Key note: forest and funnel plots also pack in a great deal, but about the studies in a meta-analysis rather than about one set of scores, and a scree plot shows only eigenvalues; among these four, the box plot is the one that describes a data distribution, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "A funnel plot, used in meta-analysis, plots each study's effect size against its precision (such as sample size); an asymmetric funnel suggests publication bias. It describes a set of studies, not the spread of one data set.", "b": "A scree plot (Raymond Cattell, 1966) graphs the eigenvalues of successive factors in factor analysis, and factors above the 'elbow' are kept; it says how many factors to retain, nothing about centre or spread.", "d": "A forest plot shows each study's effect and confidence interval in a meta-analysis, with a diamond for the pooled estimate; it summarises studies, not a single distribution of scores."}}, {"q": "A bow-shaped ROC curve suggests that the test:", "concept": "The answer is d: a ROC curve that bows up towards the top left corner shows a test that discriminates well between cases and non-cases. The receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve comes from signal detection theory (David Green and John Swets, 1966). A test with a cut-off score gives, at every possible cut-off, a pair of values: sensitivity (the true positive rate, the share of real cases the test catches) and 1 minus specificity (the false positive rate, the share of non-cases wrongly flagged). The ROC curve plots sensitivity on the vertical axis against 1 minus specificity on the horizontal axis across all cut-offs. A useless test that merely guesses gives the straight diagonal from bottom left to top right, with an area under the curve (AUC) of 0.5. The more the curve bows towards the top left corner, where sensitivity is high and false positives are few, the better the test; a perfect test has an AUC of 1.0. A common rule of thumb reads 0.7 to 0.8 as acceptable, 0.8 to 0.9 as excellent and 0.9 or more as outstanding. Example: Dr Nikhil checks a new depression screening questionnaire against diagnostic interviews; its ROC curve hugs the top left corner with an AUC of 0.90, so it separates depressed from non-depressed patients well. Option d is right because the bow means that, across cut-offs, true positives rise much faster than false positives. Exam trap: prevalence changes the positive and negative predictive values, but sensitivity and specificity, and so the ROC curve, do not depend on how common the disorder is.", "traps": {"a": "A test no better than chance gives the straight diagonal line (AUC 0.5), the opposite of a bow; any upward bow means the test beats chance.", "b": "Many false positives push the curve towards the right-hand side of the graph; a bow towards the top left means high sensitivity with few false positives.", "c": "How common the disorder is affects predictive values, not the ROC curve, because sensitivity and specificity are worked out within cases and within non-cases separately."}}, {"q": "Which of these does NOT come under specific learning disorder?", "concept": "The answer is c: dyspnoea (American spelling 'dyspnea') does not come under specific learning disorder; it means shortness of breath. In DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013), specific learning disorder is a neurodevelopmental disorder: persistent difficulty in learning and using academic skills for at least 6 months despite targeted help, with skills well below those expected for age, starting in the school years, and not better explained by intellectual disability, sensory problems, poor teaching or a language barrier. It is diagnosed with one or more specifiers. With impairment in reading covers word reading accuracy, reading rate and fluency, and comprehension; dyslexia is the accepted alternative name for this pattern. With impairment in written expression covers spelling, grammar and punctuation, and clarity of written work (often called dysgraphia). With impairment in mathematics covers number sense, memory for arithmetic facts, accurate calculation and mathematical reasoning; dyscalculia is the alternative name. Example: Tanvi, aged 9, has an average IQ but still reads single words slowly and hesitantly and often guesses at them, after two years of good teaching. Option c is right because dyspnoea is a respiratory symptom, listed among panic attack symptoms, and has nothing to do with learning. Exam trap: all four words begin with 'dys' (Greek for 'bad' or 'difficult'), so the odd one out hides by sound; ask what each ending means: '-lexia' words, '-calculia' calculation, '-praxia' action, '-pnoea' breathing. Key note: strictly, DSM-5 places dyspraxia under developmental coordination disorder, a motor disorder, not under specific learning disorder, though India's Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 lists dyspraxia with dyslexia, dysgraphia and dyscalculia under specific learning disabilities, and UK practice also groups it with the specific learning difficulties; dyspnoea is not a learning problem at all, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Dyspraxia (difficulty planning and coordinating movement) is formally developmental coordination disorder in DSM-5, but India's Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 names it among the specific learning disabilities, and UK practice groups it the same way, so it is not the clear odd one out.", "b": "Dyslexia is the accepted alternative term in DSM-5 for specific learning disorder with impairment in reading (accuracy, fluency, comprehension), so it plainly belongs.", "d": "Dyscalculia is the DSM-5 alternative term for specific learning disorder with impairment in mathematics (number sense, calculation, reasoning), so it belongs."}}, {"q": "In Sternberg's triarchic theory, practical intelligence is:", "concept": "The answer is d: in the triarchic theory of Robert Sternberg (1985), practical intelligence is the ability to adapt to, shape and select one's everyday environment. In Beyond IQ, Sternberg argued that IQ tests catch only part of intelligence and proposed three linked aspects, later grouped as successful intelligence. Analytical (componential) intelligence analyses, compares, judges and evaluates; it is what school tests and IQ tests reward. Creative (experiential) intelligence deals with novel situations and turns new skills into automatic routines; it invents and imagines. Practical (contextual) intelligence applies knowledge to real life: adapting oneself to the environment, shaping the environment to suit oneself, or, when neither works, selecting a different environment. It leans on tacit knowledge, the unspoken know-how that is rarely taught (how to handle a difficult boss), the 'street smarts' of everyday life. A classic illustration comes from Brazil: Terezinha Nunes Carraher, David Carraher and Analucia Schliemann (1985) found that children selling goods in street markets did complex sums correctly while trading but failed the same sums set as school problems. Example: Farhan cannot study in his noisy hostel; he first tries earplugs (adapting), then persuades his roommates to keep quiet hours (shaping), and finally moves to the library (selecting). Option d is right because adapting, shaping and selecting are Sternberg's own three functions of contextual, practical intelligence. Exam trap: options a and b describe the other two arms of the same triarchic theory, so knowing the theory's name will not pick the answer; match analytical to evaluating, creative to novelty and practical to the everyday context.", "traps": {"a": "Analysing, comparing and evaluating define analytical (componential) intelligence, the arm of Sternberg's theory closest to what IQ tests measure, not practical intelligence.", "b": "Dealing with new situations and creating is creative (experiential) intelligence, the second arm of the triarchic theory, not the practical one.", "c": "A general factor running through all mental tests is Charles Spearman's g (1904), the very view Sternberg's theory was meant to go beyond."}}, {"q": "Functional fixedness is best described as:", "concept": "The answer is c: functional fixedness is a mental set that always complicates problem solving. It is the tendency to see an object only in its usual function, which blocks the solver from seeing a new use for it. Karl Duncker (1945) showed it with the candle problem: people were given a candle, a box of drawing pins and matches and asked to fix the candle to a wall so that it could burn. Far fewer people thought of emptying the box and pinning it up as a shelf when it was handed over full of pins than when it came empty, because a full box looks only like a container. Norman Maier's two-string problem (1931) is similar: the solution is to tie a pair of pliers to one string and swing it as a pendulum. Functional fixedness is one kind of mental set, a readiness to approach a problem in a fixed way. Abraham Luchins (1942) showed the broader set (Einstellung) with water jars: people drilled on a long formula kept using it even when a shorter one worked. Example: Pooja needs to tighten a loose screw and spends ten minutes hunting for a screwdriver, never thinking of the coin in her pocket. Option c is right because functional fixedness is by definition the obstacle, the failure to see beyond an object's familiar use. Exam trap: mental set in general can help (a method that worked before often works again), so b fits mental set but not the named concept of functional fixedness. Key note: some texts describe functional fixedness, like any mental set, as a habit that usually saves time; but as a named concept it is defined as an obstacle that blocks new uses of an object, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "A set that always helps would be positive transfer of a good habit; functional fixedness is the opposite, an obstacle that hides an object's other uses.", "b": "Sometimes helpful and sometimes harmful describes mental set in general (Abraham Luchins's water jars), which can speed up routine problems; functional fixedness is the narrower concept that names the harmful case.", "d": "A sudden insight that solves the problem at once is Wolfgang Kohler's insight with his chimpanzees, the restructuring that overcomes fixedness, not fixedness itself."}}, {"q": "According to Guilford, creativity does NOT involve:", "concept": "The answer is c: according to Joy Paul Guilford (1950), novelty is not one of the named components of creativity. Guilford used his 1950 American Psychological Association presidential address to launch the scientific study of creativity. In his structure of intellect model (1956, 1967) he separated convergent production (finding the one correct answer) from divergent production (generating many possible answers), and tied creativity to divergent thinking. He scored divergent production on fluency, the number of ideas produced; flexibility, the number of different categories the ideas come from, or the ease of shifting between them; originality, how unusual or statistically rare the ideas are; and elaboration, the amount of detail added. He also discussed sensitivity to problems and redefinition. E. Paul Torrance's Tests of Creative Thinking (1966) originally scored the same four. Example: asked for uses of a brick, Siddharth lists 12 (fluency) from building, defence, art and exercise (flexibility), including 'a pestle for grinding spices', which no one else in his class thought of (originality), and explains how he would shape it into a doorstop with a handle (elaboration). Option c is right because 'novelty' is not one of Guilford's factor names, while fluency, flexibility and originality are. Exam trap: novelty sounds almost like originality, and creativity is often defined as producing what is novel and useful, so examiners count on that overlap. Key note: novelty overlaps with originality in everyday meaning, so c can look like a trick; but it is not one of Guilford's factor names, while the other three are, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Fluency, the sheer number of ideas a person can produce, is one of Guilford's named divergent production factors and the first score on the Torrance tests, so it belongs.", "b": "Flexibility, the variety of categories among the ideas and the ease of shifting between them, is a core Guilford factor, so it belongs.", "d": "Originality, the unusualness or statistical rarity of ideas, is one of Guilford's factors, and it is the one that 'novelty' is meant to be confused with."}}, {"q": "Which drug is used in a narcoanalysis test to induce a semi-conscious state?", "concept": "The answer is b: sodium pentothal is used in narcoanalysis to produce a semi-conscious state. Sodium pentothal is a trade name for thiopental sodium (thiopentone), a fast-acting barbiturate long used to start general anaesthesia. In narcoanalysis a small dose is injected slowly into a vein, with sodium amytal (amobarbital) as the usual alternative, until the person is drowsy but can still talk. In this twilight state inhibitions fall and people speak more freely, which earned it the popular name 'truth serum'. J. Stephen Horsley (1936) coined the term narcoanalysis and used it as a short cut in psychotherapy; in the Second World War it helped mute, stuporous or amnesic soldiers recall and abreact battle trauma. In fact drugged people can still lie, fantasise and be led by suggestion. In India investigators often put suspects through it, but in Selvi v. State of Karnataka (2010) the Supreme Court held that compulsory narcoanalysis, polygraph and brain-mapping tests violate Article 20(3), the right against self-incrimination, and Article 21, personal liberty. They may be given only with free consent, and the statements made cannot be used as evidence, though facts later discovered with the help of a voluntary test may be. Example: Raju, accused of theft, refuses a narcoanalysis test; under the Selvi ruling, the police cannot force it on him. Option b is right because a barbiturate such as sodium pentothal is what produces the drowsy, disinhibited state. Exam trap: haloperidol and chlorpromazine are sedating, so they sound plausible, but they are antipsychotics, not narcoanalysis agents.", "traps": {"a": "Lithium is a mood stabiliser for bipolar disorder, taken by mouth over weeks with blood level checks; it does not produce a quick semi-conscious state.", "c": "Haloperidol is a high-potency typical antipsychotic (a D2 blocker) used for psychosis and acute agitation; it is not used to loosen inhibitions in narcoanalysis.", "d": "Chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic (1952), is sedating, but it is a D2-blocking phenothiazine for psychosis, not a narcoanalysis agent."}}, {"q": "World No Tobacco Day is observed on:", "concept": "The answer is b: World No Tobacco Day is observed every year on 31 May. The member states of the World Health Organization created it at the World Health Assembly in 1987 to draw attention to the death and disease caused by tobacco use. The first one was actually held on 7 April 1988, the 40th anniversary of WHO; from 1989 onward it has been held on 31 May. Each year WHO chooses a theme, such as protecting young people from the tobacco industry's tactics, and gives awards to people and groups who have done notable work against tobacco. In India, tobacco control rests on the Cigarettes and Other Tobacco Products Act (COTPA, 2003), which bans smoking in public places and tobacco advertising, and on the National Tobacco Control Programme, launched in 2007 to 2008. Option b is right because 31 May has been the fixed annual date since 1989. Exam trap: 7 April is World Health Day, marking the founding of WHO in 1948, and it was also the date of the very first No Tobacco Day in 1988, so it is the most dangerous decoy; 10 October is World Mental Health Day, and 26 June is the International Day against Drug Abuse and Illicit Trafficking.", "traps": {"a": "7 April is World Health Day, the anniversary of WHO's founding in 1948; the first World No Tobacco Day did fall on 7 April 1988, but the day moved to 31 May from 1989.", "c": "10 October is World Mental Health Day, begun by the World Federation for Mental Health in 1992.", "d": "26 June is the International Day against Drug Abuse and Illicit Trafficking, set by the UN General Assembly in 1987; tobacco has its own day in May."}}, {"q": "In Tangney's Test of Self-Conscious Affect (TOSCA), alpha pride and beta pride differ in that:", "concept": "The answer is a: alpha pride is pride in one's whole self, and beta pride is pride in a specific behaviour. The distinction comes from June Tangney (1989), whose Test of Self-Conscious Affect (TOSCA, first published with colleagues in 1989, revised as TOSCA-3 in 2000) describes everyday scenarios and asks how likely the person is to react in each of several listed ways. It builds on Helen Block Lewis (1971), who separated shame from guilt by the focus of the judgement. Shame is a negative judgement of the whole self ('I am a bad person'); guilt is a negative judgement of a specific act ('I did a bad thing'). Tangney applied the same split to pride. Alpha pride is pride focused on the global self ('I am brilliant'), the positive mirror of shame. Beta pride is pride focused on what one did ('I did that well'), the positive mirror of guilt. Besides the two pride scales, the TOSCA scores shame-proneness, guilt-proneness, externalisation and detachment. A related split came later from Jessica Tracy and Richard Robins (2007): authentic pride, tied to effort and achievement, and hubristic pride, a global, arrogant glorification of the self. Example: after Zoya tops her statistics exam, thinking 'I am a genius' is alpha pride, while 'my weekly practice paid off' is beta pride. Option a is right because the TOSCA scores pride by its focus: pride in the global self is alpha pride, and pride in a particular behaviour is beta pride. Exam trap: option b simply swaps the two; remember that alpha pairs with shame (whole self) and beta with guilt (specific act).", "traps": {"b": "This reverses Tangney's definitions: pride in a specific behaviour is beta pride (the positive parallel of guilt), and pride in the whole self is alpha pride (the parallel of shame).", "c": "Public versus private is not the TOSCA distinction; both kinds of pride can be felt alone or in company, and they differ in their focus (self versus behaviour), not in their audience.", "d": "Pride in one's group or family is collective (group-based) pride, an idea closer to Henri Tajfel and John Turner's social identity theory (1979), not Tangney's beta pride, which is pride in one's own specific behaviour."}}, {"q": "On a test, the 25th percentile (Q1) is 42 and the 75th percentile (Q3) is 66. What is the interquartile range?", "concept": "The answer is a: the interquartile range is 24. The interquartile range (IQR) is the distance between the third quartile and the first quartile: IQR = Q3 minus Q1 = 66 minus 42 = 24. Q1 is the score below which 25 percent of the group falls, and Q3 the score below which 75 percent falls, so the IQR is the spread of the middle 50 percent of scores. Because it ignores the top and bottom quarters, extreme scores barely affect it, which makes it the preferred measure of spread for skewed data, reported alongside the median. It is the length of the box in a box plot, and John Tukey (1977) used it to flag outliers: scores more than 1.5 x IQR beyond the quartiles, here below 42 minus 36 = 6 or above 66 + 36 = 102. Half the IQR is the quartile deviation, or semi-interquartile range: Q = (Q3 minus Q1) / 2 = 12. Example: if Harsh's class shows these quartiles, the middle half of his classmates scored between 42 and 66, a band 24 marks wide. Option a is right because the IQR is simply Q3 minus Q1, with no halving. Exam trap: examiners place the quartile deviation (12) next to the IQR (24); 'semi' means halve it, while plain 'interquartile range' does not.", "traps": {"b": "12 is the quartile deviation (semi-interquartile range), (66 minus 42) / 2; the IQR itself is not halved.", "c": "54 is the midpoint of the two quartiles, (42 + 66) / 2, sometimes called the midhinge; it is a measure of location, not of spread.", "d": "108 is Q1 + Q3, a sum that measures nothing useful; spread needs the difference between the quartiles, not their total."}}]});
